 for Monday, June 1, 2015.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I fell on the ground. It shocks you. It hurts you. It shivers your body.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Quotation of the Day for Monday, June 1, 2015.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quotation of the Day', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-06-01T04:12:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Quote', '_id': 'nyt://article/65558dc4-7398-5325-86be-de53384cb05e', 'word_count': 41, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65558dc4-7398-5325-86be-de53384cb05e'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/01/arts/television/whats-on-tv-monday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8:15 A.M. (Sundance) ORLANDO (1993) A beautiful young nobleman (Tilda Swinton, above) adored by an aging Queen Elizabeth I (Quentin Crisp) falls asleep one night in the 17th century and wakes up five days later as a woman. “Same person, no difference at all,” she says, unflustered. “Just a different sex.” She continues to learn about life through the centuries up until the present, pondering the differences between men and women — as well as their similarities — in this adaptation by Sally Potter of Virginia Woolf’s 1928 novel. Charlotte Valandrey plays Sasha, a wanton Muscovite princess with whom the male Orlando falls in love. Billy Zane is the American with whom Orlando’s female version has a more enduring if less sexually demanding relationship. “This ravishing and witty spectacle invades the mind through eyes that are dazzled without ever being anesthetized,” Vincent Canby wrote in The New York Times. “\xa0‘Orlando’ could well become a classic of a very special kind, not mainstream perhaps, but a model for independent filmmakers who follow their own irrational muses, sometimes to unmourned obscurity, occasionally to glory.” More than anything else, he added: “\xa0‘Orlando’ is Ms. Swinton’s triumph. With the firmest but lightest of touches, she has spun gossamer.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On TV Monday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What&#8217;s On TV Monday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-01T04:13:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/9297ee5f-3cce-5393-8c1c-ad5ddc364acf', 'word_count': 736, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9297ee5f-3cce-5393-8c1c-ad5ddc364acf'}, {'abstract': 'Corrections appearing in print on Monday, June 1, 2015.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/01/pageoneplus/corrections-june-1-2015.html', 'snippet': 'Corrections appearing in print on Monday, June 1, 2015.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BUSINESS DAY', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections: June 1, 2015', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-06-01T05:14:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Correction', '_id': 'nyt://article/35ec30a0-4201-56c6-85f9-b697fee1ca6e', 'word_count': 288,