iage from the journal Science raises the question of just how much cheating goes on.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cheating in scientific and academic papers is a longstanding problem, but it is hard to read recent headlines and not conclude that it has gotten worse. Falsified or erroneous results have forced authors and editors to retract papers from journals that let themselves be duped into publishing them. Researchers at some of the nation’s most prestigious universities have been implicated. There are ways to minimize this kind of fraud, but it will require changing the process, from how scientists share their data to how their peers review it and who is allowed to enforce academic standards.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/01/opinion/01mon2/01mon2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 796, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/01/opinion/01mon2/01mon2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 796}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/01/opinion/01mon2/01mon2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 773, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/01/opinion/01mon2/01mon2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1150, 'width': 867, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/01/opinion/01mon2/01mon2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/01/opinion/01mon2/01mon2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/01/opinion/01mon2/01mon2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Scientists Who Cheat', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What Happens When Scientists Cheat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cheating', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Science (Journal)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics and Official Misconduct', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Academic and Scientific Journals', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-01T07:21:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/059c4cb2-6148-5adb-b034-a2c654ee6829', 'word_count': 506, 'uri': 'nyt://article/059c4cb2-6148-5adb-b034-a2c654ee6829'}, {'abstract': 'Despite good intentions on both sides, it is not certain that a deal will be reached between Greece and its creditors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/01/opinion/paul-krugman-that-1914-feeling.html', 'snippet': 'Despite good intentions on both sides, it is not certain that a deal will be reached between Greece and its creditors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'U.S. officials are generally cautious about intervening in European policy debates. The European Union is, after all, an economic superpower in its own right — far too big and rich for America to have much direct influence — led by sophisticated people who should be able to manage their own affairs. So it’s startling to learn that Jacob Lew, the Treasury secretary, recently warned Europeans that they had better settle the Greek situation soon, lest there be a destructive “accident.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/12/opinion/krugman-circular/krugman-circular-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 591, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/11/12/opinion/krugman-circular/krugman-circular-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 591}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/12/opinion/krugman-circular/krugman-circular-jumbo-v5.jpg', 'height': 1008, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/12/opinion/krugman-circular/krugman-circular-superJumbo-v5.jpg', 'height': 1852, 'width': 1881, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/12/opinion/krugman-circular/krugman-circular-thumbStandard-v5.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/12/opinion/krugman-circular/krugman-circular-thumbStandard-v5.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/12/opinion/krugman-circular/krugman-circular-thumbLarge-v7.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'That 1914 Feeling', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'That 1914 Feeling', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Krugman, Paul', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War I (1914-18)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Euro (Currency)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-01T07:21:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/5704abb1-9278-540f-9177-fc1ac31b2677', 'word_count': 824, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5704abb1-9278-540f-9177-fc1ac31b2677'}, {'abstract': 'Given the weakness of Iraqi forces, Americans should be training and giving weapons directly to Sunni tribal fighters in Anbar Province.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/01/opinion/whos-willing-to-fight-for-iraq.html', 'snippet': 'Given the weakness of Iraqi forces, Americans should be training and giving weapons directly to Sunni tribal fighters in Anbar Province.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Defense Secretary Ashton Carter’s recent assessment of Iraqi security forces was impolitic and true, and rarely voiced by senior officials. After the devastating loss of the Iraqi city of Ramadi to the Islamic State in May, he told CNN that while Iraqi troops vastly outnumbered the brutal extremists, they “just showed no will to fight.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/05/30/opinion/01mon1web/01mon1web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 420, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/05/30/opinion/01mon1web/01mon1web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 420}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/05/30/opinion/01mon1web/01mon1web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 717, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/05/30/opinion/01mon1web/01mon1web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1435, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/05/30/opinion/01mon1web/01mon1web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/05/30/opinion/01mon1web/01mon1web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/05/30/opinion/01mon1web/01mon1web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who’s Willing to Fight for Iraq?', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who’s Willing to Fight for Iraq?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carter, Ashton B', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-01T07:21:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/7e5d3038-0344-54f6-a3d7-19f166d898df', 'word_count': 645, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7e5d3038-0344-54f6-a3d7-19f166d898df'}, {'abstract': 'We need to understand the nature of such acts and help the survivors, and not use the issue for political points.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/01/opinion/charles-blow-surviving-child-sexual-abuse.html', 'snippet': 'We need to understand the nature of such acts and help the survivors, and not use the issue for political points.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last month came the news that Josh Duggar, now-former executive director of the Family Research Council’s lobbying arm and eldest son on the TLC reality show “19 Kids and Counting,” had apologized and said he had “acted inexcusably.” As In Touch Weekly magazine put it: “Josh Duggar was investigated for multiple sex offenses — including forcible fondling — against five minors. Some of the alleged offenses investigated were felonies.” Those minors apparently included his sisters. Duggar was around 14 years old when the reported assaults took place.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/blow-circu