 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Kimmelman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kimmelman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f027e967-9796-550e-a11f-3db5bcd73b9b', 'word_count': 1278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f027e967-9796-550e-a11f-3db5bcd73b9b'}, {'abstract': 'Whose words do you hear in your head, offering you guidance, in times of difficulty?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/01/what-are-some-words-of-wisdom-that-guide-your-life/', 'snippet': 'Whose words do you hear in your head, offering you guidance, in times of difficulty?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In difficult times or in just trying to choose between several equally good options, many people find guidance in expressions or sayings, like “one day at a time” or “don’t sweat the small stuff.” Then there are more personal credos, like the words of a wise family member or even a hero from a book or movie that come to mind in times of uncertainty.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/05/31/opinion/sunday/31WEINERsub-LN/31WEINERsub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 689, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/05/31/opinion/sunday/31WEINERsub-LN/31WEINERsub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 689}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/05/31/opinion/sunday/31WEINERsub-LN/31WEINERsub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 892, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/05/31/opinion/sunday/31WEINERsub-LN/31WEINERsub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1924, 'width': 1675, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/05/31/opinion/sunday/31WEINERsub-LN/31WEINERsub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/05/31/opinion/sunday/31WEINERsub-LN/31WEINERsub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/05/31/opinion/sunday/31WEINERsub-LN/31WEINERsub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Are Some ‘Words of Wisdom’ That Guide Your Life?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-06-01T09:01:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shannon Doyne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shannon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Doyne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d3321a8-8601-5526-bad3-83a43c6d7e40', 'word_count': 427, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d3321a8-8601-5526-bad3-83a43c6d7e40'}, {'abstract': 'A new biography tries to understand our elusive 40th president.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/books/review/reagan-the-life-by-h-w-brands.html', 'snippet': 'A new biography tries to understand our elusive 40th president.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For a man who lived most of his life on camera, Ronald Reagan eludes focus. There was, and remains, a gauziness to the picture; Reagan retained, throughout his political career, the remoteness of a screen idol, though he never achieved that status as a movie actor. He was ubiquitous for decades and, as president, left a lasting imprint on America’s political culture. Yet he was all the same an unknowable man — even to those nearest him. In White House meetings, he was mostly silent, often leaving his aides to guess at (and feud over) his views. In his personal relationships, he was unfailingly warm but rarely intimate. “He doesn’t let anybody get too close,” one observer said. “There’s a wall around him.” That the observer was his wife, Nancy, should give pause to any politician or pundit who claims to know what Reagan would do if he were here today. (It should, but it won’t.)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07SHESOL/07shesol-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07SHESOL/07shesol-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07SHESOL/07shesol-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 686, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07SHESOL/07shesol-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1371, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07SHESOL/07shesol-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07SHESOL/07shesol-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07SHESOL/07shesol-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Reagan: The Life,’ by H. W. Brands', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Unknowable Man', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brands, H W', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reagan, Ronald Wilson', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Reagan: The Life (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-01T09:30:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Shesol', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shesol', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b4c91d7a-e3c2-5520-9930-272cdd9504d8', 'word_count': 1211, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4c91d7a-e3c2-5520-9930-272cdd9504d8'}, {'abstract': 'Mat Johnson’s new novel tells a semi-autobiographical story about race and mixed-race in America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/books/review/loving-day-by-mat-johnson.html', 'snippet': 'Mat Johnson’s new novel tells a semi-autobiographical story about race and mixed-race in America.', 'lead_paragraph': ' “A house divided against itself cannot stand,” Abraham Lincoln declared in his 1858 speech presaging the Civil War. Such a house sits at the heart of Mat Johnson’s ribald, incisive novel “Loving Day.” Bequeathed to the narrator, Warren Duffy, by his deceased father, it’s a roofless, ramshackle mansion in a black neighborhood in Philadelphia: “I look at the buckling floors. I look at the cracks in all the walls, the evidence of a foundation crumbling beneath us. I smell the char of the fire, the sweet reek of mold, the insult of mouse urine. I see a million things that have to be fixed, restored, corrected, each one impossible and each task mandatory for me to escape again.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07DREISINGERCOVER/07-Dreisinger-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 453, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07DREISINGERCOVER/07-Dreisinger-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 453}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07DREISINGERCOVER/07-Dreisinger-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 773, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07DREISINGERCOVER/07-Dreisinger-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1547, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07DREISINGERCOVER/07DREISINGERCOVER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07DREISINGERCOVER/07DREISINGERCOVER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07DREISINGERCOVER/07DREISINGERCOVER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'wi