T01:39:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Cacciola', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cacciola', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc709276-949a-50a0-ae26-9b5b96e7c599', 'word_count': 911, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc709276-949a-50a0-ae26-9b5b96e7c599'}, {'abstract': 'The two patients were being treated for respiratory ailments in a hospital last month when they had contact with the nation’s first MERS patient.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/02/world/asia/south-korea-2-die-of-mers-virus.html', 'snippet': 'The two patients were being treated for respiratory ailments in a hospital last month when they had contact with the nation’s first MERS patient.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two South Koreans have died of Middle East Respiratory Syndrome, the government said\xa0 Tuesday, the country’s first confirmed deaths from the virus. The two patients — a woman, 58, and a man, 71 — died Monday, the Health Ministry said in a statement. Both were being treated for respiratory ailments in a hospital last month when they had contact with the nation’s first MERS patient, a 68-year-old man who tested positive after a trip to the Middle East. South Korea has so far detected 25 MERS cases — mostly found among the hospital’s staff, patients and visitors — and more than 680 people have been placed in isolation after having contact with those infected. The syndrome has killed hundreds of people in the Middle East. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'South Korea: 2 Die of MERS Virus', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Asia; South Korea: 2 Die of MERS Virus', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'MERS (Middle East Respiratory Syndrome)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-02T01:41:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Choe Sang-Hun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Choe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sang-Hun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/a00b9b00-f063-54fd-8341-968a1a17b817', 'word_count': 125, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a00b9b00-f063-54fd-8341-968a1a17b817'}, {'abstract': 'The auto supplier is preparing for a congressional hearing on Tuesday where it will be asked about repairs for defective vehicles, and why it took so long to recall them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/02/business/takata-says-it-will-no-longer-make-side-inflater-linked-to-airbag-defect.html', 'snippet': 'The auto supplier is preparing for a congressional hearing on Tuesday where it will be asked about repairs for defective vehicles, and why it took so long to recall them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As it prepared for a congressional hearing on Tuesday, the Japanese auto supplier Takata vowed on Monday to stop producing the driver-side inflaters that have been tied to a defect in its airbags. But the company said it would continue using an airbag propellant that experts said was dangerous.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/02/business/02airbags-web1/02airbags-web1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 416, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge'