 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/35f01cf9-37a5-5dd2-8233-d4dc10e12eaf', 'word_count': 428, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35f01cf9-37a5-5dd2-8233-d4dc10e12eaf'}, {'abstract': 'A recent discovery puts the modern world in touch with the depravity of the slave trade.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/02/opinion/finding-a-slave-ship-uncovering-history.html', 'snippet': 'A recent discovery puts the modern world in touch with the depravity of the slave trade.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The trans-Atlantic slave trade that sent 12.5 million human beings in chains from Africa to the Americas — killing about two million along the way — has been described by the historian David Brion Davis as “one of history’s greatest crimes against humanity.” It was driven not by hatred, but by greed. The colonizers — including Spaniards, Portuguese, Dutch, British, French, Danes, Swedes, Brazilians, and North Americans — wanted cheap labor for sugar, tobacco, coffee, indigo and other goods demanded by the aristocracy.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/02/opinion/02tue2web/02tue2web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/02/opinion/02tue2web/02tue2web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/02/opinion/02tue2web/02tue2web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/02/opinion/02tue2web/02tue2web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo',