 killing nine workers before fleeing, officials said.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gunmen in Northern Afghanistan Kill 9 Local Aid Workers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gunmen in Afghanistan Kill 9 Local Aid Workers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mashal, Mujib', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sukhanyar, Jawad', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Afghan National Solidarity Program', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'People in Need', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-02T09:23:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mujib Mashal and Jawad Sukhanyar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mujib', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mashal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jawad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sukhanyar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/44139455-a719-59e5-8cef-946df7c378f2', 'word_count': 828, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44139455-a719-59e5-8cef-946df7c378f2'}, {'abstract': 'Aleksandar Hemon’s comic novel about alienation and the undead.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/books/review/the-making-of-zombie-wars-by-aleksandar-hemon.html', 'snippet': 'Aleksandar Hemon’s comic novel about alienation and the undead.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Summer of Love rolled into town and left behind an S.T.D. otherwise known as 1968. The Vietnam War ratcheted up the body count. Racial strife led to nationwide riots. The murders of Martin Luther King Jr. and Robert Kennedy must have made the end times seem at bloody hand. Cue Nixon and that silent majority. Forty-seven years later we still live in the era’s apocalyptic shadow, those events a zombie horde in ever-present pursuit — race issues, ill-considered wars, the Russians — that infects us with the undead past. Oh, and you can also blame 1968 for that unfortunate metaphor, because in October of that year George A. Romero’s “Night of the Living Dead” introduced us to a new kind of zombie: slow yet relentless, brainless yet hungry, endlessly symbolic yet always supercool. These flesh eaters have fed well on our culture, evolving from B-movie ghouls to bankable, if thankless, stars of film and screen, book and video game, to the point where they should probably unionize. We are living in peak zombie. Now, into this fray tiptoes Aleksandar Hemon with his latest novel, “The Making of Zombie Wars.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07GILBERT/07-Gilbert-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 719, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07GILBERT/07-Gilbert-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 719}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07GILBERT/07-Gilbert-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 855, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07GILBERT/07-Gilbert-sup