sion (Mental)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Therapy and Rehabilitation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-02T10:30:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gordon Marino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gordon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/101b94d4-69e2-52e1-a04b-b0c8cb4a5e73', 'word_count': 1403, 'uri': 'nyt://article/101b94d4-69e2-52e1-a04b-b0c8cb4a5e73'}, {'abstract': 'If the plan’s opponents succeed, municipalities will be less likely to allow their open spaces to be used for recreation and other outdoor purposes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/02/opinion/nyus-opponents-threaten-open-spaces.html', 'snippet': 'If the plan’s opponents succeed, municipalities will be less likely to allow their open spaces to be used for recreation and other outdoor purposes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York State’s Court of Appeals is scheduled to hear a challenge today to New York University’s plan to build new academic buildings. The outcome could jeopardize the way open space is used throughout the state.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'N.Y.U.’s Opponents Threaten Open Spaces', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York University', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appeals Courts (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Parks and Recreation Department (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-02T10:45:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adrian Benepe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adrian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Benepe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/38c217bf-53ad-5f1c-841d-240c879c4ba5', 'word_count': 960, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38c217bf-53ad-5f1c-841d-240c879c4ba5'}, {'abstract': 'It wasn’t just the amendments that followed the fighting. The war changed the very way Americans relate to their government.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/02/how-the-civil-war-changed-the-constitution/', 'snippet': 'It wasn’t just the amendments that followed the fighting. The war changed the very way Americans relate to their government.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The most obvious constitutional result of the Civil War was the adoption of three landmark constitutional amendments. The 13th ended slavery forever in the United States, while the 14th made all persons born in the United States (including the former slaves) citizens of the nation and prohibited the states from denying anyone the privileges and immunities of American citizenship, due process or law, or equal protection of the law. Finally, the 15th Amendment, ratified in 1870, prohibited the states from denying the franchise to anyone based on “race, color, or previous condition of servitude.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How the Civil War Changed the Constitution', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Constitution (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Constitutional Amendments', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery (Historical)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-02T11:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Finkelman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finkelman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e72172a9-2ed9-5900-a346-2e111fe638bd', 'word_count': 2194, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e72172a9-2ed9-5900-a346-2e111fe638bd'}, {'abstract': 'Most Americans say that money has too much of an influence on politicians and that campaign finance changes are needed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2015/06/02/us/politics/money-in-politics-poll.html', 'snippet': 'Most Americans say that money has too much of an influence on politicians and that campaign finance changes are needed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most Americans say that money has too much of an influence on politicians and that campaign finance changes are needed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'ca