y Films and Programs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lauzen, Martha', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Center for the Study of Women in Television and Film', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-03T04:01:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cara Buckley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buckley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9fe9ea42-85f2-51a4-b962-d30745a121e7', 'word_count': 189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9fe9ea42-85f2-51a4-b962-d30745a121e7'}, {'abstract': 'This word has appeared in 132 New York Times articles in the past year.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/03/word-of-the-day-dapper/', 'snippet': 'This word has appeared in 132 New York Times articles in the past year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'dapper • \\ˈda-pər\\ • adjective ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Word of the Day | dapper', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-06-03T04:02:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab1b7284-3de9-5901-a6e2-9cd218b88c98', 'word_count': 132, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab1b7284-3de9-5901-a6e2-9cd218b88c98'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/03/arts/television/whats-on-tv-wednesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '5:15 P.M. (IFC) AMERICAN HISTORY X (1998) In an Oscar-nominated role, Edward Norton plays Derek Vinyard, a white supremacist who goes to prison for a gruesome killing. He renounces racism there, but his younger brother, Danny (Edward Furlong), a high school student, remains steadfast in the attitudes he learned from Derek. After Danny writes a book report on “Mein Kampf,” his principal, Dr. Robert Sweeney (Avery Brooks, above left, with Mr. Norton), creates a remedial course for him called American History X. Dr. Sweeney visits Derek in prison and they agree to try to persuade Danny to give up his bigotry. “\xa0‘American History X’ has enough fiery acting and provocative bombast to make its impact felt,” Janet Maslin wrote in The New York Times about this film directed by Tony Kaye. “For one thing, its willingness to take on ugly political realities gives it a substantial raison d’être. For another, it has been directed with a mixture of handsome photo-realism and visceral punch.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On TV Wednesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What&#8217;s On TV Wednesday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-03T05:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alec M. Priester', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alec', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/eace7cbf-3e4f-5c8f-872e-f7b39a76d54d', 'word_count': 678, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eace7cbf-3e4f-5c8f-872e-f7b39a76d54d'}, {'abstract': 'Corrections appearing in print on Wednesday, June 3, 2015.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/03/pageoneplus/corrections-june-3-2015.html', 'snippet': 'Corrections appearing in print on Wednesday, June 3, 2015.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRONT PAGE', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections: June 3, 2015', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-06-03T05:37:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a5e900a-a7d5-52a4-b368-3fdc2aeabfee', 'word_count': 659, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a5e900a-a7d5-52a4-b368-3fdc2aeabfee'}, {'abstract': 'Quotation of the day for Wednesday, June 3, 2015.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/03/pageoneplus/quotation-of-the-day.html', 'snippet': 'Quotation of the day for Wednesday, June 3, 2015.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Although the members of FIFA have given me the new mandate, this mandate does not seem to be supported by everybody in the world of football.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Quotation of the Day', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quotation of the Day', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-06-03T05:37:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Quote', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc1856d7-8d22-5aba-b5b4-b41373547afa', 'word_count': 40, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc1856d7-8d22-5aba-b5b4-b41373547afa'}, {'abstract': 'A railroad crossing sign and 300 neckties. Dozens of bicycles and a stash of Polaroids. Robert Rauschenberg lived surrounded by the everyday objects he might have turned into art. His archive contains worlds of creative possibility — and is virtually impossible to categorize.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/03/t-magazine/robert-rauschenberg-endless-combinations.html', 'snippet': 'A railroad crossing sign and 300 neckties. Dozens of bicycles and a stash of Polaroids. Robert Rauschenberg lived surrounded by the everyday objects he might have turned into art. His archive contains worlds of creative possibility — and is virtua...', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN YOU’RE LOOKING for the Robert Rauschenberg archive, everything starts to resemble a Rauschenberg. The chance juxtaposition of a vibrant yellow school bus next to a red Staples sign seems somehow intended, part of the artist’s grand design. So too might the pale lavender blossoms of wisteria that frame the gray painted door tucked away in a hardscrabble industrial zone of lots and chain-link fencing.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '86', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/02/multimedia/tmag-rausch-smile/tmag-rausch-smile-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 331, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/02/multimedia/tmag-rausch-smile/tmag-rausch-smile-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 331}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/02/multimedia/tmag-rausch-smile/tmag-rausch-smile-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 566, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/02/multimedia/tmag-rausch-smile/tmag-rausch-smile-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1131, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/02/multimedia/tmag-ra