 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-03T20:28:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephanie Clifford', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephanie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clifford', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f613c746-476a-5ea6-91d6-b113e2cc4b5a', 'word_count': 1192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f613c746-476a-5ea6-91d6-b113e2cc4b5a'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Blatter should leave immediately and allow an interim president to start introducing reforms.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/05/opinion/sepp-blatters-resignation-and-soccer-reform.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Blatter should leave immediately and allow an interim president to start introducing reforms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s not entirely clear yet what led Sepp Blatter to decide to step down as president of FIFA, world soccer’s governing body, only four days after he was defiantly re-elected to a fifth term. It may be that his lawyers advised him this was the best way to combat corruption charges that would soon entangle him, or that major sponsors urged him to move along. In any case, his departure clears the way to start reforming the culture of corruption and impunity that have long tarnished the world’s most popular sport.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/04/opinion/04blatterWeb/04blatterWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/04/opinion/04blatterWeb/04blatterWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/04/opinion/04bla