ate': '2015-06-04T08:59:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joyce Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joyce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c87e1a15-d87e-5157-95e3-2cd0c7af2a3f', 'word_count': 950, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c87e1a15-d87e-5157-95e3-2cd0c7af2a3f'}, {'abstract': 'Silicon Valley is spreading a peculiarly American vision of the future. How will the rest of the world respond?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/magazine/welcome-to-the-age-of-digital-imperialism.html', 'snippet': 'Silicon Valley is spreading a peculiarly American vision of the future. How will the rest of the world respond?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In March, the Culture Ministry of Thailand sent out an unusual communiqué. Women, it seems, both locals and tourists, were snapping pictures of their breasts from beneath, in an act known colloquially as the “underboob selfie.” The ministry reminded women that sharing such images was banned under the Computer Crime Act of 2007 and that offenders could face five years in prison. Since the shots were cropped below the face, a ministry spokesman acknowledged to Reuters, prosecuting the scofflaws would be difficult. But this flaunting of flesh, the spokesman feared, might tempt others toward the same indelicacy. “We can only warn people to not take it up,” he said. “They are inappropriate actions.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/magazine/07intro-promo/07intro-promo-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 610, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/07/magazine/07intro-promo/07intro-promo-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 610}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/magazine/07intro-promo/07intro-promo-jumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1007, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/magazine/07intro-promo/07intro-promo-superJumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2014, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/magazine/07intro-promo/07intro-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/07/magazine/07intro-promo/07intro-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/magazine/07intro-promo/07intro-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Welcome to the Age of Digital Imperialism', 'kicker': 'The Tech & Design Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Tech &amp; Design Issue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wasik, Bill', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Silicon Valley (Calif)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smartphones', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Thailand', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-04T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Wasik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wasik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/56d204ec-5d3e-572d-bf54-c97a3355a971', 'word_count': 1491, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56d204ec-5d3e-572d-bf54-c97a3355a971'}, {'abstract': 'Google and other tech dreamers think machines can make languages superfluous.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/magazine/is-translation-an-art-or-a-math-problem.html', 'snippet': 'Google and other tech dreamers think machines can make languages superfluous.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One Enlightenment aspiration that the science-\xadfiction industry has long taken for granted, as a necessary intergalactic conceit, is the universal translator. In a 1967 episode of “Star Trek,” Mr. Spock assembles such a device from spare parts lying around the ship. An elongated chrome cylinder with blinking red-and-green indicator lights, it resembles a retracted light saber; Captain Kirk explains how it works with an off-the-cuff disquisition on the principles of Chomsky’s “universal grammar,” and they walk outside to the desert-\xadisland planet of Gamma Canaris N, where they’re being held hostage by an alien. The alien, whom they call The Companion, materializes as a fraction of sparkling cloud. It looks like an orange Christmas tree made of vaporized mortadella. Kirk grips the translator and addresses their kidnapper in a slow, patronizing, put-down-the-gun tone. The all-\xadpowerful Companion is astonished.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '48', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit