shot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Irwin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Irwin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/10a6c492-2fa2-5d5d-907f-d5cb4da5dc86', 'word_count': 1795, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10a6c492-2fa2-5d5d-907f-d5cb4da5dc86'}, {'abstract': 'Twenty-six years after the military suppression of the protests at Tiananmen Square in Beijing, thousands gathered in Hong Kong to mourn those killed.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/04/scenes-from-tiananmen-memorial-events-in-hong-kong/', 'snippet': 'Twenty-six years after the military suppression of the protests at Tiananmen Square in Beijing, thousands gathered in Hong Kong to mourn those killed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'June 4 marks the 26th anniversary of the deadly crackdown on protesters in Beijing, which left hundreds, possibly thousands, of people dead. In Hong Kong, residents have long gathered to remember those killed. This year, different groups in the semiautonomous Chinese city held separate vigils, a sign of the increasing complex sense of local identity and concerns among some that commemorating the 1989 protests at Tiananmen Square detracts from efforts to cultivate democracy in Hong Kong, as Crystal Tse and Alan Wong report.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Scenes from the Tiananmen Memorial Events in Hong Kong', 'kicker': 'Sinosphere', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Beijing (China)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hong Kong', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tiananmen Square (Beijing)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-04T11:22:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Austin Ramzy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Austin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ramzy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8ef3f25-1074-5187-a082-0e210e718a9d', 'word_count': 145, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8ef3f25-1074-5187-a082-0e210e718a9d'}, {'abstract': 'Divisions over how to commemorate the Tiananmen crackdown show how Hong Kong residents are increasingly seeing the push for a freer China as someone else’s fight.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/05/world/asia/hong-kong-disconnected-from-china-democracy-on-tiananmen-anniversary.html', 'snippet': 'Divisions over how to commemorate the Tiananmen crackdown show how Hong Kong residents are increasingly seeing the push for a freer China as someone else’s fight.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — For the first time in years, Hui King-to chose not to take part in the candlelight vigil in Victoria Park here commemorating those who died during the Chinese government’s suppression of protests in Tiananmen Square in Beijing on June 4, 1989.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/05/world/05Sino-Rally3/05Sino-Rally3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/05/world/05Sino-Rally3/05Sino-Rally3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/05/world/05Sino-Rally3/05Sino-Rally3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_n