how his prose has weathered the years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'William Styron’s literary reputation was in limbo, if not apocalyptic decline, well before his death in 2006. His big novels — “Lie Down in Darkness” (1951), “The Confessions of Nat Turner” (1967) and “Sophie’s Choice” (1979) — can seem like elegiac museum pieces, or mass-market paperbacks left too long in the sun.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/05/arts/06BOOKSTYRONJPALT/06BOOKSTYRONJPALT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 717, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/05/arts/06BOOKSTYRONJPALT/06BOOKSTYRONJPALT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 717}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/05/arts/06BOOKSTYRONJPALT/06BOOKSTYRONJPALT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 857, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/05/arts/06BOOKSTYRONJPALT/06BOOKSTYRONJPALT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1714, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/05/arts/06BOOKSTYRONJPALT/06BOOKSTYRONJPALT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/05/arts/06BOOKSTYRONJPALT/06BOOKSTYRONJPALT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/05/arts/06BOOKSTYRONJPALT/06BOOKSTYRONJPALT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLa