one, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Preston, Julia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nelson, Bill (1942- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Homeland Security Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Disney, Walt, World (Lake Buena Vista, Fla)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Visas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Workers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Jeh C', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-05T00:25:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Preston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Preston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/40549a2c-8af7-52e7-9576-46c662001f67', 'word_count': 379, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40549a2c-8af7-52e7-9576-46c662001f67'}, {'abstract': 'The Aboriginal star David Gulpilil plays the title character in Rolf de Heer’s film about a man who feels out of place in his own country.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/05/movies/review-in-charlies-country-a-refugee-in-his-homeland.html', 'snippet': 'The Aboriginal star David Gulpilil plays the title character in Rolf de Heer’s film about a man who feels out of place in his own country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Australian cinema, the wilderness has long served as a rebuke to the primacy of Western civilization. With “Twelve Canoes“ and now “Charlie’s Country,” the Dutch-Australian director Rolf de Heer has portrayed the lived experience of Aboriginals that are often treated as part of an exotic landscape. Using a combination of bleak realism, fatalistic humor and a healthy dose of sentimentality, Mr. de Heer traces the downward spiral of a man who has become a refugee in his own homeland.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/05/arts/06CHARLIE/06CHARLIE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 902, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/05/arts/06CHARLIE/06CHARLIE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 902}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/05/arts/06CHARLIE/06CHARLIE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/05/arts/06CHARLIE/06CHARLIE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1363, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/05/arts/06CHARLIE/06CHARLIE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/05/arts/06CHARLIE/06CHARLIE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/05/arts/06CHARLIE/06CHARLIE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: In ‘Charlie’s Country,’ a Refugee in His Homeland', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Review: In ‘Charlie’s Country,’ a Refugee in His Homeland', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Heer, Rolf', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gulpilil, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-05T00:25:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicolas Rapold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicolas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rapold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe151dd0-a77e-53b2-9696-232a50d27763', 'word_count': 255, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe151dd0-a77e-53b2-9696-232a50d27763'}, {'abstract': 'Peter Cousens’s film follows two narratives: one set in Virginia and points north in 1856, the other on a trans-Atlantic slave ship in 1748.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/05/movies/review-freedom-stars-cuba-gooding-jr-as-a-slave-fleeing-to-canada.html', 'snippet': 'Peter Cousens’s film follows two narratives: one set in Virginia and points north in 1856, the other on a trans-Atlantic slave ship in 1748.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Freedom” takes a compelling chapter in American history — the flight of slaves from Southern bondage to Canada via the Underground Railroad — and flattens its drama. The film pursues twin narratives: one, starting in 1856, presents the trek of a slave, Samuel Woodward (Cuba Gooding Jr.), and his family from the Richmond, Va., area to points north; the other, beginning in 1748, follows the fact-based story of John Newton (Bernhard Forcher), an English slave trader voyaging from Gambia to Charleston, S.C.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘Freedom’ Stars Cuba Gooding Jr. as a Slave Fleeing to Canada', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Review: ‘Freedom’ Stars Cuba Gooding Jr. as a Slave Fleeing to Canada', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cousens, Peter (1955- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gooding, Cuba Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Freedom (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-05T00:34:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3942c606-4206-5525-895f-ac18cd4e6614', 'word_count': 234, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3942c606-4206-5525-895f-ac18cd4e6614'}, {'abstract': 'Two vagabonds go on a Catskills adventure in this film from Eddie Mullins.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/05/movies/review-doomsdays-a-jaunt-into-strangers-homes-in-the-catskills.html', 'snippet': 'Two vagabonds go on a Catskills adventure in this film from Eddie Mullins.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Nowhere in particular” is only a throwaway line in “Doomsdays,” but it couldn’t be more essential to our understanding of its two main characters. It’s where they’re from, where they’re going and where their heads are at, these vagabonds who invade empty vacation homes in the Catskills and squat until forced to move on.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘Doomsdays,’ a Jaunt Into Strangers’ Homes in the Catskills', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Review: ‘Doomsdays,’ a Jaunt Into Strangers’ Homes in the Catskills', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mullins, Eddie (Film Director)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Doomsdays (Movie)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-05T00:41:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeannette Catsoulis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeannette', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Catsoulis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/09b929ff-9424-534f-9fcb-b4f2c0850cb9', 'word_count': 280, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09b929ff-9424-534f-9fcb-b4f2c0850cb9'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Juntwait, who became the Met’s third radio host in 2004, hosting 229 Saturday radio broadcasts, also presided over 898 broadcasts on the Met’s satellite-radio channel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/05/arts/music/margaret-juntwait-the-voice-of-the-met-on-radio-dies-at-58.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Juntwait, who became the Met’s third radio host in 2004, hosting 229 Saturday radio broadcasts, also presided over 898 broadcasts on the Met’s satellite-radio channel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A teacher who heard Margaret Juntwait talking had some advice: “You should consider a career in radio.” She did not pay much attention. She was preoccupied with studying for a sixth-grade math test.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/05/arts/05juntwait-obit/05juntwait-obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 435, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/05/arts/05juntwait-obit/05juntwait-obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 435}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, '