ominated-for-a-james-beard-award.html', 'snippet': 'At tonight’s ceremony, Matthew Maddy — who takes T on a tour of his Clinton Hill duplex — will find out whether he’s won for Outstanding Restaurant Design.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If there can be said to be a Brooklyn look in the restaurant world today, Matthew Maddy is at least partially responsible for it. Many of the borough’s most beloved and stylish eateries sport warm, eclectic and detail-rich interiors created by the designer-builder-entrepreneur: the tile-adorned cocktail den Weather Up, name-checked on “Girls”; the Fort Greene institution No. 7; Grand Army, a new project whose partners include the owners of Mile End and Rucola and which opens to the public this week; and the casually elegant Colonia Verde, for which Maddy is nominated for the James Beard Foundation Award for Outstanding Restaurant Design, to be awarded tonight. (He has also been involved with places in Manhattan, London, Oakland and beyond.) But to hear Maddy tell it, his design signature is really about not having one. “My aesthetic is just having an opinion and knowing what I like,” he says. “It’s not a principled school of thought. All the restaurants involve such different people and cultures and communities.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At Home With a Brooklyn Restaurant Designer, Nominated for a James Beard Award', 'kicker': 'Home and Work', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-06-05T04:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TDesign', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eviana Hartman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eviana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hartman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/11411101-be39-572e-b890-9852fac22017', 'word_count': 