/e54503f3-fc07-572b-a64a-bdc8105877c2'}, {'abstract': 'From mining to power to waste, there is a physical footprint in the online experience.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/magazine/making-and-unmaking-the-digital-world.html', 'snippet': 'From mining to power to waste, there is a physical footprint in the online experience.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The center of the virtual world, economically and culturally, may still be the United States, but the work of creating that world leaves a real and growing footprint overseas. American technology giants have established data centers in about two dozen countries, and electronics manufacturers draw on materials fraught with ethical and environmental concerns. Those include the so-called conflict minerals — tin, tantalum and tungsten (and also gold) — as well as rare earth metals that are available almost exclusively from China. At the same time, the rapid cycle of obsolescence and replacement that feeds the expansion also produces a lot of garbage — nearly 42 million metric tons of toxic e-waste in 2014, less than a sixth of which made it into the regulated recycling stream, with much of the rest being broken down by workers at dumps throughout the developing world. Following are a few scenes from the digital life cycle.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '55', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/magazine/07ewastemap1/07ewastemap1-articleLarge-v5.jpg', 'height': 376, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/07/magazine/07ewastemap1/07ewastemap1-articleLarge-v5.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 376}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/magazine/07ewastemap1/07ewastemap1-jumbo-v5.jpg', 'height': 642, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/magazine/07ewastemap1/07ewastemap1-superJumbo-v5.jpg', 'height': 1284, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/magazine/07ewastemap1/07ewastemap1-thumbStandard-v5.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/07/magazine/07ewastemap1/07ewastemap1-thumbStandard-v5.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/magazine/07ewastemap1/07ewastemap1-thumbLarge-v5.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Making and Unmaking the Digital World', 'kicker': 'The Tech & Design Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cloud Atlas', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Benko, Jessica', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Waste Materials and Disposal', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Data Centers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rare Earths', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cloud Computing', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Third World and Developing Countries', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-05T09:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jessica Benko', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Benko', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': '