d', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ac0e2861-4b1c-5d65-b3a6-01da50e73fc2', 'word_count': 1095, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ac0e2861-4b1c-5d65-b3a6-01da50e73fc2'}, {'abstract': 'Merger Mania in Media Sector | Vodafone in Early Talks With Liberty Media on Asset Sale | Greece Puts Off Debt Payment | Bulgarian Man Accused of Making Fake Takeover Bids', 'web_url': 'https://news.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/05/morning-agenda-merger-mania-in-media-sector/', 'snippet': 'Merger Mania in Media Sector | Vodafone in Early Talks With Liberty Media on Asset Sale | Greece Puts Off Debt Payment | Bulgarian Man Accused of Making Fake Takeover Bids', 'lead_paragraph': 'MERGER MANIA IN MEDIA SECTOR | Faced with a future controlled by digital and mobile, media companies are on the hunt for acquisitions as they bet that a larger scale will help them survive the disruption to their sector,  Emily Steel and David Gelles write in DealBook. Dish Network and T-Mobile are the latest to seek a merger, according to people briefed on the discussions, in a year that has already seen deals in the global media and entertainment sector reach $155.3 billion. What’s driving the frantic pace of acquisitions is the rapidly changing habits of consumers, who are increasingly shunning cable television subscriptions in favor of streaming services. “Netflix and digital is pressuring the entire system,” said Anthony DiClemente, a Nomura media analyst. If Dish does acquire T-Mobile, one person briefed on the negotiations said that instead of trying to bundle a Dish satellite television subscription with a T-Mobile wireless package, the new company would focus on creating a network that can stream mobile video to smartphones and tablets. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Agenda: Merger Mania in Media Sector', '