source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘And Down the Stretch They Come!’ (All Rights Reserved)', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Famed Call at the Track, Punctuated With ! and ®', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horse Racing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trademarks and Trade Names', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Dave W (1943- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Collmus, Larry', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Patent and Trademark Office (US)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-05T14:32:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Sandomir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sandomir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7bb972e-72fd-5e5a-85c3-054b80189e67', 'word_count': 721, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7bb972e-72fd-5e5a-85c3-054b80189e67'}, {'abstract': 'Kent Haruf’s final novel traces a budding late-in-life relationship.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/books/review/our-souls-at-night-by-kent-haruf.html', 'snippet': 'Kent Haruf’s final novel traces a budding late-in-life relationship.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kent Haruf, who died in November at the age of 71, was best known for his justly praised novel “Plainsong” (1999). Haruf set all of his books in the fictional small town of Holt, Colo., integrating his bare-bones descriptions of the high plains so strikingly and crucially into his plots that setting is generally the first thing people mention about his work. But this emphasis can make Haruf sound parochial. In fact, his great subject was the struggle of decency against small-mindedness, and his rare gift was to make sheer decency a moving subject.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-Silber-SUB/07-Silber-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 447, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-Silber-SUB/07-Silber-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 447}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-Silber-SUB/07-Silber-SUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 762, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-Silber-SUB/07-Silber-SUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1049, 'width': 1409, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-Silber-SUB/07-Silber-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-Silber-SUB/07-Silber-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Our Souls at Night,’ by Kent Haruf', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Senior Moments', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Haruf, Kent (1943-2014)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-05T14:40:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joan Silber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/622d4a8c-0a1f-5901-96e9-c8260f788365', 'word_count': 925, 'uri': 'nyt://article/622d4a8c-0a1f-5901-96e9-c8260f788365'}, {'abstract': 'H. W. Brands’s new book is the latest attempt to capture the 40th president on the page.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/books/review/inventing-reagan.html', 'snippet': 'H. W. Brands’s new book is the latest attempt to capture the 40th president on the page.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A quarter-century after Ronald Reagan left the White House, he remains a beacon — and a mystery — to many Americans. This week, Jeff Shesol reviews a new biography by H.\u2009W. Brands, the latest attempt to capture the 40th president on the page.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-openbook/07-openbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 264, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-openbook/07-openbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 264}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-openbook/07-openbook-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 449, 'width': 1019, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-openbook/07-openbook-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 449, 'width': 1019, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-openbook/07-openbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-openbook/07-openbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-openbook/07-openbook-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inventing Reagan', 'kicker': 'Open Book', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inventing Reagan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-05T14:42:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b4c1d628-3da9-5aa3-8870-0c11cf6cb10a', 'word_count': 435, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4c1d628-3da9-5aa3-8870-0c11cf6cb10a'}, {'abstract': 'A painter and a gallerist enter an open relationship.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/books/review/paris-he-said-by-christine-sneed.html', 'snippet': 'A painter and a gallerist enter an open relationship.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If the American dream is a steady income, a home of one’s own and the promise of a better future for the kids, then the American Fantasy might be summed up in one word: Paris. (Paree!) A Parisian vision of romance, indulgence and beauty has resided in the less responsible quarter of our national psyche since Ben Franklin spent nine years there, wined and dined, wooing and wooed. Franklin, Hemingway, Stein — and Carrie Bradshaw, too. The imagined Parisian life is a heady one, so when the aspiring painter Jayne Marks, the central figure in Christine Sneed’s intricate new novel, “Paris, He Said,” is offered an all-expenses-paid existence in the City of Light by Laurent Moller, a handsome, elegant, older gallery owner who also happens to be her lover, how can she possibly turn him down?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Paris, He Said,’ by Christine Sneed', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Art Appreciation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Black, Robin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sneed, Christine', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-05T14:43:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Black', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Black', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2cee398d-587f-5c28-bbed-adb44c3c71d0', 'word_count': 641, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2cee398d-587f-5c28-bbed-adb44c3c71d0'}, {'abstract': 'Philip Glass recalls his Baltimore childhood and the friends and teachers who influenced him.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/books/review/words-without-music-by-philip-glass.html', 'snippet': 'Philip Glass recalls his Baltimore childhood and the friends and teachers who influenced him.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Given that all the events of a long, rich and full artistic life can scarcely be squeezed into a moderate-size book, how does one choose, at age 78, what to put into a memoir and what to leave out? The Promethean composer Philip Glass provokes this question in his lively and colorful new book, “Words Without Music,” in which he offers stories from his life in varyingly detailed magnification. Whatever you think of him as a \xadcomposer — self-\xadimitator or icon of postmodern symphony and opera — Glass is one of the most articulate composers around. Insight and practical common sense pervade his new book, and reading it reminded me of hearing him speak: He’s ever thoughtful and \xadloquacious, but he doesn’t answer any questions he doesn’t want to.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07subGANN/07subGANN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07subGANN/07subGANN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07subGANN/07subGANN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07subGANN/07subGANN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07subGANN/07subGANN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07subGANN/07subGANN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07subGANN/07subGANN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Words Without Music,’ by Philip Glass', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Self-Composed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Glass, Philip', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Words Without Music (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-05T14:43:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kyle Gann', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kyle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gann', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/572b4d1e-6b45-5e42-a9e5-c4a5903b96fc', 'word_count': 1044, 'uri': 'nyt://article/572b4d1e-6b45-5e42-a9e5-c4a5903b96fc'}, {'abstract': 'Few in baseball have been as fiery, and as misunderstood, as Billy Martin.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/books/review/billy-martin-baseballs-flawed-genius-by-bill-pennington.html', 'snippet': 'Few in baseball have been as fiery, and as misunderstood, as Billy Martin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'They were both All-Star Yankee infielders, won five championships apiece in their playing days, and have yet to be elected to baseball’s Hall of Fame (though one of them is surely on his way). Derek Jeter and Billy Martin share this much — and little else. Jeter was the game’s ice-cold ambassador. Martin, who may be the only manager to have thrown dirt at an umpire, was anything but. Jeter’s self-effacing persona positioned his success front and center. Martin’s histrionics nudged his achievements offstage, madness overshadowing the method. In “Billy Martin: Baseball’s Flawed Genius,” the New York Times sportswriter Bill Pennington gives long-overdue flesh to the caricature.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Billy Martin: Baseball’s Flawed Genius,’ by Bill Pennington', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Damn Yankee', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pennington, Bill', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Martin, Billy Jr (1928-89)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "Billy Martin: Baseball's Flawed Genius (Book)", 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-05T14:43:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maxwell Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maxwell', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2207863-b8b4-534e-9d86-71ad5efcaff7', 'word_count': 982, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2207863-b8b4-534e-9d86-71ad5efcaff7'}, {'abstract': 'A biography of Jonas Salk, the first man to develop a vaccine against polio and a popular hero.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/books/review/jonas-salk-a-life-by-charlotte-decroes-jacobs.html', 'snippet': 'A biography of Jonas Salk, the first man to develop a vaccine against polio and a popular hero.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nobody could recall a medical press conference quite like this one. On April 12, 1955, families huddled around radios, as if listening to the World Series or a championship fight. Crowds watched on television sets lining department store windows. Work stopped in offices and factories as word spread and tearful celebrations erupted. “Polio Is Conquered,” the headlines screamed. The vaccine developed by Jonas Salk, a 40-year-old University of Pittsburgh researcher, had been judged safe and effective following the largest public health experiment in our history.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07OSHINSKY/07oshinsky-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 766, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07OSHINSKY/07oshinsky-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 766}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07OSHINSKY/07oshinsky-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1019, 'width': 798, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07OSHINSKY/07oshinsky-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1019, 'width': 798, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07OSHINSKY/07oshinsky-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07OSHINSKY/07oshinsky-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07OSHINSKY/07oshinsky-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Jonas Salk: A Life,’ by Charlotte DeCroes Jacobs', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The People’s Scientist', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jacobs, Charlotte DeCroes (1946- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Salk, Jonas E', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Jonas Salk: A Life (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-05T14:45:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Oshinsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Oshinsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ec88b25-530b-5c52-819e-7505847c3057', 'word_count': 1363, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ec88b25-530b-5c52-819e-7505847c3057'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Pye sees the North Sea and its periphery as crucial to the development of Europe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/books/review/the-edge-of-the-world-by-michael-pye.html', 'snippet': 'Michael Pye sees the North Sea and its periphery as crucial to the development of Europe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michael Pye’s new book is bristling, wide-ranging and big-themed. It’s the sort of historical work whose thesis is virtually impossible to prove, but it’s also a reminder that history isn’t an exact science. At its most meaningful, history involves a good deal of art and storytelling. Pye’s book is full of both.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07SHORTO-sub/07SHORTO-1432670295556-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 638, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07SHORTO-sub/07SHORTO-1432670295556-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 638}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07SHORTO-sub/07SHORTO-1432670295556-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 963, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07SHORTO-sub/07SHORTO-1432670295556-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1639, 'width': 1541, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07SHORTO-sub/07SHORTO-1432670295556-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07SHORTO-sub/07SHORTO-1432670295556-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07SHORTO-sub/07SHORTO-1432670295556-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Edge of the World,’ by Michael Pye', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Northmen', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shorto, Russell', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pye, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-05T14:45:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Russell Shorto', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Russell', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shorto', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9829c9f-1bbc-5e53-9a76-4b48ee31e111', 'word_count': 1223, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9829c9f-1bbc-5e53-9a76-4b48ee31e111'}, {'abstract': 'In the early 1900s, Americans flocked to see the “African Pygmy.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/books/review/spectacle-the-astonishing-life-of-ota-benga-by-pamela-newkirk.html', 'snippet': 'In the early 1900s, Americans flocked to see the “African Pygmy.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The internment of “exotic” peoples in expositions and world fairs was no rare event in the 19th century, when Europeans swarmed Africa and other colonial lands in search of “subhumans” to display. Bodies, typically brown and black, were exhibited by scientists as well as impresarios like P.\u2009T. Barnum. In fact, Barnum’s first success was an 1830s attraction featuring Joice Heth, fictitiously billed as the 161-year-old “mammy” of George Washington.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07subWASHINGTON/07subWASHINGTON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 333, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07subWASHINGTON/07subWASHINGTON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 333}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07subWASHINGTON/07subWASHINGTON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 568, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07subWASHINGTON/07subWASHINGTON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 806, 'width': 1452, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07subWASHINGTON/07subWASHINGTON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07subWASHINGTON/07subWASHINGTON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07subWASHINGTON/07subWASHINGTON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Spectacle: The Astonishing Life of Ota Benga,’ by Pamela Newkirk', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Captivity', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Newkirk, Pamela', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Spectacle: The Astonishing Life of Ota Benga (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Benga, Ota', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-05T14:46:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Harriet A. Washington', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Harriet', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Washington', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/80c2f4b2-e5c1-5825-9f92-bac7a899477f', 'word_count': 580, 'uri': 'nyt://article/80c2f4b2-e5c1-5825-9f92-bac7a899477f'}, {'abstract': 'A novelist’s coming-of-age in 1960s and ’70s North Carolina.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/books/review/how-i-shed-my-skin-by-jim-grimsley.html', 'snippet': 'A novelist’s coming-of-age in 1960s and ’70s North Carolina.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Several years ago, the actress Nichelle Nichols, who played Lieutenant Uhura on “Star Trek,” recounted the story of meeting Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. She told King that she planned to leave “Star Trek,” and he persuaded her to stay. “Stop! You cannot!” she recalled him saying. “You are the first nonstereotypical role in television. .\u2008.\u2008. For the first time, not only our little children and people can look on and see themselves, but people who don’t look like us .\u2008.\u2008. from all over the world, for the first time, the first time on television, they can see us, as we should be.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘How I Shed My Skin,’ by Jim Grimsley', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'White Like Him', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grimsley, Jim', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-05T14:48:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randall Kenan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randall', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kenan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/784cd95e-3874-590d-bafc-9b4b1ae91368', 'word_count': 672, 'uri': 'nyt://article/784cd95e-3874-590d-bafc-9b4b1ae91368'}, {'abstract': 'The real-life scientific career of Lord Byron’s daughter provided the inspiration for this graphic novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/books/review/the-thrilling-adventures-of-lovelace-and-babbage-by-sydney-padua.html', 'snippet': 'The real-life scientific career of Lord Byron’s daughter provided the inspiration for this graphic novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the late 1970s, a French computer scientist under contract to the United States Defense Department developed Ada, a programming language for military computer systems. Today the language is widely used in “safety critical” settings: in the military, in banks and nuclear power plants, in medical devices and air traffic control. Ada was named for the woman who has been called the world’s first computer programmer, Augusta Ada Byron King, Countess of Lovelace \xad(1815-52), daughter of Lord Byron.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-Redniss/07-Redniss-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 568, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-Redniss/07-Redniss-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 568}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-Redniss/07-Redniss-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 969, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-Redniss/07-Redniss-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1465, 'width': 1548, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-Redniss/07-Redniss-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-Redniss/07-Redniss-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-Redniss/07-Redniss-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Thrilling Adventures of Lovelace and Babbage,’ by Sydney Padua', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Steampunk Computing ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Padua, Sydney', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-05T14:49:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lauren Redniss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lauren', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Redniss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/85500e4d-9041-5125-b932-e1c0a4ab6073', 'word_count': 998, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85500e4d-9041-5125-b932-e1c0a4ab6073'}, {'abstract': 'Lives in the U.S. are turned upside down by the Vietnam War in this debut novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/books/review/the-given-world-by-marian-palaia.html', 'snippet': 'Lives in the U.S. are turned upside down by the Vietnam War in this debut novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The story of Riley, the heroine of “The Given World” — Marian Palaia’s ardent, ambitious debut novel — might sound familiar at first. It has all the elements of a particular kind of post-60s American \xadcoming-of-age story: Farm kid leaves home for the big city (in Riley’s case, rural Montana is abandoned for San Francisco) and dives headlong into adulthood, with all of its requisite heartbreak. Drugs are ingested, Janis Joplin is played on a jukebox, and many drives are taken through the countryside on motorcycles or a beat-up Mustang. But “The Given World” contains what feels like 12 times the usual dose of tragedy within its pages, because Riley and her generation come of age poisoned by the Vietnam War. They are its secondhand casualties; the war has upended their world and rewritten its rules of gravity, so that they fall hard and fall often.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Given World,’ by Marian Palaia', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Secondhand Casualties', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Palaia, Marian M (1955- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-05T14:51:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Violet Kupersmith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Violet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kupersmith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/103fffc2-e347-5c36-949c-a8b501fe125b', 'word_count': 588, 'uri': 'nyt://article/103fffc2-e347-5c36-949c-a8b501fe125b'}, {'abstract': 'A teenager in medieval England becomes a religious hermit.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/books/review/the-anchoress-by-robyn-cadwallader.html', 'snippet': 'A teenager in medieval England becomes a religious hermit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Earlier this year, three British-born Muslim girls, ages 15 and 16, ran away from London to Syria to join ISIS. What could possibly have attracted them to a sect of fundamentalist thugs who use religion to justify violence and misogyny? Might the rebellious, hormonal storm of their age be partly to blame? Robyn Cadwallader, probably didn’t have ISIS in mind when she sat down to write “The Anchoress,” her affecting novel about a teenage girl walled up in a village in 1255 England, but the parallels are there if one chooses to consider them. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-bks-Dunant/07-bks-Dunant-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 934, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-bks-Dunant/07-bks-Dunant-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 934}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-bks-Dunant/07-bks-Dunant-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 658, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-bks-Dunant/07-bks-Dunant-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1049, 'width': 674, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-bks-Dunant/07-bks-Dunant-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07-bks-Dunant/07-bks-Dunant-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtyp