75724e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/19cecdc4-6694-5de5-9b88-fcb8b275724e'}, {'abstract': 'This novella is a thinly — or not so thinly — veiled account of the Dominique Strauss-Kahn affair.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/books/review/coup-de-foudre-by-ken-kalfus.html', 'snippet': 'This novella is a thinly — or not so thinly — veiled account of the Dominique Strauss-Kahn affair.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You can always count on a murderer for a fancy prose style, as Nabokov’s Humbert Humbert admitted, and in his spectacular novella “Coup de Foudre,” Ken Kalfus has created an equally articulate monster. First appearing in Harper’s and collected now with his latest short stories, “Coup de Foudre” is a thinly — or possibly barely — veiled account of the Dominique Strauss-Kahn affair, in which Strauss-Kahn was accused of sexually assaulting a housekeeper at a New York City hotel. The criminal charges, which led to the disclosure of a hidden world of sex parties and erotic compulsion, came to nothing. But Strauss-Kahn eventually paid a hefty fine after a civil action, and his reputation was ruined, perhaps because of the question that was impossible to ignore: “What made him do it?” What would cause the managing director of the International Monetary Fund, wealthy and successful beyond measure, to risk everything in such a fashion? ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Coup de Foudre,’ by Ken Kalfus', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Charming Monster', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kalfus, Ken', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-05T14:53:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Sean Greer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'Sean', 'lastname': 'Greer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d925cb8-0845-5532-819d-89603bcfd1ce', 'word_count': 591, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d925cb8-0845-5532-819d-89603bcfd1ce'}, {'abstract': 'A Turkish photographer must confront the past when his grandfather leaves the family home to an Armenian woman. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/books/review/orhans-inheritance-by-aline-ohanesian.html', 'snippet': 'A Turkish photographer must confront the past when his grandfather leaves the family home to an Armenian woman. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'Orhan Turkoglu, the protagonist of Aline Ohanesian’s first novel, stands to gain a lot when his 93-year-old grandfather, Kemal, dies in 1990. But although the old man’s will grants him the family’s thriving rug business, their home in Anatolia is, to everyone’s surprise, left to a stranger, a woman named Seda Melkonian, now 90 and living in a nursing home in Los Angeles. Wasting little time, Orhan is soon boarding a flight to California, intent on confronting this mystery woman. Who is she, and what was she to Kemal?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07TEPPER/07TEPPER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 839, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07TEPPER/07TEPPER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 839}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07TEPPER/07TEPPER-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 899, 'width': 643, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07TEPPER/07TEPPER-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 899, 'width': 643, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07TEPPER/07tepper-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07TEPPER/07tepper-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07TEPPER/07tepper-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Orhan’s Inheritance,’ by Aline Ohanesian', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inherited Grief', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ohanesian, Aline', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-05T14:53:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anderson Tepper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anderson', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tepper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/656cb612-9629-5351-9bcd-60f79f35f223', 'word_count': 589, 'uri': 'nyt://article/656cb612-9629-5351-9bcd-60f79f35f223'}, {'abstract': 'The dashing protagonist of Goddard’s historical espionage thriller is a refreshing throwback to an earlier romantic tradition of heroes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/books/review/robert-goddards-the-ways-of-the-world-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'The dashing protagonist of Goddard’s historical espionage thriller is a refreshing throwback to an earlier romantic tradition of heroes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the company of fictional detectives who emerged from their wartime experiences shellshocked and haunted, former Lt. James (Max) Maxted, the dashing protagonist of Robert Goddard’s historical espionage thriller THE WAYS OF THE WORLD (Mysterious Press, $25), is a refreshing throwback to an earlier romantic tradition of heroes. Max was an ace fighter pilot with the Royal Flying Corps in World War I, and having had what the British call “a good war,” he’s not content to settle down on his family’s ancestral estate now that it’s over. But Max’s dreams of starting a flying school are dashed when his diplomat father, Sir Henry Maxted, falls to his death while in Paris as a member of the British delegation laboring over the Treaty of Versailles.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '37', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07CRIME/07-Crime-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07CRIME/07-Crime-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 599}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07CRIME/07-Crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1023, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07CRIME/07-Crime-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2045, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07CRIME/07CRIME-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07CRIME/07CRIME-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/books/review/07CRIME/07CRIME-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Robert Goddard’s ‘The Ways of the World,’ and More', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'French Connections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goddard, Robert', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harvey, Michael', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McPherson, Catriona (1965- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miller, Jax (Author)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-05T14:54:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/be508107-0a86-59f2-b3e2-468235d7a0d8', 'word_count': 871, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be508107-0a86-59f2-b3e2-468235d7a0d8'}, {'abstract': 'Chuck Palahniuk, whose “Make Something Up” is No. 8 on the hardcover fiction list, is also promoting his first graphic novel, a sequel to his 1996 novel “Fight Club.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/14/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Chuck Palahniuk, whose “Make Something Up” is No. 8 on the hardcover fiction list, is also promoting his first graphic novel, a sequel to his 1996 novel “Fight Club.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Save the Ducklings: Chuck Palahniuk — who remains best known for the 1996 novel “Fight Club,” despite a string of subsequent hits — has another hardcover best seller with his first story collection, “Make Something Up,” new at No.\u202f8. There’s no mistaking Palahniuk’s sense of plotting. Here’s how Robert Brink of the website The Hundreds sums up the collection: “So this one kid, he’s pledging for a frat and drops acid, the Hello Kitty kind, right before he’s about to be a contestant on ‘The Price Is Right.’ And this other guy, he slaps his prostate-cancer-\xadridden, morphine-\xadpickled father square in the face with a pie as he’s dying in his hospital bed. And then there’s these high school kids who begin to lobotomize themselves with cardiac defibrillators because they discovered the key to true happiness.” Yep, that’s Palahniuk. Even casual fans, though, may be interested to know that “Make Something Up” is not the only new book Palahniuk is promoting — he’s also written his first graphic novel, and it’s a sequel bearing the straightforward title “Fight Club 2.” In an interview with The Wrap, he explained he needed some distance before he felt ready to revisit this story. “If I followed it up too soon,” he said, “my whole career would be writing progressively less interesting sequels to ‘Fight Club.’\u200a” He chose the comics format, he said, because “if it was written as a novel or a film, it’d be compared to previous versions. .\u2008.\u2008. Certain things work in comics because there’s a slight aspect of unreality.” Given his reputation for disturbing readers, Palahniuk was asked, what makes him personally squeamish? “I saw a headline about animal abuse recently,” he replied. “\u200a‘Man Runs Over Ducklings With Lawnmower.’ That kind of stuff.”'