 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hilary Moss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hilary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90c811fa-2594-56af-b394-b36f93494012', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90c811fa-2594-56af-b394-b36f93494012'}, {'abstract': 'Curtis spent 30 years on his remarkably ambitious project of photographing all the remaining Indian tribes in North America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/travel/photographer-edward-s-curtiss-southwest.html', 'snippet': 'Curtis spent 30 years on his remarkably ambitious project of photographing all the remaining Indian tribes in North America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A wind knifed high across the desert, bounded up and over the mesa top and side-winded through the alleys of Acoma Pueblo, pummeling us. We shuffled toward the centuries-old San Estevan del Rey Mission Church, whose portals lay mercifully open. Acoma, also known as Sky City, sits atop a 367-foot-high sandstone bluff in western New Mexico, and is utterly exposed to the elements. A bunch of us had come up from the Sky City Cultural Center, 30 tourists clinging to one another’s sleeves, angling our torsos into the wind. At one point I was almost horizontal as I clenched an older woman’s hand, hanging on for dear life.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Photographer Edward S. Curtis’s Southwest', 'kicker': 'Footsteps', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Tireless Witness to Lost Worlds', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Curtis, Edward Sheri