we watch those ballets, we can still believe him. (“Symphony in Three” was danced early in the New York City Ballet season that ends this weekend. It and “Serenade” return to repertory next May.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/06/arts/06NYCB/06NYCB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/06/arts/06NYCB/06NYCB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/06/arts/06NYCB/06NYCB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/06/arts/06NYCB/06NYCB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/06/arts/06NYCB/06NYCB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/06/arts/06NYCB/06NYCB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/06/arts/06NYCB/06NYCB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Lesson for New York City Ballet: The Past Is Always Present', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ballet, Where the Past Is Always Present ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Peck, Justin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Ballet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-05T22:13:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b6df35b-2c53-5dc9-a033-5394e7e4dcdd', 'word_count': 1114, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b6df35b-2c53-5dc9-a033-5394e7e4dcdd'}, {'abstract': 'The drama shifts gears to focus on the female leads, but it might be best to take a wait-and-see attitude.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/06/arts/television/halt-and-catch-fire-on-amc-switches-gears-in-its-new-season.html', 'snippet': 'The drama shifts gears to focus on the female leads, but it might be best to take a wait-and-see attitude.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Early in the new season of “Halt and Catch Fire,” AMC’s drama about daddy issues, 1980s home décor and the beginnings of the computer industry, the action takes place mostly in a beat-up Dallas house furnished with water guns and pizza crusts. It’s the headquarters of Mutiny, a nascent company offering early versions of online gaming and chat rooms, whose corporate structure consists of two more or less mature women trying to wrangle a herd of childish young men — Prodigy crossed with “Animal House.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/06/arts/06HALT/06HALT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 432, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/0