ri': 'nyt://article/7ae7f529-2663-5d6a-83bb-af61470146a8'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/06/arts/television/whats-on-tv-saturday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (MAX) BLADE RUNNER (1982) Rick Deckard (Harrison Ford) is a blade runner — an agent tasked with eliminating, or “retiring,” rogue robots, or replicants. Deckard struggles with emotional connections to one replicant as he dangerously engages with others. Ridley Scott directed this gritty and ethereal noir. “Science-fiction devotees may find ‘Blade Runner’ a wonderfully meticulous movie and marvel at the comprehensiveness of its vision,” Janet Maslin wrote in The New York Times. “Even those without a taste for gadgetry cannot fail to appreciate the degree of effort that has gone into constructing a film so ambitious and idiosyncratic.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on TV Saturday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On TV Saturday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-06T05:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alec M. Priester', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alec', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/83310913-1ec7-565e-a244-fcc3aed75337', 'word_count': 725, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83310913-1ec7-565e-a244-fcc3aed75337'}, {'abstract': 'The restaurant features dishes from the Argentine city its owner hails from, and its décor reflects Mendoza’s status as the capital of the nation’s wine country.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/nyregion/a-heartfelt-letter-home.html', 'snippet': 'The restaurant features dishes from the Argentine city its owner hails from, and its décor reflects Mendoza’s status as the capital of the nation’s wine country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s impossible to be anonymous when the owner of a restaurant greets you with a smile and a big hug. This happened to me recently at Café Mendoza in Commack. The person doing the hugging was Hugo Garcia, the owner of the almost four-month-old restaurant. Mr. Garcia, along with his business partner, Fabio Machado, also owns Café Buenos Aires and Bistro Cassis in Huntington as well as Bistro Citron in Roslyn and Manhattan.', 'print_section': 'LI', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/nyregion/07DINELI2-3COMBO/07DINELI2-3COMBO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/07/nyregion/07DINELI2-3COMBO/07DINELI2-3COMBO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/nyregion/07DINELI2-3COMBO/07DINELI2-3COMBO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/nyregion/07DINELI2-3COMBO/07DINELI2-3COMBO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/nyregion/07DINELI2-3COMBO/07DINELI2-3COMBO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/07/nyregion/07DINELI2-3COMBO/07DINELI2-3COMBO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/nyregion/07DINELI2-3COMBO/07DINELI2-3COMBO-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Heartfelt Letter Home ', 'kicker': 'Dining Review | Long Island', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Heartfelt Letter Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Commack (NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cafe Mendoza (Commack, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-06T05:09:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanne Starkey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Starkey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/db56c963-4f3a-5d1b-8fef-0e0bf0d215fe', 'word_count': 631, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db56c963-4f3a-5d1b-8fef-0e0bf0d215fe'}, {'abstract': 'Crawford, Chicago’s top goalie, has recovered after being benched in three games against the Nashville Predators.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/06/sports/hockey/with-first-round-difficulties-forgotten-blackhawks-corey-crawford-rights-himself-for-the-finals.html', 'snippet': 'Crawford, Chicago’s top goalie, has recovered after being benched in three games against the Nashville Predators.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TAMPA, Fla. — The mood was light around Corey Crawford’s locker stall on Friday. He laughed along with the banter and joked with Chicago television reporters about their fresh Florida suntans. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/06/sports/hockey/06cup/06cup-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 456, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/06/sports/hockey/06cup/06cup-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 456}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/06/sports/hockey/06cup/06cup-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 779, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/06/sports/hockey/06cup/06cup-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1558, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/06/sports/hockey/06cup/06cup-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/06/sports/hockey/06cup/06cup-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/06/sports/hockey/06cup/06cup-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'With First-Round Difficulties Forgotten, Blackhawks’ Corey Crawford Rights Himself for the Finals', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'With First-Round Difficulties Forgotten, Blackhawks&#8217; Corey Crawford Rights Himself for the Finals', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stanley Cup', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chicago Blackhawks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Crawford, Corey', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-06T05:11:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tom Spousta', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Spousta', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/74b300d8-a244-5a66-b264-d73092fd6360', 'word_count': 748, 'uri': 'nyt://article/74b300d8-a244-5a66-b264-d73092fd6360'}, {'abstract': 'You call this a debate?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/06/views-differ-on-shape-of-macroeconomics-2/', 'snippet': 'You call this a debate?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The doctrine of expansionary austerity — the claim that slashing spending would actually boost demand and employment, because it would have such positive effects on confidence that this would outweigh the direct drag — was immensely popular among policymakers in 2010, as the great turn toward austerity began. But the statistical underpinnings of the doctrine fell apart under scrutiny: the methods Alberto Alesina used to identify changes in fiscal policy did not, it turned out, do a very good job, and more careful work found that historically austerity has in fact been contractionary after all. Moreover, the experience of austerity programs seemed to confirm what Keynesians new and old had warned from the beginning — that the negative effects of austerity are much larger under conditions where they cannot be offset by conventional monetary policy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Views Differ on Shape of Macroeconomics', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-06-06T05:55:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/daa07195-f155-5904-92e2-53601ca213d0', 'word_count': 459, 'uri': 'nyt://article/daa07195-f155-5904-92e2-53601ca213d0'}, {'abstract': 'I’m happily remarried, but I didn’t stop loving my first husband when he died.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/06/i-am-a-bigamist/', 'snippet': 'I’m happily remarried, but I didn’t stop loving my first husband when he died.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among the many popular misconceptions about grief and its trajectory, one that particularly rankles widowed people is the assumption that if you’ve found a new love relationship, you’ve “moved on” from your grief. How very tidy.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/opinion/opinionator-theend-series-icon/opinionator-theend-series-icon-articleLarge.png', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/30/opinion/opinionator-theend-series-icon/opinionator-theend-series-icon-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/opinion/opinionator-theend-series-icon/opinionator-theend-series-icon-jumbo.png', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/opinion/opinionator-theend-series-icon/opinionator-theend-series-icon-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/opinion/opinionator-theend-series-icon/opinionator-theend-series-icon-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/30/opinion/opinionator-theend-series-icon/opinionator-theend-series-icon-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/opinion/opinionator-theend-series-icon/opinionator-theend-series-icon-thumbLarge-v2.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'I Am a Bigamist', 'kicker': 'The End', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'I Am a Bigamist', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Death and Dying', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marriages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-06T06:30:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jill Smolowe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smolowe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8c1e6d8f-62bd-53f4-8384-e889efaa1722', 'word_count': 1082, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8c1e6d8f-62bd-53f4-8384-e889efaa1722'}, {'abstract': 'It knows when you’ve been sleeping, it knows when you’re awake.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/06/the-truth-about-ai-silly/', 'snippet': 'It knows when you’ve been sleeping, it knows when you’re awake.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Izabella Kaminska, at a meeting about the future of artificial intelligence, muses/jokes that the AI might already be here, but choosing not to show itself. She’s not watching the right TV shows! That’s the premise of the terrific Person of Interest. Here’s Root (Amy Acker), a hacker temporarily confined to a mental health facility but still in contact with the Machine:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Truth About AI (Silly)', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-06-06T09:37:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3df5a3fd-1262-5996-8b4d-8e9fc16db806', 'word_count': 61, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3df5a3fd-1262-5996-8b4d-8e9fc16db806'}, {'abstract': 'Foster Huntington was an up-and-comer in the New York fashion industry. Then he ditched it all and built his own personal paradise in the sky.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/style/escape-to-bro-topia.html', 'snippet': 'Foster Huntington was an up-and-comer in the New York fashion industry. Then he ditched it all and built his own personal paradise in the sky.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHOUGAL, WASH. — People talk about chucking their jobs. They say they will leave behind the madness of the city and hit the road.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/07/fashion/07TREEHOUSE1/07TREEHOUSE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'le