me': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Trip Gabriel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Trip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gabriel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4db9af79-d00d-55f6-ad9e-6762f49a89a1', 'word_count': 894, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4db9af79-d00d-55f6-ad9e-6762f49a89a1'}, {'abstract': 'Luna will play the Northside Festival, the band’s first New York show in a decade, at Brooklyn’s version of South by Southwest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/07/arts/music/northside-festival-features-lunas-return-and-other-indie-rock.html', 'snippet': 'Luna will play the Northside Festival, the band’s first New York show in a decade, at Brooklyn’s version of South by Southwest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Northside Festival, Brooklyn’s emulation of South by Southwest, continues to expand. Its core is still the indie-rock clubs of Williamsburg and Greenpoint and the local bands that play them. But it’s also attracting headliners with bigger names. Its opening-night concert, Thursday at McCarren Park, features the return — for its first New York show after a 10-year hiatus — of Luna, a band that carried the Velvet Underground legacy of drone-rock and deadpan insight from 1991-2004. There are also big showcases at 50 Kent Street — a.k.a. Live at the Inlet — with Neko Case and Rhye on Friday, Best Coast and Built to Spill on Saturday, and Run the Jewels and Sleigh Bells on Sunday. The Nigerian Afrobeat band Femi Kuti and the Positive Force visits Brooklyn Bowl on Thursday. But in a festival with hundreds of bands, leave time for lesser-knowns like Jacco Gardner, a Dutch psychedelic-pop revivalist, on Thursday at Rough Trade, or the Minimalist composer Delia Gonzalez next Sunday at Manhattan Inn. (northsidefestival.com.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xla