'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-07T19:00:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kareem Fahim', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kareem', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fahim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/454e7391-909d-520d-8b0d-6f435b31ebff', 'word_count': 396, 'uri': 'nyt://article/454e7391-909d-520d-8b0d-6f435b31ebff'}, {'abstract': 'When protesters succeeded in ousting President Ali Abdullah Saleh in 2012, it seemed especially promising for women. But that hope has unraveled.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/12/world/middleeast/yemen-trials-of-spring.html', 'snippet': 'When protesters succeeded in ousting President Ali Abdullah Saleh in 2012, it seemed especially promising for women. But that hope has unraveled.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO — When protesters in Yemen succeeded in ousting President Ali Abdullah Saleh in 2012, their success stirred hopes for change in a place that had suffered mightily from poverty and corruption for decades under his autocratic rule.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Yemen, a Brief Moment Before Women Were Pushed Aside Again', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Yemen', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Women's Rights", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-07T19:00:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kareem Fahim', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kareem', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fahim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca46fca6-1078-5c88-bcf2-3f320f94257a', 'word_count': 399, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca46fca6-1078-5c88-bcf2-3f320f94257a'}, {'abstract': 'Before the revolution, Ghazala Mhamdi led the fight for jobs, freedom and human rights, and suffered for it. But when democracy arrived, she ran for Parliament.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/08/world/africa/tunisia-trials-of-spring.html', 'snippet': 'Before the revolution, Ghazala Mhamdi led the fight for jobs, freedom and human rights, and suffered for it. But when democracy arrived, she ran for Parliament.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TUNIS — In December 2010 a fruit-vendor in Tunisia, Mohamed Bouazizi, set himself alight in a protest against the police who stopped him from setting up his stall and earning a daily wage. His act of self-immolation — he died three weeks later — triggered a popular uprising around the country, where thousands of Tunisians seized on Mr. Bouazizi’s singular act and took to the streets to stand against state oppression and government corruption.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Making Herself Heard, Before and After Tunisia’s Uprising', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tunisia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Women's Rights", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ben Ali, Zine El-Abidine', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-07T19:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name':