ml', 'snippet': 'A report by The Chronicle of Higher Education found that most leaders received housing and cars, and some got perks like personal trainers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even with college costs and student debt in the national spotlight, the pay packages — and especially the benefits — of public university presidents continue to grow. The median salary for public university presidents was $428,250 in the 2014 fiscal year, up about 7 percent from the previous year, according to an annual survey by The Chronicle of Higher Education.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Benefits Grow for Public University Presidents, Survey Finds', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Perks Grow for Presidents of Colleges, Survey Finds', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chronicle of Higher Education', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executive Compensation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pennsylvania State University', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Texas A&M University', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ohio State University', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Erickson, Rodney A', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-07T22:01:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tamar Lewin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tamar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lewin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f4eff335-fae9-5911-9e94-074f1ba8