 'pub_date': '2015-06-08T09:17:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Forsythe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Forsythe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/111b9397-043b-5741-a7c8-e72dd16ece0b', 'word_count': 232, 'uri': 'nyt://article/111b9397-043b-5741-a7c8-e72dd16ece0b'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Miles, who won a Tony Award for her performance, sings “Something Wonderful” from “The King and I,” which also won the Tony for best musical revival.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/08/in-performance-ruthie-ann-miles-of-the-king-and-i/', 'snippet': 'Ms. Miles, who won a Tony Award for her performance, sings “Something Wonderful” from “The King and I,” which also won the Tony for best musical revival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week’s video features a song from Ruthie Ann Miles, who won the Tony Award on Sunday for best featured actress in a musical for her role as Lady Thiang in Lincoln Center Theater’s revival of “The King and I.” Here she sings the ballad “Something Wonderful” with Ted Sperling, the show’s music director, on piano. “The King and I,” which won the Tony for best musical revival, is at Lincoln Center’s Vivian Beaumont Theater.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Performance: Ruthie Ann Miles of ‘The King and I’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tony Awards (Theater Awards)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miles, Ruthie Ann', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sperling, Ted', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-08T09:24:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Piepenburg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Piepenburg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f5d125c-c033-5622-8232-c4eba28c8b75', 'word_count': 124, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f5d125c-c033-5622-8232-c4eba28c8b75'}, {'abstract': 'Threats, real and imagined, loom in the lives of restive New Yorkers. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/14/books/review/the-sunken-cathedral-by-kate-walbert.html', 'snippet': 'Threats, real and imagined, loom in the lives of restive New Yorkers. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kate Walbert’s new novel, her fourth, begins and ends amid high winds and storm surge, a lethal deluge that the book’s characters — post-9/11, post-Sandy New Yorkers — endure with nervous resignation. Walbert is in her element. She’s an anatomist of disaster, or, more precisely, of its psychic toll, of the myriad subtle ways calamities — natural, man-made, self-\xadinflicted — reverberate through lives and across generations. Her style is understated, elegiac, her perspective oblique. In her books, war and social crisis typically lurk just off the page; what she’s after is quieter, more elusive stuff: the collateral damage of haunted minds and thwarted ambition. Her characters, you might say, are afflicted by history.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14-Eagan/14-Eagan-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 192, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14-Eagan/14-Eagan-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 192}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14-Eagan/14-Eagan-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 328, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14-Eagan/14-Eagan-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 657, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14-Eagan/14-Eagan-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14-Eagan/14-Eagan-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14-Eagan/14-Eagan-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Sunken Cathedral,’ by Kate Walbert', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'High Alerters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Walbert, Kate', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-08T09:30:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily Eakin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eakin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/06e1bceb-58a8-5f2b-bd4c-b73961b89283', 'word_count': 1108, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06e1bceb-58a8-5f2b-bd4c-b73961b89283'}, {'abstract': 'An elephant trainer becomes an apprentice to the Ottoman Empire’s chief architect. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/14/books/review/the-architects-apprentice-by-elif-shafak.html', 'snippet': 'An elephant trainer becomes an apprentice to the Ottoman Empire’s chief architect. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you happen to be born in an Indian village in the 16th century and end up on a ship sailing for the court of the Ottoman ruler Suleiman the Magnificent accompanied by a pet albino elephant named \xadChota, you had better be quick — on your feet and in your mind. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14subATAMIAN/14subATAMIAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 791, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14subATAMIAN/14subATAMIAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 791}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14subATAMIAN/14subATAMIAN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 777, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14subATAMIAN/14subATAMIAN-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2000, 'width': 1517, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14subATAMIAN/14subATAMIAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14subATAMIAN/14subATAMIAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14subATAMIAN/14subATAMIAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Architect’s Apprentice,’ by Elif Shafak', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Monuments Men', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shafak, Elif', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "The Architect's Apprentice (Book)", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-08T09:30:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Atamian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Atamian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a3e723d-266c-58ba-b885-8a9a2037d106', 'word_count': 560, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a3e723d-266c-58ba-b885-8a9a2037d106'}, {'abstract': 'Kamel Daoud’s inventive novel looks at Albert Camus’s “The Stranger” with new eyes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/14/books/review/the-meursault-investigation-by-kamel-daoud.html', 'snippet': 'Kamel Daoud’s inventive novel looks at Albert Camus’s “The Stranger” with new eyes.', 'lead_paragraph': ' When I was 15, a shy and bookish sophomore at a high school in Morocco, my French class was assigned Albert Camus’s “The Stranger.” I remember being intrigued by the fact that the story was set in neighboring Algeria. I remember the novel’s indelible first line: “Mother died today.” I remember what an odd hero Meursault seemed to me, unabashed and remorseless, a man who shows no emotion at his mother’s funeral. And I still remember how distressing it was to come upon the crucial scene in which Meursault, walking on a beach under the midday sun, shoots a nameless Arab. Our class discussed Meursault, his mother, his fight with the priest, existentialism and the absurd. All the while, I had to quiet the voice inside me that kept asking, “But what about the Arab?” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14LALAMISUB/14-Lalami-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14LALAMISUB/14-Lalami-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14LALAMISUB/14-Lalami-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 769, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14LALAMISUB/14-Lalami-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1538, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14LALAMISUB/14LALAMISUB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14LALAMISUB/14LALAMISUB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14LALAMISUB/14LALAMI