 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/042b93f6-6665-512c-b202-62b7ac47ccb8', 'word_count': 1009, 'uri': 'nyt://article/042b93f6-6665-512c-b202-62b7ac47ccb8'}, {'abstract': 'Hundreds are officers, but there’s no telling when they will be eligible for command.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/09/opinion/bina-shah-high-can-pakistans-air-force-women-fly.html', 'snippet': 'Hundreds are officers, but there’s no telling when they will be eligible for command.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KARACHI, Pakistan — Flight Lt. Ayesha Farooq, Pakistan’s only combat-ready female air force pilot, has become both an international celebrity and a symbol of a new Pakistan, where women are breaking barriers and taking on roles traditionally closed to them. Yet Pakistan is also known as a country where women’s place in society yo-yos up and down. For example, in the 1990s it entrusted the leadership of the entire nation to Benazir Bhutto while still resisting girls’ education and advances in women’s rights.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/10/04/opinion/shah-contributor/shah-contributor-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 940, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/10/04/opinion/shah-contributor/shah-contributor-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 940}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/10/04/opinion/shah-contributor/shah-contributor-jumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 654, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/10/04/opinion/shah-contributor/shah-contributor-superJumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1307, 'subType'