8jpDB-Deutsche/08jpDB-Deutsche-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/08/business/08jpDB-Deutsche/08jpDB-Deutsche-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/08/business/08jpDB-Deutsche/08jpDB-Deutsche-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Anshu Jain’s Painful Lessons for New Deutsche Boss', 'kicker': 'Breakingviews', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Deutsche Bank AG', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jain, Anshu', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cryan, John (1960- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-08T14:44:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dominic Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dominic', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0c08d76-19b0-54bc-b73c-ffaf6506d596', 'word_count': 381, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0c08d76-19b0-54bc-b73c-ffaf6506d596'}, {'abstract': 'A look at three very different lives: Lewis Carroll’s, Alice Liddell’s and that of the literary creation they both had a part in.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/14/books/review/the-story-of-alice-lewis-carroll-and-the-secret-history-of-wonderland.html', 'snippet': 'A look at three very different lives: Lewis Carroll’s, Alice Liddell’s and that of the literary creation they both had a part in.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lewis Carroll loved puns, like those about the tortoise who taught us, or the lessons that lessen the need for any more of them. He especially loved puns that could be found hiding in one word, like wonderland: a place full of wonders and a place where you wonder what is happening to you. These meanings are not only different, they can be read as opposites, as Robert \xadDouglas-Fairhurst reminds us, quoting a Victorian evocation of a person “who, being in a chronic state of wonder, is surprised at nothing.” Sentimental memories of the Alice books often stress the first, dazzled meaning, but a fresh reading of Carroll is likely to leave us much closer to the second, which Douglas-Fairhurst neatly formulates as “being puzzled at what we do not know.” This is how the flowers talk in “Through the Looking-Glass”: “I wonder how you do it,” a rose says to \xadAlice, referring to her odd human ability to move about. “You’re always wondering,” an irritated tiger-lily says.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14WOOD/14WOOD-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 580, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14WOOD/14WOOD-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 580}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14WOOD/14WOOD-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 989, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14WOOD/14WOOD-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1429, 'width': 1479, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumb