ongtime experience to give readers a portrait of the literary world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/09/books/review-in-muse-the-publisher-jonathan-galassi-writes-what-he-knows.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Galassi, president of Farrar, Straus and Giroux, draws on his longtime experience to give readers a portrait of the literary world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jonathan Galassi’s keenly observed debut novel, “Muse,” is a carefully constructed literary echo chamber, haunted by the ghosts of two classics and peopled with some thinly veiled portraits of well-known publishing figures. As in Philip Roth’s “The Ghost Writer,” a young man with literary aspirations — in this case, an editor instead of a writer — finds himself torn between two rival father figures. As in Henry James’s “The Aspern Papers,” an editor obsessed with a famous poet goes to Venice in search of some answers about the poet and the poet’s lover and has a surprising encounter that will affect both his career and his apprehension of himself. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/09/arts/09BOOKGALASSI/09BOOKGALASSI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 848, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/09/arts/09BOOKGALASSI/09BOOKGALASSI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 848}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/09/arts/09BOOKGALASSI/09BOOKGALASSI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 725, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/09/arts/09BOOKGALASSI/09BOOKGALASSI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1450, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/09/arts/09BOOKGALASSI/09BOOKGALASSI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/09/arts/09BOOKGALASSI/09BOOKGALASSI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/09/arts/09BOOKGALASSI/09BOOKGALASSI-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: In ‘Muse,’ the Publisher Jonathan Galassi Writes What He Knows', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Publishing Rivals, Feuding Over a Poet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Galassi, Jonathan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Farrar, Straus & Giroux', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Muse (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-08T19:20:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michiko Kakutani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michiko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kakutani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e544ae27-2f3b-5ba3-8738-dc1fc45e28f4', 'word_count': 730, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e544ae27-2f3b-5ba3-8738-dc1fc45e28f4'}, {'abstract': 'This annual summer concert was hampered by a modest lineup onstage and a face-off between police and concertgoers outside MetLife Stadium.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/09/arts/music/review-only-suspense-at-summer-jam-was-outside-the-stadium.html', 'snippet': 'This annual summer concert was hampered by a modest lineup onstage and a face-off between police and concertgoers outside MetLife Stadium.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EAST RUTHERFORD, N.J. — It seems that every year of late, the annual Summer Jam presented by the radio station Hot 97 has to find new purpose. In the 1990s and into the mid-2000s, it was the New York hip-hop concert of record. Now it is a showcase for the genre’s scattered regional identities and Hot 97’s decreasingly steady footing as an arbiter of its future. Summer Jam was once the essential proving ground; now it’s a hit-or-miss revue.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/09/arts/09SUMMERJAMSUB/09SUMMERJAMSUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/09/arts/09SUMMERJAMSUB/09SUMMERJAMSUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/09/arts/09SUMMERJAMSUB/09SUMMERJAMSUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/09/arts/09SUMMERJAMSUB/09SUMMERJAMSUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/09/arts/09SUMMERJAMSUB/09SUMMERJAMSUB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/09/arts/09SUMMERJAMSUB/09SUMMERJAMSUB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}