-tuesday-june-9-2015.html', 'snippet': 'Quotation of the Day for Tuesday, June 9, 2015.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Now the government is belatedly trying to mend the fence after the cow had already been stolen.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Quotation of the Day for Tuesday, June 9, 2015', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quotation of the Day', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-06-09T04:32:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Quote', '_id': 'nyt://article/8cc3c995-37c7-50ee-93bb-d105bfe93f1a', 'word_count': 45, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8cc3c995-37c7-50ee-93bb-d105bfe93f1a'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/09/arts/television/whats-on-tv-tuesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '1 P.M. (SYFY) THE PRESTIGE (2006) Robert Angier (Hugh Jackman) and Alfred Borden (Christian Bale) work together as assistants in magic performances before a tragedy turns them into lifelong enemies. As performing magicians, they seek vengeance on each other through obsessive one-upmanship. Rebecca Hall and Michael Caine play supporting roles as Angier’s wife and his manager. “\xa0‘The Prestige,’ directed by Christopher Nolan from a script he wrote with his brother Jonathan, is an intricate and elaborate machine designed for the simple purpose of diversion,” A. O. Scott wrote in The New York Times. “Set in a stylized late-Victorian world of dueling music-hall magicians and diabolically clever inventors, it has a satisfyingly puzzlelike structure, zipping around in time and scattering clues throughout its busy scenes and frames. Like ‘Memento,’ also directed by Christopher Nolan and based on a story by his brother, ‘The Prestige’ is a t