me': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Koblin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Koblin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa85cc42-fb18-537d-8a3b-5f4e7f43755d', 'word_count': 1718, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa85cc42-fb18-537d-8a3b-5f4e7f43755d'}, {'abstract': 'Daniel Rossen of Grizzly Bear created the sparkling soundtrack for “Valedictorian,” directed by his old NYU friend Matthew Yeager.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/09/daniel-rossen-valedictorian-film-score/', 'snippet': 'Daniel Rossen of Grizzly Bear created the sparkling soundtrack for “Valedictorian,” directed by his old NYU friend Matthew Yeager.', 'lead_paragraph': '“It was very low-conflict,” says Daniel Rossen, the musician responsible for the band Grizzly Bear’s lush guitar arrangements and occasional vocals, who also records as Department of Eagles and under his own name. He’s talking about his first time scoring a film, “Valedictorian,” which was directed by his old NYU friend Matthew Yeager. Back in the day, the pair worked together at a store in the West Village that specialized in cinema classics and foreign film, in “the city that’s long gone,” as Rossen puts it. Rossen and Yeager bonded over understated films and music, an aesthetic that would later inform their own art. “In film and music, I think we prefer a much subtler approach,” Rossen explains. That’s evident in both “Valedictorian” itself, a poetic film about a young man detaching from his social and work landscape in New York City, and its sparkling, guitar-driven soundtrack, which streams exclusively here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/09/t-magazine/09symonds-rossen/09symonds-rossen-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600,