, 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/41a5d56e-6b83-5c15-957f-552873da013e', 'word_count': 624, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41a5d56e-6b83-5c15-957f-552873da013e'}, {'abstract': 'The mysteries of three-cup chicken explained.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/14/magazine/a-taste-of-taiwan.html', 'snippet': 'The mysteries of three-cup chicken explained.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The chicken is tender and velvety, a little crisp at its edges, surrounded by coins of ginger and cloves of garlic. The sauce is dark and pungent — soy sauce cut through with aged rice wine and studded with bits of scallion, perfect for ladling onto rice. There is a sweetness in there as well, sugar and sesame against the salt of the soy, with a faint hit of peppery fire. A handful or two of Thai basil added at the end imparts a floral punch.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/magazine/14eat/14mag-14eat-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/14/magazine/14eat/14mag-14eat-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/magazine/14eat/14mag-14eat-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 767, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/magazine/14eat/14mag-14eat-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1535, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail'