Financing Unit for $12 Billion', 'web_url': 'https://news.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/10/morning-agenda-china-moves-closer-to-inclusion-in-msci-index/', 'snippet': 'China Moves Closer to Inclusion in MSCI Index | Wall Street Embraces Financial Tech Start-Ups | Tokio Marine to Buy HCC for $7.5 Billion | G.E. Sells Buyout Financing Unit for $12 Billion', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHINA MOVES CLOSER TO INCLUSION IN MSCI INDEX | Though MSCI decided on Tuesday that it would hold off on adding Chinese domestic shares to its emerging market index, China is moving closer to joining the global benchmark,  Neil Gough writes in DealBook. In its decision, the index company cited concerns over China’s cumbersome investment quotas, restrictions on money flows, and questions over the legal status of foreign shareholders. But MSCI said that in the coming months it would work with China’s main securities regulator to address the company’s concerns and that it may make a special decision to include Chinese stocks, also known as A-shares, in its benchmarks before the next scheduled annual review, which takes place a year from now. “It is clear from MSCI’s announcement today that it is only a matter of time before A-shares are added to global indexes,” Louis Lu, a portfolio manager at CSOP Asset Management, which invests in mainland shares, said in an email. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Agenda: China Moves Closer to Inclusion in MSCI Index', 'kicker': 'News', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Investments', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hedge Funds', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Indexers and Indexing', 'rank':