Aurora (Colo)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-11T22:49:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed0eb71c-455f-5a15-b5d9-ab9925d74722', 'word_count': 974, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed0eb71c-455f-5a15-b5d9-ab9925d74722'}, {'abstract': 'The director discusses a scene from “Me and Earl and the Dying Girl.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/12/movies/me-and-earl-and-the-dying-girl-with-movie-trailer-alfonso-gomez-rejon-narrates-a-scene.html', 'snippet': 'The director discusses a scene from “Me and Earl and the Dying Girl.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the comedic drama “Me and Earl and the Dying Girl,” a movie-obsessed teenager named Greg (Thomas Mann) befriends his classmate Rachel (Olivia Cooke), who has been diagnosed with leukemia. The friendship begins reluctantly when Greg’s mother (Connie Britton) forces him to call Rachel in this early scene from the film. The sequence plays out in one continuous take as the camera roams through Greg’s home and we listen in on his conversation with Rachel.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Me and Earl and the Dying Girl’ (With Movie Trailer): Alfonso Gomez-Rejon Narrates a Scene', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gomez-Rejon, Alfonso (1972- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Britton, Connie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Offerman, Nick', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Me and Earl and the Dying Girl