'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-12T01:18:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Juliet Macur', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Juliet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macur', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/86c30f4d-78a4-587a-9a69-c240bd9ff0d4', 'word_count': 1001, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86c30f4d-78a4-587a-9a69-c240bd9ff0d4'}, {'abstract': 'Saudi Arabia rejected foreign criticism of the flogging punishment of Raif Badawi, a blogger who has come to symbolize the kingdom’s oppressive restraints on free speech.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/12/world/middleeast/saudi-arabia-bloggers-allies-rebuffed.html', 'snippet': 'Saudi Arabia rejected foreign criticism of the flogging punishment of Raif Badawi, a blogger who has come to symbolize the kingdom’s oppressive restraints on free speech.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Saudi Arabia on Thursday rejected foreign criticism of the conviction and flogging punishment of Raif Badawi, a blogger who has come to symbolize what human rights advocates call the kingdom’s oppressive restraints on free speech and its cruel justice system. The Foreign Ministry, in a brief statement carried by the official Saudi Press Agency, described the criticism as interference in the kingdom’s internal affairs. The United States government commission on religious freedom, the European Union and human rights groups have asked King Salman to pardon Mr. Badawi, 31, whose sentence of 1,000 lashes, to be administered in weekly rounds of 50 each, was suspended in January after the first round nearly killed him. But this month the kingdom’s highest court affirmed the validity of the conviction and the punishment, which also includes a 10-year prison term and a $267,000 fine. The court’s decision appeared to allow for the resumption of the floggings, which could happen as early as Friday. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Saudi Arabia: Blogger’s Allies Rebuffed', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Middle East; Saudi Arabia: Blogger’s Allies Rebuffed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Saudi Arabia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Badawi, Raif', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blogs and Blogging (Internet)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-12T01:26:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/59c027ec-ffe5-5e49-a898-d54dae4594e1', 'word_count': 159, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59c027ec-ffe5-5e49-a898-d54dae4594e1'}, {'abstract': 'When he left Britain in the wake of a phone-hacking scandal, the younger Murdoch seemed too tarnished to inherit his father’s mantle.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/12/business/media/once-down-james-murdoch-is-guided-back-to-the-top.html', 'snippet': 'When he left Britain in the wake of a phone-hacking scandal, the younger Murdoch seemed too tarnished to inherit his father’s mantle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It would be hard to oversta