9a3884cfa'}, {'abstract': 'The Department of Labor is investigating two India-based firms for possible violations of rules for visas for foreign technology workers under contracts they held with Southern California Edison.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/12/us/politics/outsourcing-companies-under-scrutiny-over-visas-for-technology-workers.html', 'snippet': 'The Department of Labor is investigating two India-based firms for possible violations of rules for visas for foreign technology workers under contracts they held with Southern California Edison.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Department of Labor has opened an investigation into two India-based outsourcing companies for possible violations of rules for visas for foreign technology workers under contracts they held with an electric utility, Southern California Edison. The power company recently laid off more than 500 technology workers. Many said they were made to train replacements who were immigrants on temporary visas, known as H-1B, brought in by the Indian firms, Tata Consultancy Services and Infosys. Senators Richard Durbin of Illinois, a Democrat, and Jeff Sessions of Alabama, a Republican, announced the investigation after they were notified by the department.\xa0 ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Outsourcing Companies Under Scrutiny Over Visas for Technology Workers', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Washington', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Outsourcing Companies Under Scrutiny Over Visas for Technology Workers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Workers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Preston, Julia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Outsourcing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Southern California Edison (SCE)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Infosys Technologies Ltd', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tata Consultancy Services', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Labor Department (US)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Visas', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sessions, Jefferson B III', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Durbin, Richard J', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-12T01:56:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Preston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Preston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ebbc96d-1a5f-55e7-87f2-c1015659954f', 'word_count': 98, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ebbc96d-1a5f-55e7-87f2-c1015659954f'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Guttenfelder has been to North Korea about 40 times. He answers questions on working conditions, local cuisine and American TV.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/12/world/asia/david-guttenfelder-a-photojournalist-answers-readers-questions-on-north-korea.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Guttenfelder has been to North Korea about 40 times. He answers questions on working conditions, local cuisine and American TV.', 'lead_paragraph': 'David Guttenfelder, a photojournalist and National Geographic Fellow, recently spent six days in North Korea on assignment for The New York Times. He answered readers’ questions during a Q. and A. on Facebook on Thursday on everyday life in North Korea and his working conditions there. Mr. Guttenfelder, who has been to North Korea about 40 times, received questions on a wide range of topics, including malnutrition, censorship and the availability of American TV shows like “Keeping Up With the Kardashians.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/12/world/NORTHKOREAQA/NORTHKOREAQA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/12/world/NORTHKOREAQA/NORTHKOREAQA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/12/world/NORTHKOREAQA/NORTHKOREAQA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/12/world/NORTHKOREAQA/NORTHKOREAQA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/12/world/NORTHKOREAQA/NORTHKOREAQA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/12/world/NORTHKOREAQA/NORTHKOREAQA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/12/world/NORTHKOREAQA/NORTHKOREAQA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'David Guttenfelder, a Photojournalist, Answers Readers’ Questions on North Korea', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Associated Press', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Guttenfelder, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-12T01:57:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/091397e8-0dd6-59da-8324-10953567a6aa', 'word_count': 1512, 'uri': 'nyt://article/091397e8-0dd6-59da-8324-10953567a6aa'}, {'abstract': 'City investigators said that Denzel Thompson, 24, had falsified logbooks and stopped monitoring the home’s residents, three of whom are accused of raping and robbing a woman in Manhattan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/12/nyregion/former-worker-arrested-in-brooklyn-group-home-investigation.html', 'snippet': 'City investigators said that Denzel Thompson, 24, had falsified logbooks and stopped monitoring the home’s residents, three of whom are accused of raping and robbing a woman in Manhattan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A worker at a group home for boys in Brooklyn has been arrested after three boys he claimed he was monitoring slipped out of the home, city investigators said on Thursday. The boys are accused of raping, robbing and beating a woman while they were out.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Former Worker Arrested in Brooklyn Group Home Investigation', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Arrest in Inquiry of Group Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thompson, Denzel (1990- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boys Town', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Department of Investigation (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-12T01:59:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Southall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Southall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a038b9b-b9cd-53ae-89ba-48c58e0a33d4', 'word_count': 247, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a038b9b-b9cd-53ae-89ba-48c58e0a33d4'}, {'abstract': 'This two-hander by Rajiv Joseph dramatizes a dark myth about the construction of the Taj Mahal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/12/theater/review-guards-at-the-taj-two-ordinary-guys-ordered-to-do-the-unthinkable.html', 'snippet': 'This two-hander by Rajiv Joseph dramatizes a dark myth about the construction of the Taj Mahal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Taj Mahal is among the world’s most ravishingly beautiful creations, but should you have a chance to see it in person after watching “Guards at the Taj,” a new play by Rajiv Joseph, you may find yourself viewing it through a bloodstained haze. In this mostly absorbing two-hander, which opened on Thursday in an Atlantic Theater Company production directed by the noted actor Amy Morton, Mr. Joseph dramatizes a dark myth about its building that stands as a grim allegory of the supreme divide between the powerful and the powerless in 17th-century India and, perhaps by extension, many places today. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/12/arts/12GUARDS/12GUARDS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/0