+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carlene Bauer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carlene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bauer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7733bbd8-2ec5-549d-85f1-6be3f9a1793b', 'word_count': 1058, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7733bbd8-2ec5-549d-85f1-6be3f9a1793b'}, {'abstract': 'Food producers focus on factors such as yield, at the expense of flavor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/14/books/review/the-dorito-effect-by-mark-schatzker.html', 'snippet': 'Food producers focus on factors such as yield, at the expense of flavor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few years ago I sat with a friend and looked at a menu, and we wondered why everything seemed to be flavored like a taco. Or Caesar salad or maple syrup. I couldn’t quite say what about it bothered me, but I held a conviction that something was wrong in a thing — a Mexican snack, salad dressing, tree sap — being turned into a flavor.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14ADLER/14-Adler-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 165, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14ADLER/14-Adler-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 165}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14ADLER/14-Adler-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 281, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/books/review/14ADLER/14-Adler-