ganization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/eb518448-ccc1-5fc3-a14f-946e2e9be02e', 'word_count': 663, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eb518448-ccc1-5fc3-a14f-946e2e9be02e'}, {'abstract': 'Taryn Simon’s “Black Square XVII” is made from nuclear waste and will go on view in 3015, once it has been sapped of radioactive material.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/12/moscow-garage-museum-dasha-zhukova/', 'snippet': 'Taryn Simon’s “Black Square XVII” is made from nuclear waste and will go on view in 3015, once it has been sapped of radioactive material.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week in Moscow, all of the exhibitions and works have been installed for the globally buzzy opening of the Garage Museum of Contemporary Art’s new Rem Koolhaas-designed building — including a Ping-Pong-based performance by Rirkrit Tiravanija called “Tomorrow is the Question?” and the selfie heaven of Yayoi Kusama’s “Infinity Theory,” the artist’s first-ever show in Russia — except for one, leaving a blank cube in the wall where it will eventually be placed. But don’t hold your breath for that: Taryn Simon’s “Black Square XVII” is made from nuclear waste, and it will take 1,000 years for the vitrification process to rid it of radioactive material. It’ll be on view in 3015.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/12/t-magazine/12simon-freeman/12simon-freeman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/12/t-magazine/12simon-freeman/12simon-freeman-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/12/t-magazine/12simon-freeman/12simon-freeman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_na