ding to a criminal complaint.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DANNEMORA, N.Y. — More than a month before two killers escaped from a maximum-security prison in far northern New York State last weekend, a worker there who befriended them provided them with some of the tools to do the deed: hacksaw blades, chisels, a punch and a screwdriver bit, according to criminal complaints.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/13/nyregion/13MANHUNT2sub/13MANHUNT2sub-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/13/nyregion/13MANHUNT2sub/13MANHUNT2sub-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/13/nyregion/13MANHUNT2sub/13MANHUNT2sub-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/13/nyregion/13MANHUNT2sub/13MANHUNT2sub-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/13/nyregion/13MANHUNT2sub/13MANHUNT2sub-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/13/nyregion/13MANHUNT2sub/13MANHUNT2sub-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/13/nyregion/13MANHUNT2sub/13MANHUNT2sub-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Prison Worker Who Befriended Killers Is Accused of Providing Tools for Escape', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Prison Worker Who Befriended Killers Is Accused of Providing Tools for Escape', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McKinley, Jesse', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mitchell, Joyce E (1964- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Matt, Richard W (1967- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nir, Sarah Maslin', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sweat, David P (1980- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prison Escapes', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Clinton Correctional Facility', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Dannemora (NY)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-12T17:07:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jesse McKinley, Sarah Maslin Nir and William K. Rashbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jesse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': 'Maslin', 'lastname': 'Nir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': 'K.', 'lastname': 'Rashbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5402ad88-4c15-5830-9a7d-a94d16bf40dc', 'word_count': 1170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5402ad88-4c15-5830-9a7d-a94d16bf40dc'}, {'abstract': 'Judy Blume, whose adult novel “In the Unlikely Event” is No. 4 on the hardcover fiction list, had two million books in print in 1976, well before The Times had a children’s best-seller list.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Judy Blume, whose adult novel “In the Unlikely Event” is No. 4 on the hardcover fiction list, had two million books in print in 1976, well before The Times had a children’s best-seller list.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Blume’s Day: In the summer of 1976, the Book Review took note of a “new frankness in children’s literature,” and cited as \xadExhibit A the novels of Judy Blume: “She is a kind of heroine to the kids who read and reread her books, and a kind of Little League Grace Metalious to the mothers.” Metalious, you may remember, was the author of “Peyton Place” — that decidedly Major League novel of suburban smut — and being seen as a junior version didn’t hurt Blume’s popularity any. Starting with “Wifey” in 1978 and continuing right up to “In the Unlikely Event” (new at No. 4 on the hardcover fiction list), all four of her adult novels have been best sellers. That’s more than you can say for her most celebrated children’s books, including “Blubber” and “Are You There God? It’s Me, Margaret,” although it’s not their fault: The Times didn’t introduce a children’s best-seller list until 2000. Had one existed in 1976, Blume would surely have been on it. She had some two million books in print that year, according to the Times article, which further observed that “Blume’s appeal lies in her ability to speak directly to her readers’ experience. Her characters are usually suburban kids, and not always nice ones.” Blume recently told NPR’s “Weekend Edition” that when she was an adolescent herself, she preferred grown-up literature. “Starting at 12, really, and onward, I was reading adult books,” she said. “Everything that I was curious about, about the adult world, I learned from reading those books. Or I got from them, you know, some idea of what it was like to be grown-up. Which is what I was very, very curious about.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21insidelist-blume/21insidelist-blume-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 451, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21insidelist-blume/21insidelist-blume-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 451, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21insidelist-blume/21insidelist-blume-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21insidelist-blume/21insidelist-blume-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21insidelist-blume/21insidelist-blume-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blume, Judy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-12T17:08:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e766bb7-9a2d-5733-a5c7-d3a69897f73f', 'word_count': 498, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e766bb7-9a2d-5733-a5c7-d3a69897f73f'}, {'abstract': 'Ben Ratliff, Maria Sherman and Liz Pelly discuss the band’s new album.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/12/popcast-tenement-punk-and-the-diy-scene/', 'snippet': 'Ben Ratliff, Maria Sherman and Liz Pelly discuss the band’s new album.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Predatory Headlights” is the new album by Tenement, the punk trio from Appleton, Wis. It’s an accomplishment. It’s a double album, with 25 songs, and it took more than three years to make. It gives you an enlarged sense of what the primary songwriter, Amos Pitsch, is most trained at: tightly written riff songs with an almost classical pop sense, descended from pop-punk while transcending its clichés. But it also includes experimental compositions and arrangements involving strings and field recordings and an out-of-tune piano. “Predatory Headlights” is strong and strange and detailed and cohesive; if people are still listening to records from 2015 in 20 years, this might be one of them. Tenement started in 2006 and has spent a lot of its life on the DIY circuit, gigging in living rooms and basements. For quite a while, if you wanted a Tenement record, you had to buy one at a gig. It appears on bills and has recorded split EPs with far more noisy, simple straight-ahead hardcore bands, but it plays music that sounds as if it could have been almost popular at some point in the recent past. Where does Tenement fit into the current punk-hardcore-DIY scene? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/09/arts/09NEWMUSIC-TENEMENT/09NEWMUSIC-TENEMENT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/09/arts/09NEWMUSIC-TENEMENT/09NEWMUSIC-TENEMENT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/09/arts/09NEWMUSIC-TENEMENT/09NEWMUSIC-TENEMENT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/09/arts/09NEWMUSIC-TENEMENT/09NEWMUSIC-TENEMENT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1398, 'width': 1398, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/09/arts/09NEWMUSIC-TENEMENT/09NEWMUSIC-TENEMENT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/09/arts/09NEWMUSIC-TENEMENT/09NEWMUSIC-TENEMENT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/09/arts/09NEWMUSIC-TENEMENT/09NEWMUSIC-TENEMENT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Popcast: Tenement, Punk and the DIY Scene', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-12T17:08:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b883e583-29f3-5234-921a-28c6f0dbadac', 'word_count': 412, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b883e583-29f3-5234-921a-28c6f0dbadac'}, {'abstract': 'The actor and literary agent gained space and a tree-filled view when he moved to his current apartment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/14/realestate/edward-hibbert-on-his-greenwich-village-studio.html', 'snippet': 'The actor and literary agent gained space and a tree-filled view when he moved to his current apartment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For years, the actor Edward Hibbert lived contentedly in a studio apartment in Hell’s Kitchen, an area that “has become so chic we now call it Chez Hades,” said Mr. Hibbert, 59, who is playing an uncannily prescient wedding planner in the Broadway musical “It Shoulda Been You.” ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Edward Hibbert on His West Village Studio', 'kicker': 'What I Love', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Actor and Literary Agent as Editor of His Space', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'West Village (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hibbert, Edward', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-12T17:13:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanne Kaufman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaufman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f14f799-409e-5c06-ad2c-f96369307024', 'word_count': 1186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f14f799-409e-5c06-ad2c-f96369307024'}, {'abstract': 'Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo’s recent positions on housing policy have seemed ironic and confounding and largely informed by his adversarial relationship with Mayor Bill de Blasio.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/14/nyregion/cuomos-housing-game.html', 'snippet': 'Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo’s recent positions on housing policy have seemed ironic and confounding and largely informed by his adversarial relationship with Mayor Bill de Blasio.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If your Internet wanderings this week happened to land you on the web page for the governor of New York, you would have immediately found images of Andrew M. Cuomo scouring the bowels of the Clinton Correctional Facility, immersing his head in the pipes and looking like Tommy Lee Jones in “The Fugitive.”', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/nyregion/14BIGCITYSUB/14BIGCITYSUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 384, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/14/nyregion/14BIGCITYSUB/14BIGCITYSUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 384}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/nyregion/14BIGCITYSUB/14BIGCITYSUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 656, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/nyregion/14BIGCITYSUB/14BIGCITYSUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1312, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/14/nyregion/14BIGCITYSUB/14BIGCITYSUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/14/nyregion/14BIGC