tion': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/13/arts/13PARRENO/13PARRENO-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/13/arts/13PARRENO/13PARRENO-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/13/arts/13PARRENO/13PARRENO-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘H {N)Y P N(Y} OSIS,’ Out of This World, at Park Avenue Armory', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Boisterous Love Letter to New York ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Park Avenue Armory (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Parreno, Philippe', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-12T23:42:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ebff548-1f78-5adb-93fa-e649b392934e', 'word_count': 1025, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ebff548-1f78-5adb-93fa-e649b392934e'}, {'abstract': 'New York City’s effort to promote diversity among the staffs, boards and audiences of its cultural organizations has been buttressed by $150,000 from foundations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/13/arts/citys-diversity-effort-gets-financial-backers.html', 'snippet': 'New York City’s effort to promote diversity among the staffs, boards and audiences of its cultural organizations has been buttressed by $150,000 from foundations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York City’s effort to promote diversity among the staffs, boards and audiences of its cultural organizations — announced earlier this year — has been buttressed by $150,000 from foundations. The most recent award, of $100,000, comes from the Ford Foundation to support the roll out of the plan after the city’s Department of Cultural Affairs has completed a survey of arts groups later this year. The Mertz-Gilmore Foundation and the Rockefeller Brothers Fund each previously provided $25,000 to design the survey. Tom Finkelpearl, the department’s commissioner, was to announce the new funding on Saturday during a presentation on diversity with Jane Chu, chairwoman of the National Endowment for the Arts, at the annual Americans for the Arts convention in Chicago. “Getting a frank discussion started about the issue of diversity at cultural organizations is absolutely critical to the future of the sector and of the City,” Darren Walker, the president of the Ford Foundation, said in a statement. The survey, designed by the firm Ithaka S+R, will collect data on the diversity at cultural organizations, taking into account factors like age, race, ethnicity, disability and gender.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'City’s Diversity Effort Gets Financial Backers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Diversity Effort Gets Donation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Culture (Arts)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Foundation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Finkelpearl, Tom', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-12T23:46:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/c6a6a7af-e9b0-5977-a065-24e844e85393', 'word_count': 187, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c6a6a7af-e9b0-5977-a065-24e844e85393'}, {'abstract': 'Foreign organizations operating in Pakistan were told they faced greater scrutiny and the possibility of expulsion if they failed to adhere to unspecified rules and laws.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/13/world/asia/pakistan-warns-aid-groups-to-follow-unspecified-rules.html', 'snippet': 'Foreign organizations operating in Pakistan were told they faced greater scrutiny and the possibility of expulsion if they failed to adhere to unspecified rules and laws.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ISLAMABAD, Pakistan — After the police shut down the offices of a major Western aid group, Pakistan’s interior minister warned Friday that other foreign organizations operating in Pakistan faced greater scrutiny and the possibility of expulsion if they failed to adhere to unspecified rules and laws.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pakistan Warns Aid Groups to Follow Unspecified Rules', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pakistan Warns Aid Groups to Follow Unspecified Rules', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Save the Children', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nonprofit Organizations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name'