ue': 'Minnesota', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Priests', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-15T10:52:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mitch Smith and Laurie Goodstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mitch', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Laurie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goodstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/76c20160-0236-5a42-8183-65f58f60bbb0', 'word_count': 1142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76c20160-0236-5a42-8183-65f58f60bbb0'}, {'abstract': 'The West sees President Sisi as a man it can do business with, never mind the repressions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/16/opinion/egypts-vanishing-youth.html', 'snippet': 'The West sees President Sisi as a man it can do business with, never mind the repressions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO — Esraa el-Taweel, 23, went out to dinner with friends on June 1 and has not been seen since. Ms. Taweel, a student and photojournalist who was shot in the spine last year while covering protests on the anniversary of Egypt’s 2011 revolution, is said to have difficulty walking. Her family believes she was forcibly “disappeared” along with two young friends, Sohaib Mohamed and Amr Mohamed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Egypt’s Vanishing Youth', 'kicker': 'Contributing Op-Ed Writer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Egypt’s Vanishing Youth', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sisi, Abdel Fattah el-', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Youth', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-15T10:54:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mona Eltahawy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mona', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eltahawy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b4ad724-8ed4-5389-8aea-cd6ae8024fca', 'word_count': 952, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b4ad724-8ed4-5389-8aea-cd6ae8024fca'}, {'abstract': 'Not really. But we often think and talk about them as though they do, and that is cause for concern.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/15/do-corporations-have-minds/', 'snippet': 'Not really. But we often think and talk about them as though they do, and that is cause for concern.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Back in 2007, Viacom filed a copyright infringement lawsuit. It alleged that the defendants had created a website that was hosting copyrighted Viacom videos, including everything from the “The Colbert Report” to “SpongeBob SquarePants.” But that was not all. Viacom made a series of allegations about the defendants’ mental states. It alleged that they specifically “intended” to host these illegal videos, that they were doing so “knowingly” and with “brazen disregard” for the law.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-thestone/opinionator-pog-thestone-articleLarge.png', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-thestone/opinio