z’s new book gives a full historical timeline of the defining features of adulthood.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/books/review/the-prime-of-life-by-steven-mintz.html', 'snippet': 'Steven Mintz’s new book gives a full historical timeline of the defining features of adulthood.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The notion that adulthood, despite its perks, is little more than a booby prize for surviving the scrapes and trials of youth has more or less become a cultural given in the modern age. From the 1950s narrative of soul-crushed men in gray flannel suits to the existential torment of hippies-turned-yuppies in the 1980s to the \xadtyrannical scheduling demands of today’s Lean In movement, grown-up life in America is generally framed as a sort of bland entree following the assortment of colorful appetizers that make up childhood and young adulthood. Moreover, as any armchair social anthropologist will tell you, maturity seems to kick in later and later with each passing generation.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Daum/21-Daum-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 552, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Daum/21-Daum-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 552}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Daum/21-Daum-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 942, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Daum/21-Daum-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1464, 'width': 1591, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Daum/21-Daum-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Daum/21-Daum-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Daum/21-Daum-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Prime of Life,’ by Steven Mintz', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Prime of Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daum, Meghan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mintz, Steven (1953- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-15T20:28:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Meghan Daum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Meghan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Daum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/52ef2793-c494-5da2-8758-1274e2f9f51a', 'word_count': 1270, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52ef2793-c494-5da2-8758-1274e2f9f51a'}, {'abstract': 'Julie Lythcott-Haims cautions against helicopter parenting that keeps kids from growing up.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/books/review/how-to-raise-an-adult-by-julie-lythcott-haims.html', 'snippet': 'Julie Lythcott-Haims cautions against helicopter parenting that keeps kids from growing up.', 'lead_paragraph': ' When did the central aim of parenting become preparing children for success? This reigning paradigm, which dictates that every act of nurturing be judged on the basis of whether it will usher a child toward a life of accomplishment or failure, embodies the fundamental insecurity of global capitalist culture, with its unbending fixation on prosperity and the future. It’s no surprise that parenting incites such heated debates, considering how paradoxical these principles can be when they’re applied to children. When each nurturing act is administered with the distant future in mind, what becomes of the present? A child who soaks in the ambient anxiety that surrounds each trivial choice or activity is an anxious child, formed in the hand-wringing, future-focused image of her anxious parents.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Havrilesky/21-Havrilesky-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 678, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Havrilesky/21-Havrilesky-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 678}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Havrilesky/21-Havrilesky-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 907, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Havrilesky/21-Havrilesky-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1813, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Havrilesky/21-Havrilesky-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Havrilesky/21-Havrilesky-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Havrilesky/21-Havrilesky-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘How to Raise an Adult,’ by Julie Lythcott-Haims', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When I Grow Up', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Havrilesky, Heather', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lythcott-Haims, Julie (1967- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-15T20:29:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Heather Havrilesky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Heather', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Havrilesky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/795f4c71-a4a9-526e-8891-c23cac71d092', 'word_count': 1379, 'uri': 'nyt://article/795f4c71-a4a9-526e-8891-c23cac71d092'}, {'abstract': 'The photographer and artist’s book “Babe” is out this month; she discusses the artists, movies and more that inspire her.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/15/petra-collins-favorite-things/', 'snippet': 'The photographer and artist’s book “Babe” is out this month; she discusses the artists, movies and more that inspire her.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Describing Petra Collins as a photographer is reductive, though it’s her photographs for which she’s best known. That said, she’s also well recognized from the many photographs that have been taken of her, notably by her mentors Richard Kern and Ryan McGinley. And she is probably most infamous for her drawings, particularly her “Period Power” illustration for an American Apparel T-shirt that generated significant media notoriety. (To further complicate things, that illustration was originally based on a neon light sculpture that Collins had previously created.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/10/t-magazine/10collins-miller-slide-29Z0/10collins-miller-slide-29Z0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/10/t-magazine/10collins-miller-slide-29Z0/10collins-miller-slide-29Z0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 800}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/10/t-magazine/10collins-miller-slide-29Z0/10collins-miller-slide-29Z0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 769, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/10/t-magazine/10collins-miller-slide-29Z0/10collins-miller-slide-29Z0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1537, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/10/t-magazine/10collins-miller-slide-29Z0/10collins-miller-slide-29Z0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/10/t-magazine/10collins-miller-slide-29Z0/10collins-miller-slide-29Z0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/10/t-magazine/10collins-miller-slide-29Z0/10collins-miller-slide-29Z0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Petra Collins Shares Her Favorite Things', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Collins, Petra', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gevinson, Tavi', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kern, Richard', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McGinley, Ryan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-15T20:30:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Miller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c2ba457-c64f-5f58-86d8-aa7593788d0c', 'word_count': 176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c2ba457-c64f-5f58-86d8-aa7593788d0c'}, {'abstract': 'This Baroque violinist played Bach’s Partita in A minor for solo flute, along with Tartini and Biber works, at the Baryshnikov Arts Center.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/16/arts/music/review-rachel-podger-plays-bach-for-flute-with-her-violin.html', 'snippet': 'This Baroque violinist played Bach’s Partita in A minor for solo flute, along with Tartini and Biber works, at the Baryshnikov Arts Center.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a rule, violinists rarely pilfer foreign repertory. They don’t need to. With so much first-rate music written for the violin, the motivation to adapt and transcribe works written for other instruments is low. So why did Rachel Podger, one of the leading performers on the Baroque violin, open her masterly recital at the Baryshnikov Arts Center on Sunday with Bach’s Partita in A minor for solo flute, transposed into the key of G minor? Because when it comes to Bach, Ms. Podger told the audience, “You can’t get enough.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/16/arts/16PODGER/16PODGER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 462, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/16/arts/16PODGER/16PODGER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 462}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/16/arts/16PODGER/16PODGER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 788, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/16/arts/16PODGER/16PODGER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1080, 'width': 1403, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/16/arts/16PODGER/16PODGER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/16/arts/16PODGER/16PODGER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/16/arts/16PODGER/16PODGER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Rachel Podger Plays Bach for Flute With Her Violin', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bach for Violin, Transposed and Composed ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Baryshnikov Arts Center', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Podger, Rachel (1968- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-15T20:33:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corinna da Fonseca-Wollheim', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/861241ea-69fc-594e-86ad-7a153602671e', 'word_count': 431, 'uri': 'nyt://article/861241ea-69fc-594e-86ad-7a153602671e'}, {'abstract': 'In her new book, the philosopher Susan Neiman takes on the predicament of maturity in an “infantile age” that dates back to the 18th century.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/books/review/why-grow-up-by-susan-neiman.html', 'snippet': 'In her new book, the philosopher Susan Neiman takes on the predicament of maturity in an “infantile age” that dates back to the 18th century.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A great deal of modern popular culture — including just about everything pertaining to what French savants like to call le nouvel âge d’or de la comédie américaine — runs on the disavowal of maturity. The ideal consumer is a mirror image of a familiar comic archetype: a man-child sitting in his parents’ basement with his video games and his “Star Wars” figurines; a postgraduate girl and her pals treating the world as their playground. Baby boomers pursue perpetual youth into retirement. Gen-Xers hold fast to their skateboards, their Pixies T-shirts and their Beastie Boys CDs. Nobody wants to be an adult anymore, and every so often someone writes an article blaming Hollywood, attachment parenting, global capitalism or the welfare state for this catastrophe. I’ve written one or two of those myself. It’s not a bad racket, and since I’m intimately acquainted, on a professional basis, with the cinematic oeuvre of Adam Sandler, I qualify as something of an expert.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Cover/21-Cover-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 476, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Cover/21-Cover-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 476}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Cover/21-Cover-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 812, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Cover/21-Cover-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1235, 'width': 1558, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Cover/21-Cover-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Cover/21-Cover-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Cover/21-Cover-thumbLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': '