t (and the offshoot Mister Sunday, which finds a spirited summer crowd gathering at Industry City, Brooklyn) knew it was home for their new all-outdoor urban oasis bar, Nowadays.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/16/t-magazine/16nowadays-donnelly-1/16nowadays-donnelly-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/16/t-magazine/16nowadays-donnelly-1/16nowadays-donnelly-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/16/t-magazine/16nowadays-donnelly-1/16nowadays-donnelly-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/16/t-magazine/16nowadays-donnelly-1/16nowadays-donnelly-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/16/t-magazine/16nowadays-donnelly-1/16nowadays-donnelly-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/16/t-magazine/16nowadays-donnelly-1/16nowadays-donnelly-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/16/t-magazine/16nowadays-donnelly-1/16nowadays-donnelly-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Playlist From Nowadays, the New Outdoor Bar From the Guys Behind Mister Saturday Night', 'kicker': 'Food Matters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-06-16T04:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TDesign', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Donnelly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Donnelly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ac5eacd9-a25a-56cf-a500-5a7cdeafb695', 'word_count': 529, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ac5eacd9-a25a-56cf-a500-5a7cdeafb695'}, {'abstract': 'The artist’s seven-month-long project, “Here Hear,” includes an exhibition at his alma mater, Cranbrook.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/16/t-magazine/nick-cave-here-hear-detroit.html', 'snippet': 'The artist’s seven-month-long project, “Here Hear,” includes an exhibition at his alma mater, Cranbrook.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the Cranbrook Art Museum asked to host his inaugural solo outing in Michigan, the artist and dancer — and Cranbrook Academy of Art alum — Nick Cave agreed, on one condition: “I said, I will only do it if I can do work in Detroit.” For the uninitiated, the school’s campus sits about 20 miles away from the downtown area, Cave’s first lesson upon his arrival in the late ’80s. “Girl, thank God for the city,” he says. “I got here and I was the sole black person, so Detroit saved my life. I became connected to this circle of creative people.” He recalls a fearlessness to the way they came together and fed off of one another. “I don’t know if I could have done Cranbrook without Detroit,” he avers. “That’s why this is so important; I’m reconnecting with the city that really allowed me to create a balance in my higher education.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nick Cave Revisits Detroit, Soundsuits in Tow', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-06-16T04:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TDesign', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hilary Moss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hilary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bfc30d0e-6ecd-5cbf-b564-b63c476902d2', 'word_count': 414, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bfc30d0e-6ecd-5cbf-b564-b63c476902d2'}, {'abstract': "At the brand's exclusive new SoHo space, its performance wear proves itself in workout classes for fashion editors, insiders and the like.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/16/t-magazine/inside-nikes-supersecret-showroom-and-fitness-studio.html', 'snippet': "At the brand's exclusive new SoHo space, its performance wear proves itself in workout classes for fashion editors, insiders and the like.", 'lead_paragraph': 'In the fashion world, showrooms are essentially a safe place for editors, buyers and stylists of very important clients to experience a collection — to ask questions, inspect details, feel fabrics and understand a garment’s nuances. At certain showroom appointments, models are even on hand to help visitors visualize pieces on the wearer — how they move with the body or drape across it, which features they accentuate and what proportions they require. And at Nike’s newest showroom, 45 Grand (so named for its address), the goal is, similarly, to educate and provide an experience. But the interiors are nothing like what the industry has come to expect.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/16/t-magazine/16nike-grand-slide-61NL/16nike-grand-slide-61NL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': '