ganization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a998055f-5286-53fa-a854-8b7c1d5b5093', 'word_count': 258, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a998055f-5286-53fa-a854-8b7c1d5b5093'}, {'abstract': 'With its video game metaphor, this exhibition at Postmasters examines the fuzzy border between war and games.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/19/arts/design/review-eddo-sterns-vietnam-romance-is-art-you-can-watch-or-play.html', 'snippet': 'With its video game metaphor, this exhibition at Postmasters examines the fuzzy border between war and games.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Video games surpassed Hollywood movies in popularity over a half-decade ago, but few artists have dealt seriously with the aesthetics or technology of the form. Eddo Stern is an exception, and his current project, “Vietnam Romance” (2015), is a seasoned meditation on the relationship between war and gaming.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/19/arts/19EDDOSTERNGALLERY2/19EDDOSTERNGALLERY2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/19/arts/19EDDOSTERNGALLERY2/19EDDOSTERNGALLERY2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/19/arts/19EDDOSTERNGALLERY2/19EDDOSTERNGALLERY2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 575, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/19/arts/19EDDOSTERNGALLERY2/19EDDOSTERNGALLERY2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1077, 'width': 1919, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', '