re gone.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/17/experts-on-aging-dying-as-they-lived/', 'snippet': 'The way my parents died — in comfort — was the way they cared for me after they were gone.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At 10 years old I knew my parents did not wish to be resuscitated nor plugged into machines in the event of serious illness. They told me they were not afraid of death but rather of being kept alive at any cost. I knew they would refuse medical interventions, if they felt there was no purpose except to separate the dying from their deaths. They were wary of doctors who my parents said were trained by a medical culture that had lost touch with what should be its major focus: ending suffering.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/opinion/opinionator-theend-series-icon/opinionator-theend-series-icon-articleLarge.png', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/30/opinion/opinionator-theend-series-icon/opinionator-theend-series-icon-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/opinion/opinionator-theend-series-icon/opinionator-theend-series-icon-jumbo.png', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/opinion/opinionator-theend-series-icon/opinionator-theend-series-icon-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/opinion/opinionator-theend-series-icon/opinionator-theend-series-icon-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/30/opinion/opinionator-theend-series-icon/opinionator-theend-series-icon-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/opinion/opinionator-theend-series-icon/opinionator-theend-series-icon-thumbLarge-v2.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Experts on Aging, Dying as They Lived', 'kicker': 'The End', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Death and Dying', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Butler, Robert N', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-17T10:45:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexandra Butler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Butler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/06785132-1718-57a0-a369-33d6c0789167', 'word_count': 1186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06785132-1718-57a0-a369-33d6c0789167'}, {'abstract': 'About 600,000 customer transactions were not processed overnight, after an earlier episode left millions unable to access their accounts for several weeks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/18/business/dealbook/rbs-technology-failure.html', 'snippet': 'About 600,000 customer transactions were not processed overnight, after an earlier episode left millions unable to access their accounts for several weeks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — The Royal Bank of Scotland said on Wednesday that about 600,000 customer transactions had not been processed as a result of a technology failure overnight. The issue affected transactions at the company’s R.B.S., National Westminster Bank, Coutts and Ulster Bank brands.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'R.B.S. Is Again Hit by a Technology Failure', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Bank of Scotland PLC', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Coutts & Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-17T10:55:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chad Bray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/83be882c-130d-5c7f-82ac-82549da28c5f', 'word_count': 283, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83be882c-130d-5c7f-82ac-82549da28c5f'}, {'abstract': 'Food is this year’s theme at the expo, and the president of the USA Pavilion will show American cuisine in a cultural context.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/travel/dorothy-cann-hamilton-talks-about-the-world-expo.html', 'snippet': 'Food is this year’s theme at the expo, and the president of the USA Pavilion will show American cuisine in a cultural context.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dorothy Cann Hamilton is no stranger to the world of food. In 1984 she started the French Culinary Institute, now the International Culinary Center, in New York and recruited renowned chefs like José Andrés and Jacques Pépin as teachers. Ms. Hamilton, 65, is the president of the USA Pavilion at the 2015 World Expo, in Milan through Oct. 31. The theme is “Feeding the Planet, Energy for Life,” and she is running a 35,000-square-foot pavilion that puts American food culture in the spotlight. Following are excerpts from a conversation with Ms. Hamilton.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/travel/21QA1/21QA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/21/travel/21QA1/21QA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/travel/21QA1/21QA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/travel/21QA1/21QA1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1248, 'width': 832, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/travel/21QA1/21QA1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/21/travel/21QA1/21QA1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/travel/21QA1/21QA1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Dorothy Cann Hamilton Talks About the World Expo', 'kicker': 'Q&A', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dorothy Cann Hamilton on Feeding the Planet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hamilton, Dorothy Cann', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "World's Fairs", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-17T11:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shivani Vora', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shivani', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vora', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a03c2d89-1f29-5851-8363-f01aa642ec27', 'word_count': 560, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a03c2d89-1f29-5851-8363-f01aa642ec27'}, {'abstract': 'What to do with a wedding ring from a brawl?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/magazine/taxicab-confessions.html', 'snippet': 'What to do with a wedding ring from a brawl?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last night I witnessed an assault on a taxi driver. After the passenger jumped out of the cab and kicked it repeatedly, the driver got out and confronted the man. The man suddenly began throwing punches, and a legitimate brawl ensued during which the man yelled things like “go back where you came from” and “I was born in this country, you weren’t.” A small crowd gathered to break up the fight. When the police arrived, I stuck around to defend the cabdriver. The police listened but told me they did not need me as an official witness. I wished the cabdriver luck and went across the street to watch the aftermath play out. The passenger, a black man, was claiming that the cabdriver would not take him to Harlem but that he probably would have if he were white. The police did not arrest the man, nor did they appear to do much of anything in the way of even taking information. During the brawl I heard a ring bounce toward me across the street. I picked it up, and after the cabdriver called the police, I asked him if the ring was his. He said it belonged to the other man. I asked somewhat conspiratorially if he wanted the ring anyway, but he said no. When I got home, I found it was a Cartier wedding band that sold new from $1,100 to $1,600. Today I found a posting on Craigslist, seemingly from the passenger, seeking his ring, lost during an assault, in exchange for a reward. So what do I do? J.P., NEW YORK', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21ethicists/21mag-21ethicists-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 603, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21ethicists/21mag-21ethicists-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 603}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21ethicists/21mag-21ethicists-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1019, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21ethicists/21mag-21ethicists-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1360, 'width': 1353, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21ethicists/21mag-21ethicists-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21ethicists/21mag-21ethicists-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21ethicists/21mag-21ethicists-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Taxicab Confessions', 'kicker': 'The Ethicists', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What to Do With a Wedding Ring From a Brawl?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicists, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yoshino, Kenji', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assaults', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloom, Amy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lost and Found Property', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Appiah, Kwame Anthony', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Taxicabs and Taxicab Drivers', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-17T11:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kwame Anthony Appiah, Amy Bloom and Kenji Yoshino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kwame', 'middlename': 'Anthony', 'lastname': 'Appiah', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bloom', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Kenji', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yoshino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/023c1101-31b8-5b52-9383-a66ca2d26ce1', 'word_count': 882, 'uri': 'nyt://article/023c1101-31b8-5b52-9383-a66ca2d26ce1'}, {'abstract': 'What should it take for judges to change their minds?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/magazine/better-judgment.html', 'snippet': 'What should it take for judges to change their minds?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nearly 100 years ago, Oliver Wendell Holmes Jr. stood at an upright desk in his study, dipped a pen into an inkwell inherited from his father and wrote a Supreme Court opinion allowing the government to imprison people for speaking out against World War I. Holmes was 77 on that day in February 1919. He came to Washington 16 years earlier from Massachusetts after stints as a law professor at Harvard and a judge on the state Supreme Court, and he looked the part of a patrician New Englander, with his handlebar mustache and a bristling shock of white hair.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '46', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21mag-judges1/21mag-judges1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 730, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21mag-judges1/21mag-judges1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 730}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21mag-judges1/21mag-judges1-jumbo-v2.gif', 'height': 775, 'width': 945, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21mag-judges1/21mag-judges1-superJumbo-v2.gif', 'height': 775, 'width': 945, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21mag-judges1/21mag-judges1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21mag-judges1/21mag-judges1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21mag-judges1/21mag-judges1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Better Judgment', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Better Judgment', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bazelon, Emily', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Courts and the Judiciary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holmes, Oliver Wendell Jr', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scalia, Antonin', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Posner, Richard A', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voter Registration and Requirements', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-17T11:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily Bazelon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bazelon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d345450-02fa-5313-ab2b-67f0eda85e62', 'word_count': 4315, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d345450-02fa-5313-ab2b-67f0eda85e62'}, {'abstract': 'Two social-media campaigns take on how women’s bodies are (and are not) represented online.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/magazine/our-bodies-our-feeds.html', 'snippet': 'Two social-media campaigns take on how women’s bodies are (and are not) represented online.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last month, my friend Tracy posted a series of messages on Twitter, including one that read, “I look like a marshmallow tied up in rubber bands.” Another said, “Scientific fact: Salt and vinegar chips taste 46% better when you’re on your period.” Once I stopped laughing, I noticed she was using a hashtag I had never seen before: #LiveTweetYourPeriod. A quick tour of the tag revealed hundreds of other posts, ranging from the oblique (images of junk food) to the grotesquely exaggerated — one tweet included an image of the pivotal pigs’ blood moment in “Carrie”; another, the part in “Alien” when a creature bursts from John Hurt’s chest.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21mag-21search-t_CA0/21mag-21search-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21mag-21search-t_CA0/21mag-21search-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21mag-21search-t_CA0/21mag-21search-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 675, 'width': 675, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21mag-21search-t_CA0/21mag-21search-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 675, 'width': 675, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21mag-21search-t_CA0/21mag-21search-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandar