'nyt://article/091ef874-42fa-55f1-8012-887038fe9d57', 'word_count': 232, 'uri': 'nyt://article/091ef874-42fa-55f1-8012-887038fe9d57'}, {'abstract': 'In the end, it was the pro-democracy camp that killed a proposal for direct election of the city’s top leader, citing Beijing’s limits on who could be a candidate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/19/world/asia/hong-kong-votes-down-beijing-election-plan.html', 'snippet': 'In the end, it was the pro-democracy camp that killed a proposal for direct election of the city’s top leader, citing Beijing’s limits on who could be a candidate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — In quiet negotiations as well as public protests, they have pleaded and demanded for nearly two decades that Beijing allow Hong Kong’s leader to be elected by the general public. But in a dramatic vote on Thursday, pro-democracy lawmakers here rejected a bill that could have been their last best chance to achieve that goal.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hong Kong Legislature Rejects Beijing-Backed Election Plan', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hong Kong Lawmakers Reject Beijing Election Plan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Forsythe, Michael', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hong Kong', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wong, Alan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Communist Party of China', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voter Registration and Requirements', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-18T04:38:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Forsythe and Alan Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Forsythe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/18b158c3-0d35-56a5-a841-b1197df6a04a', 'word_count': 1304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18b158c3-0d35-56a5-a841-b1197df6a04a'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/18/arts/television/whats-on-tv-thursday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (ABC) THE ASTRONAUT WIVES CLUB Life magazine bought the rights to their stories. Jacqueline Kennedy invited them to the White House. Created by Stephanie Savage (“Gossip Girl”) from Lily Koppel’s book, this series ventures into the kitchens and boudoirs of the First Ladies of Space: seven women with iconic last names — Carpenter, Cooper, Glenn, Grissom, Schirra, Shepard and Slayton — who kept their families grounded while their men shot for the stars. “The series purports to tell the real story behind the Project Mercury publicity machine, but it is so enamored of the early-’60s aesthetic that it can’t resist adding its own flawless, cameo-faced gloss to its heroines,” Alessandra Stanley wrote in The New York Times. But the excitement “is found not in motel rooms or boozy barbecues but inside the command center at NASA, where early launches were terrifying.” The creators, she added, “may have wanted to avoid belittling their heroines in a space age version of ‘Desperate Housewives,’ but they did them a different disservice by sugarcoating them almost as much as NASA did some 50 years ago.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on TV Thursday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What&#8217;s On TV Thursday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-18T05:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/bce82021-a68f-59c1-aa7b-e6b6b710a701', 'word_count': 705, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bce82021-a68f-59c1-aa7b-e6b6b710a701'}, {'abstract': 'The United Nations refugee agency finds that war and persecution have displaced a record number of people.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/18/world/60-million-people-fleeing-chaotic-lands-un-says.html', 'snippet': 'The United Nations refugee agency finds that war and persecution have displaced a record number of people.', 'lead_paragraph': 'UNITED NATIONS — Nearly 60 million people have been driven from their homes by war and persecution, an unprecedented global exodus that has burdened fragile countries with waves of newcomers and littered deserts and seas with the bodies of those who died trying to reach safety.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/18/world/18MIGRANTS/18MIGRANTS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 376, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/18/world/18MIGRANTS/18MIGRANTS-articleLarge.jpg', 'x