, 'value': 'Helmand Province (Afghanistan)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kunduz (Afghanistan)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'North Atlantic Treaty Organization', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Musa Qala (Afghanistan)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-18T13:37:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joseph Goldstein and Taimoor Shah', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joseph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Taimoor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shah', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1044931-b89f-5e30-bc84-cfafd8028cc5', 'word_count': 633, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1044931-b89f-5e30-bc84-cfafd8028cc5'}, {'abstract': 'One group recommends a “chess clock” format, in which each candidate has 45 minutes of speaking time — and then must keep quiet.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/18/checkmating-the-presidential-debates/', 'snippet': 'One group recommends a “chess clock” format, in which each candidate has 45 minutes of speaking time — and then must keep quiet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Imagine a presidential debate in which the two finalists have far more power to clash on the show, partisan audience claques are barred, social media are offered innovative roles, and a “chess clock” format allows a candidate to slap an interruption of an adversary’s point for instant rebuttal. No less fantastic, imagine a format that scrapped the noxious post-debate “Spin Alley” where partisan cadres of campaign gurus immediately descend to massage news wo