'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Astorga (Spain)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-18T13:45:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Raphael Minder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Raphael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Minder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b9001cf-7dfa-587e-a123-31dc6e228162', 'word_count': 786, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b9001cf-7dfa-587e-a123-31dc6e228162'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Larson’s “Fireflies” series consists of photographic art made by manipulating the technology of an early fax machine.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/19/arts/design/review-in-william-larsons-fireflies-fax-transmissions-as-art.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Larson’s “Fireflies” series consists of photographic art made by manipulating the technology of an early fax machine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before there was email or the Internet, there was the revolutionary fax machine. In a statement accompanying this show, the photographer William Larson describes seeing a demonstration of the technology in 1969, in which an image of “a well-known celebrity” (Muhammad Ali) was sent from Chicago to Philadelphia. The transmission took six minutes, and Mr. Larson, witnessing the event in Philadelphia, was impressed by the picture’s high quality. After this, he obtained a Graphic Sciences DEX 1 Teleprinter for the Tyler School of Art at Temple University, where he taught. From 1969 to 1978, he made the electro-carbon prints on view here.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/19/arts/19WILLIAMLARSONGALLERY6/19WILLIAMLARSONGALLERY6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 777, 'width': 600, 'subTy