in a wave of “regeneration” that leaves niche businesses withering in the wake of big brands. Smithfield, the capital’s 800-year-old meat market is the next (contentiously) earmarked for a facelift. Others, like Borough Market – nowadays more of a gourmet tourist destination than a local amenity — are victims of their own success. Which makes below-the-radar spots like Netil Market, a small community of creative start-ups in London Fields, to the east of the city, such a rarity. This motley assortment of sheds, vans and shipping crates, which sits in the shadow of the London Overground, has a charming, if ramshackle, feel. “There’s a barber, an optician, a jeweler and lots of street food vendors,” says Paul Firmin, a co-founder of the lifestyle boutique and scent brand Earl of East London, which launched on the market last summer. “It’s like a little modern-day High Street.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/18/t-magazine/18market-farrell-slide-2791/18market-farrell-slide-2791-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/18/t-magazine/18market-farrell-slide-2791/18market-farrell-slide-2791-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/18/t-magazine/18market-farrell-slide-2791/18market-farrell-slide-2791-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/18/t-magazine/18market-farrell-slide-2791/18market-farrell-slide-2791-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None