isconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-18T17:06:47+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'AP', 'person': [], 'organization': 'AP'}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/97865146-250e-5564-a92c-d6f365ad4283', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/97865146-250e-5564-a92c-d6f365ad4283'}, {'abstract': 'There’s a lot of star power and screen charisma in the new season of this crime drama, but the four central characters live in a haze of gloom.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/19/arts/television/review-true-detective-season-2-on-hbo-plays-a-symphony-of-misery.html', 'snippet': 'There’s a lot of star power and screen charisma in the new season of this crime drama, but the four central characters live in a haze of gloom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are plenty of people who insist that auteur-driven television series like “True Detective” are the 19th-century novels of the visual age. That’s certainly arguable, though in fairness, the compliment should go both ways — Balzac, Dickens and Trollope are the showrunners of the written word. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/19/arts/19TRUED/19TRUED-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 498, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/19/arts/19TRUED/19TRUED-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 498}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/19/arts/19TRUED/19TRUED-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 850, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/19/arts/19TRUED/19TRUED-superJumbo-v2