', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/787bfdea-5c60-5603-afa3-48b068e17578', 'word_count': 550, 'uri': 'nyt://article/787bfdea-5c60-5603-afa3-48b068e17578'}, {'abstract': 'The Frick Collection said its display of Frederic Leighton’s masterpiece “Flaming June” was the first in New York City. But an East Flatbush man’s memory said otherwise.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/18/paintings-first-time-in-new-york-no-way-no-how-says-brooklyn-man/', 'snippet': 'The Frick Collection said its display of Frederic Leighton’s masterpiece “Flaming June” was the first in New York City. But an East Flatbush man’s memory said otherwise.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s not often that art history – even a small, relatively recent piece of it – is rewritten by a retired school-bus-company manager from Brooklyn. But that’s what happened this week, when Ed Miranda, of East Flatbush, read a review in The New York Times of an exhibition at the Frick Collection featuring Frederic Leighton’s lush Victorian masterpiece, “Flaming June,” which the museum said has made its maiden voyage to New York from its permanent home at the Museo de Arte de Ponce in Puerto Rico.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/19/arts/19ARTSBEATFLAMING/19ARTSBEATFLAMING-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/19/arts/19ARTSBEATFLAMING/19ARTSBEATFLAMING-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/19/arts/19ARTSBEATFLAMING/19ARTSBEATFLAMING-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': No