l, which picks up where his “The Power of the Dog” ended.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the opening page of “The Cartel,” Don Winslow mixes a baby’s cry with the sound of a helicopter’s blades in a predawn raid. That’s reminiscent of his 2005 novel, “The Power of the Dog,” which also used a baby on its opening page. “The baby is dead in his mother’s arms,” that book began, and went on to describe the horrific image of a bullet-riddled Madonna and child in Mexico. It was the first step into what would become Mr. Winslow’s drug war version of “The Godfather,” with all the grand ambition that implies.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/19/books/19cartel/19cartel-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 796, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/19/books/19cartel/19cartel-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 796}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/19/books/19cartel/19cartel-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 772, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/19/books/19cartel/19cartel-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1544, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/19/books/19cartel/19cartel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/19/books/19cartel/19cartel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/19/books/19cartel/19cartel-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘The Cartel,’ by Don Winslow, Continues a Drug Saga', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Drug War, Long and Brutal ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Winslow, Don', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Cartel: A Novel (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-18T22:15:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Janet Maslin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Janet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maslin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9bc8a771-5b43-5283-a674-68aa22a312c3', 'word_count': 971, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9bc8a771-5b43-5283-a674-68aa22a312c3'}, {'abstract': 'The choreographer arranges old snippets into something new and evocative at Roulette in Brooklyn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/20/arts/dance/review-keely-garfield-mines-kitsch-and-past-material-in-pow.html', 'snippet': 'The choreographer arranges old snippets into something new and evocative at Roulette in Brooklyn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anything by the choreographer Keely Garfield is worth watching multiple times. There is so much to see, so much to delight in and puzzle through, that once is rarely enough. Happily for her audience, Ms. Garfield, too, believes in revisiting her own material. In “Pow,” seen on Wednesday at Roulette in Brooklyn, she doesn’t restage her previous work so much as reimagine and repurpose it, arranging old snippets into something new.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedi