65'}, {'abstract': 'Poem selected by Natasha Trethewey.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/magazine/the-good-survives.html', 'snippet': 'Poem selected by Natasha Trethewey.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a child, I would often recite poems to banish some painful or unpleasant memory. Words became a kind of talisman, as they are in this poem, a way of willing the mind to recollect all the good that lives alongside what we want to forget.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21poem/21poem-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 601, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21poem/21poem-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 601}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21poem/21poem-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 704, 'width': 703, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21poem/21poem-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 704, 'width': 703, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21poem/21mag-21poem-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21poem/21mag-21poem-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21poem/21mag-21poem-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Good Survives’', 'kicker': 'Poem', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Good Survives', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trethewey, Natasha', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Webb, Charles Harper', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-19T10:59:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Harper Webb', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': 'Harper', 'lastname': 'Webb', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aebbefd8-8bd0-59d9-9604-851069b068e6', 'word_count': 51, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aebbefd8-8bd0-59d9-9604-851069b068e6'}, {'abstract': 'Find an incurious animal. Use your own blood.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/magazine/how-to-train-a-cadaver-dog.html', 'snippet': 'Find an incurious animal. Use your own blood.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Drug dogs are trained on drugs, and cadaver dogs are trained on cadaver,” says Mary E. Cablk, a scientist at the Desert Research Institute in Nevada who studies scent detection. Training a cadaver dog requires regular contact with human blood, decaying flesh and bones. In the United States, dog handlers can legally obtain bodily components like human placenta and blood, but not always easily, and trainers like Cablk often resort to using their own blood. Some substitute commercially available ersatz odors (the most common is Sigma Pseudo Corpse Scent, which comes in three kinds: recently dead, decomposed and drowned). But deceased humans produce unique volatile organic compounds, and canines have a keenly attuned sense of smell, so you should practice on the real thing. “You don’t want to put other people at risk,” Cablk says — searches are often in dangerous areas, like collapsed buildings — “to find what is ultimately a dead pet.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21tip/21tip-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 515, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21tip/21tip-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 515}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21tip/21tip-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 878, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21tip/21tip-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1757, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21tip/21tip-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21tip/21tip-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21tip/21tip-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Train a Cadaver Dog', 'kicker': 'Tip', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How to Train a Cadaver Dog', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dogs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wollan, Malia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Death and Dying', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smells and Odors', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cablk, Mary E', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cadavers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-19T10:59:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Malia Wollan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Malia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wollan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eca560b1-bce7-5464-b03f-c2158332d466', 'word_count': 371, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eca560b1-bce7-5464-b03f-c2158332d466'}, {'abstract': 'The holiday marking the final end of slavery should be more than just an African-American celebration.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/19/opinion/juneteenth-is-for-everyone.html', 'snippet': 'The holiday marking the final end of slavery should be more than just an African-American celebration.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SOME two months after Gen. Robert E. Lee surrendered on April 9, 1865, effectively ending the Civil War, Maj. Gen. Gordon Granger steamed into the port of Galveston, Tex. With 1,800 Union soldiers, including a contingent of United States Colored Troops, Granger was there to establish martial law over the westernmost state in the defeated Confederacy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Juneteenth Is for Everyone', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Juneteenth Is for Everyone', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery (Historical)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emancipation Proclamation (1863)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Southern States (US)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Juneteenth', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-19T11:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kenneth C. Davis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kenneth', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Davis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/56a0dc50-53c0-5281-b098-1ae475e6f9e0', 'word_count': 852, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56a0dc50-53c0-5281-b098-1ae475e6f9e0'}, {'abstract': 'The graphic designers Justus Oehler and Uta Tjaden live in a 1928 Bauhaus-style villa in Berlin.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/magazine/exclamation-point-architecture.html', 'snippet': 'The graphic designers Justus Oehler and Uta Tjaden live in a 1928 Bauhaus-style villa in Berlin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The graphic designers Justus Oehler and Uta Tjaden live in a 1928 Bauhaus-style villa in Berlin, designed by Arthur Korn, who fled Nazi Germany in the 1930s. When the couple first saw the landmark house in 2003, it had been empty for three years and badly damaged by water from burst pipes. With the help of the architect Florian Höll, who largely preserved Arthur Korn’s floor plan, the villa was returned to its original design. The villa is one of only about a dozen buildings Korn designed in Berlin, not all of which survived.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Exclamation-Point Architecture', 'kicker': 'Domains', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Exclamation-Point Architecture', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stauss, Luise', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Berlin (Germany)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oehler, Justus', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tjaden, Uta', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Korn, Arthur (1870-1945)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-19T11:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Luise Stauss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Luise', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stauss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c8aab28-e99d-5bce-bb20-b02685f1577e', 'word_count': 114, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c8aab28-e99d-5bce-bb20-b02685f1577e'}, {'abstract': 'Countries, religions and corporations sometimes do awful things in their names. It doesn’t diminish them to note their failures; it elevates them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/19/opinion/an-apology-for-slavery.html', 'snippet': 'Countries, religions and corporations sometimes do awful things in their names. It doesn’t diminish them to note their failures; it elevates them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A WEEK of absurdity around a confused racial con artist, and a massacre in a black church brings us to this: Today is the 150th anniversary of Juneteenth, when the last of the American slaves were told they were free. Now, to put it to good use, at a time when a post-racial era seems very much out of reach.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/egan-circular/egan-circular-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/egan-circular/egan-circular-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/egan-circular/egan-circular-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/egan-circular/egan-circular-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/egan-circular/egan-circular-thumbLarge-v4.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Apologize for Slavery', 'kicker': 'Contributing Op-Ed Writer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Apologize for Slavery', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery (Historical)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Egan, Timothy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Apologies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Charleston (SC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hate Crimes', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-19T11:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Timothy Egan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Timothy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Egan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/65ab56a4-6eda-595d-b4b9-3323148cf7ca', 'word_count': 857, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65ab56a4-6eda-595d-b4b9-3323148cf7ca'}, {'abstract': 'Women have had the right to vote for nearly 100 years, but it has taken a lot longer than that to accept the notion of one in the highest office.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/20/upshot/changing-views-on-a-female-president.html', 'snippet': 'Women have had the right to vote for nearly 100 years, but it has taken a lot longer than that to accept the notion of one in the highest office.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Saturday, Hillary Rodham Clinton told thousands of people at a rally on Roosevelt Island in New York City that she wanted the United States to be a place “where a father can tell his daughter yes, you can be anything you want to be, even president of the United States.” It was a line she used in her concession speech in 2008 and one that highlights what could happen if she or, less likely, Carly Fiorina win her party’s nomination and ultimately the presidency. Nearly 100 years after women were given the right to vote, Americans may send their first woman to the Oval Office.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/18/upshot/18UP-VAVRECK/18UP-VAVRECK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 411, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/18/upshot/18UP-VAVRECK/18UP-VAVRECK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 411}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/18/upshot/18UP-VAVRECK/18UP-VAVRECK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 702, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/18/upshot/18UP-VAVRECK/18UP-VAVRECK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1403, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/18/upshot/18UP-VAVRECK/18UP-VAVRECK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/18/upshot/18UP-VAVRECK/18UP-VAVRECK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/18/upshot/18UP-VAVRECK/18UP-VAVRECK-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Changing Views on a Female President', 'kicker': "A Woman's Place", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Hillary Rodham', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-19T11:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Vavreck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vavreck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d62d116-d9ad-5536-bfad-257a9ccb79b6', 'word_count': 920, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d62d116-d9ad-5536-bfad-257a9ccb79b6'}, {'abstract': 'Learning how to use a field guide can make you feel at home anywhere in the world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/magazine/identification-please.html', 'snippet': 'Learning how to use a field guide can make you feel at home anywhere in the world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m standing on a high lookout near a spectacular three-tiered waterfall in Australia’s Blue Mountains National Park. The peaks in the far distance reflect sunshine scattered through a haze of aromatic eucalyptus oil; the light has turned them a pale and dusty blue. At my feet the land falls away into a virgin forest of graceful, pale-barked trees that runs as far as the eye can see. Farther up the slope are leggy shrubs with flowers resembling bright plastic hair curlers: banksias, I think. When a small bird appears in the foliage below I fix it in my binoculars. White, black and acid yellow, it has eyes like tiny silver coins, and it is wiping its downcurved beak on a branch of a shrub with strappy leaves. I don’t know what the shrub is, and I’m not sure what the bird is, either. I think it’s a honeyeater, but I don’t know what anything is, not precisely. The air smells faintly of old paper and something strangely like jet fuel. I feel lost and very far from home.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21onnature1/21mag-21onnature-t_CA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21onnature1/21mag-21onnature-t_CA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21onnature1/21mag-21onnature-t_CA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21onnature1/21mag-21onnature-t_CA1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21onnature1/21onnature1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21onnature1/21onnature1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/magazine/21onnature1/21onnature1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Identification, Please', 'kicker': 'On Nature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Learning the Names of Creatures and Plants Can Make You Feel at Home Anywhere in the World.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wilderness Areas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Macdonald, Helen (Author)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flowers and Plants', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insects', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANIMALS', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parks and Other Recreation Areas', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Australia', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-19T11:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Helen Macdonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Helen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macdonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df5557e8-211f-5797-a744-6111626c7df7', 'word_count': 1423, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df5557e8-211f-5797-a744-6111626c7df7'}, {'abstract': 'Donna Brothers, a racing analyst for NBC and a former jockey, had to check her emotions while covering American Pharoah’s Triple Crown victory.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/sports/holding-back-tears-after-american-pharoahs-triple-crown.html', 'snippet': 'Donna Brothers, a racing analyst for NBC and a former jockey, had to check her emotions while covering American Pharoah’s Triple Crown victory.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Given time to think about how I felt witnessing American Pharoah’s Triple Crown sweep on June 6, my conclusion may be surprising: I felt robbed and a bit sad. Sure, I also felt joy and enormous gratitude toward my employer, NBC, which entrusts me with the responsibility of interviewing the winning jockey on horseback. But I have waited my whole life for this Triple Crown.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/20/sports/21RACING-01/21RACING-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 480, 'width': 600, 'subType':