man feels free to be anyone she chooses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Midway through “The Diver’s Clothes Lie Empty,” Vendela Vida’s nameless protagonist finds herself sitting in a private V.I.P. lounge in the Regency Hotel in Casablanca. She is chatting with the personal bodyguard of an American movie star. The bodyguard describes his interest in the evolutionary theory of “punctuated equilibrium,” in which species, faced with a dramatic change in their circumstances, adapt rapidly. The young woman, who is being vetted for the part of stand-in to the famous actress in a film being shot in Casablanca, feigns amiable curiosity. She is desperate to appear sufficiently sane and normal to get the job; she doesn’t want the bodyguard to realize that she herself is living proof of “punctuated equilibrium,” that the sequence of events that have occurred in the 48 hours since her arrival in Casablanca have propelled this previously unremarkable woman into committing a series of frauds that could get her into an awful lot of trouble.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21EBERSTADT/21Eberstadt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21EBERSTADT/21Eberstadt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21EBERSTADT/21Eberstadt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21EBERSTADT/21Eberstadt-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21EBERSTADT/21EBERSTADT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21EBERSTADT/21EBERSTADT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21EBERSTADT/21EBERSTADT-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Diver’s Clothes Lie Empty,’ by Vendela Vida', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Foreign Exchange', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vida, Vendela', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "The Diver's Clothes Lie Empty (Book)", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-19T15:43:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fernanda Eberstadt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fernanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eberstadt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6295950f-9b9b-5601-87c1-b6b3724c7d86', 'word_count': 1153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6295950f-9b9b-5601-87c1-b6b3724c7d86'}, {'abstract': 'Ali Wentworth, whose essay collection “Happily Ali After” is No. 13 on the hardcover nonfiction list, once said: “I was born funny. I think it’s instinctual. I don’t think you can be taught.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Ali Wentworth, whose essay collection “Happily Ali After” is No. 13 on the hardcover nonfiction list, once said: “I was born funny. I think it’s instinctual. I don’t think you can be taught.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Born Funny: The actress and comedian Ali Wentworth hits this week’s hardcover nonfiction list with “Happily Ali After” (new at No. 13), an essay collection about her mostly sincere efforts at self-\xadimprovement as her 50th birthday approached. “I realized I faced some obstacles in my path to spiritual enlightenment,” she writes in the introduction. “I have been accused of being cynical and jaded on this particular subject. I don’t read self-help books, except the ones that guarantee I’ll lose 40 pounds in one hour. And I enjoy being co\xaddependent. My best life is never going to resemble, say, Angelina Jolie’s; I don’t have the cheekbones, can’t fly my own plane, and refugees make me too sad. But perhaps I could step up the consciousness a notch.” This is Wentworth’s second book, after the 2012 best seller “Ali in Wonderland,” a comic memoir about her political childhood (her mother was Nancy Reagan’s social secretary), her Hollywood career and her life as Mrs. George Stephanopoulos. That book’s fans included Jackie Collins — who called it, in these pages, the funniest thing she had ever read — and Charlie Rose, who on his show asked Wentworth where her sense of humor came from. “I was born funny,” she told him. “I think it’s instinctual. I don’t think you can be taught. .\u2008.\u2008. And I love being unscripted. I mean, this kind of thing to me is one of my favorite things to do. I don’t think you’re going to see me on ‘Law and Order: SVU’ any time soon, you know.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28insidelist/28insidelist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 454, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28insidelist/28insidelist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 454, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28insidelist/28insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28insidelist/28insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28insidelist/28insidelist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-19T15:43:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a066c22-bd3e-5cc6-98c1-4d52be365d9f', 'word_count': 498, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a066c22-bd3e-5cc6-98c1-4d52be365d9f'}, {'abstract': 'The hunt for a missing pop star takes a philosophical turn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/books/review/the-ghost-network-by-catie-disabato.html', 'snippet': 'The hunt for a missing pop star takes a philosophical turn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When you start a movement to shift culture away from “the society of the spectacle,” who better to push its goals than a pop star? That question, and the action it inspires, is the driving force behind Catie Disabato’s “The Ghost Network,” a smart and thorny debut novel with a mystery at its heart.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21GRAY/21GRAY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 903, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21GRAY/21GRAY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 903}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21GRAY/21GRAY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 681, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21GRAY/21GRAY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1361, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21GRAY/21GRAY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21GRAY/21GRAY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21GRAY/21GRAY-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Ghost Network,’ by Catie Disabato', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Search Engine', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Disabato, Catherine (1986- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-19T15:44:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amelia Gray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amelia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4a61c35-c27a-5540-9274-687999eb5284', 'word_count': 599, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4a61c35-c27a-5540-9274-687999eb5284'}, {'abstract': 'We can’t help being just a bit phony, Eric G. Wilson argues, but maybe that isn’t so bad.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/books/review/keep-it-fake-by-eric-g-wilson.html', 'snippet': 'We can’t help being just a bit phony, Eric G. Wilson argues, but maybe that isn’t so bad.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Toward the end of his terrific new philosophical investigation, “Keep It Fake: Inventing an Authentic Life,” Eric G. Wilson admits that, as with many of us, learning how to be a good dad was something he had a tough time figuring out. On the cusp of fatherhood he was working too much, and drinking too much — sound familiar? — and battling depression. But he had the good sense and good luck to find an excellent psychiatrist.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Martin/21-Martin-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 332, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Martin/21-Martin-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 332}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Martin/21-Martin-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 566, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Martin/21-Martin-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1132, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Martin/21-Martin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Martin/21-Martin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21-Martin/21-Martin-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Keep It Fake,’ by Eric G. Wilson', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Way Things Ought to Be', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Eric G', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-19T15:44:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clancy Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clancy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2229375-8d01-5f46-9e6e-3da1cbb6df6e', 'word_count': 1168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2229375-8d01-5f46-9e6e-3da1cbb6df6e'}, {'abstract': 'Nine stories give voice to the people of the Filipino diaspora.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/books/review/in-the-country-by-mia-alvar.html', 'snippet': 'Nine stories give voice to the people of the Filipino diaspora.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Tagalog movies, the bida, or hero, battles the kontrabida, or villain, for the affections of a beautiful woman. As his family cheers at the bida’s victory in the opening story of “In the Country,” Steve, a Filipino expat on a visit home, reflects: “The script had succumbed, in the end, to our demands.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21RAMAK/21Ramak-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21RAMAK/21Ramak-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21RAMAK/21Ramak-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21RAMAK/21Ramak-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1365, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21RAMAK/21Ramak-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21RAMAK/21Ramak-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21RAMAK/21Ramak-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘In the Country,’ by Mia Alvar', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Home Bittersweet Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Alvar, Mia C (1978- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-19T15:44:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. R. Ramakrishnan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Ramakrishnan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4787e2a2-824f-5c78-80c7-b57674973760', 'word_count': 611, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4787e2a2-824f-5c78-80c7-b57674973760'}, {'abstract': 'Old rivalries surface amid the 1992 Los Angeles riots.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/books/review/all-involved-by-ryan-gattis.html', 'snippet': 'Old rivalries surface amid the 1992 Los Angeles riots.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In recent years, riot footage has practically become a subgenre of documentary filmmaking. We recognize the hallmarks — advancing police phalanxes, protesters in retreat, looters, burning cruisers, \xadcameras tracking airborne projectiles — without always understanding the causes. While the 1992 Los Angeles riots lacked the exhaustive citizen reportage of more recent incidents, there was enough videotape running back then to give the rest of the country the lasting impression of a restless city entirely engulfed in chaos. In his latest novel, “All Involved,” Ryan Gattis imagines a narrative that, while mostly tangential to that chaos, attempts to give it more meaningful context through personalization. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21DRUMMING/21DRUMMING-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 408, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21DRUMMING/21DRUMMING-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 408}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21DRUMMING/21DRUMMING-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 697, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21DRUMMING/21DRUMMING-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1394, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21DRUMMING/21DRUMMING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21DRUMMING/21DRUMMING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21DRUMMING/21DRUMMING-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘All Involved,’ by Ryan Gattis', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'City Under Siege', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gattis, Ryan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'All Involved: A Novel (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-19T15:44:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Drumming', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Drumming', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b124242e-c3c2-5ef4-b8ab-d9c3b7de875e', 'word_count': 605, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b124242e-c3c2-5ef4-b8ab-d9c3b7de875e'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Roy’s new novel is a Depression-era Southern Gothic story set in a Kentucky farming town.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/books/review/lori-roys-let-me-die-in-his-footsteps-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Roy’s new novel is a Depression-era Southern Gothic story set in a Kentucky farming town.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Open LET ME DIE IN HIS FOOTSTEPS (Dutton, $26.95) anywhere and Lori Roy’s melodious voice will float off the page, calling your attention to the way honeybees are soothed into a kind of stupor by the scent of lavender, or instructing you in the wisdom of hanging milk snakes by the front door to keep away evil. And when the teller of this Southern Gothic yarn really wants to engage your mind, her insinuating voice will direct you to the last public hanging held in the United States and a lingering curse on the Kentucky farming town that hosted the spectacle in the early dawn of a cold, damp day in 1936. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21CRIME/21-Crime-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 797, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21CRIME/21-Crime-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 797}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21CRIME/21-Crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 772, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21CRIME/21-Crime-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1543, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21CRIME/21CRIME-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21CRIME/21CRIME-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21CRIME/21CRIME-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lori Roy’s ‘Let Me Die in His Footsteps,’ and More', 'kicker': 'Crime | ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Long Way Down', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hughes, Andrew (Author)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lotz, Sarah', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roy, Lori', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hall, Rachel Howzell (1970- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-19T15:44:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f805bccf-e9cf-5d20-84ba-2dd290779b67', 'word_count': 922, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f805bccf-e9cf-5d20-84ba-2dd290779b67'}, {'abstract': 'Children witness fantastical events at a theme park and a mysterious carnival in two middle-grade novels. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/books/review/circus-mirandus-and-return-to-augie-hobble.html', 'snippet': 'Children witness fantastical events at a theme park and a mysterious carnival in two middle-grade novels. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'So all the world’s a stage, and we are merely players hoofing upon it? I bet Shakespeare picked up that nugget at Stratford-upon-Avon Middle School. Middle school ushers in the Narcissistic Age, a mannerist phase that comes between the magic years of learning to read and the disheartening Enlightenment that occurs between early high school and, say, midlife crisis. An Enlightenment that has to take in the reality of death.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Circus Mirandus’ and ‘Return to Augie Hobble’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Where Magic Thrives', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beasley, Cassie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Lane', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sudyka, Diana (1972- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-19T15:44:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Maguire', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maguire', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/caea2e7c-4758-509a-a21b-32825a41274f', 'word_count': 700, 'uri': 'nyt://article/caea2e7c-4758-509a-a21b-32825a41274f'}, {'abstract': 'In four picture books, the evening gives rise to dreams and the urge to explore shadowy worlds. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/books/review/dahlov-ipcars-black-and-white-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'In four picture books, the evening gives rise to dreams and the urge to explore shadowy worlds. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'The night, as a fervent lyric made famous by Patti Smith implies, may not belong to preschoolers. But it is nonetheless a time — and mystery-laden phenomenon — that fascinates all young children, and arouses fear in many. From the ritualized consolations of “Goodnight Moon” to the antic \xadrebellion of “In the Night Kitchen,” picture books have charted countless courses through the dark. The tradition continues in three new books and one notable reissue.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21MARCUS1SUB/21MARCUS1SUB-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 375, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21MARCUS1SUB/21MARCUS1SUB-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 375}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21MARCUS1SUB/21MARCUS1SUB-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 640, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21MARCUS1SUB/21MARCUS1SUB-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1125, 'width': 1800, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21MARCUS1SUB/21-Marcus-1-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21MARCUS1SUB/21-Marcus-1-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/books/review/21MARCUS1SUB/21-Marcus-1-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Dahlov Ipcar’s ‘Black and White,’ and More', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Night Visions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marcus, Leonard S', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gerstein, Mordicai', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Potter, Giselle', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Desbordes, Astrid', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ipcar, Dahlov (1917- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boutavant, Marc (1970- )', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Black and White (Book)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-19T15:44:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Leonard S. Marcus', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leonard', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Marcus', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f08ac096-92aa-56da-be18-59e73569cab2', 'word_count': 1000, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f08ac096-92aa-56da-be18-59e73569cab2'}, {'abstract': 'Two books feature epic battles on a minuscule scale and heroes in the form of vermin.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/books/review/ratscalibur-and-super-fly.html', 'snippet': 'Two books feature epic battles on a minuscule scale and heroes in the form of vermin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Animals can be an author’s best friend. Talking animals, to be precise. Since the dawn of folklore, anthropomorphic beasties have been reliable go-to guys when a story simply wouldn’t be as much fun with plain old human protagonists. And so it is that Josh Lieb, in “Ratscalibur,” layers on the fur — and the claws, and the skinny pink tails — to transform the archetypal “unassuming kid fulfills prophecy and saves the kingdom” plot into a witty mash-up of favorite fantasy motifs. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Ratscalibur’ and ‘Super Fly’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Knights in Tiny Armor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lieb, Josh', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Doodler, Todd H (1968- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-19T15:44:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f6d810e-3b8d-5ab8-b885-2d23be1151c1', 'word_count': 700, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f6d810e-3b8d-5ab8-b885-2d23be1151c1'}, {'abstract': 'A Bronx teenager struggles with sexuality and painful memories in this debut young adult novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/books/review/more-happy-than-not-by-adam-silvera.html', 'snippet': 'A Bronx teenager struggles with sexuality and painful memories in this debut young adult novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If only for the outsize cultural imprint left by “The Hunger Games,” it is reasonable to argue that young adult fiction has done a far more aggressive job grappling with social inequality than much of what is rendered today in the name of literary fiction, a world where poor and \xadworking-class characters are so often visible merely at the periphery, if they are visible at all. The novel “Panic,” for example, by the best-selling Y.A. author Lauren Oliver, is set in a small town so devoid of prospects that graduating seniors spend their time battling it out in a game whose grand prize is the unimaginable sum of $67,000. In Laurie Halse Anderson’s “The Impossible Knife of Memory,” a teenager returns home after several years on the road with her truck-driver father, an Iraq war veteran who suffers from PTSD. We are far, in other words, from the moneyed insularities of Exeter.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘More Happy Than Not,’ by Adam Silvera', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Clean Slate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Silvera, Adam (1990- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-19T15:44:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ginia Bellafante', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bellafante', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce05d3f6-84ee-5361-bbbf-54f7090cf4be', 'word_count': 738, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce05d3f6-84ee-5361-bbbf-54f7090cf4be'}, {'abstract': 'Senator Lindsey Graham and an N.R.A. board member had incredibly inappropriate reactions to this act of hate.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/19/how-not-to-respond-to-the-murders-in-charleston/', 'snippet': 'Senator Lindsey Graham and an N.R.A. board member had incredibly inappropriate reactions to this act of hate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The world has seen an outpouring of grief and outrage since nine people were gunned down in a clearly racist act of vicious murder in a historic black church in Charleston, South Carolina. There are many appropriate and decent ways to mourn those victims, to denounce the avowed racist who is accused of murdering them, and to ruminate on the state of race relations in our country.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/19/opinion/19rosenthalWEB2/19rosenthalWEB2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/19/opinion/19rosenthalWEB2/19rosenthalWEB2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 391}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/19/opinion/19rosenthalWEB2/19rosenthalWEB2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'supe