': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d59be52e-03a4-5f1f-bec3-4d2a00ae3a94', 'word_count': 140, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d59be52e-03a4-5f1f-bec3-4d2a00ae3a94'}, {'abstract': 'Jon Caramanica and Ben Ratliff discuss the women of country music.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/19/popcast-women-and-country-music/', 'snippet': 'Jon Caramanica and Ben Ratliff discuss the women of country music.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On this week’s Popcast, we discuss the situation for women in country music right now. The occasion is Kacey Musgraves’ new album “Pageant Material” — for which, as Mr. Caramanica wrote in Arts and Leisure this week, “she’ll be pegged as an agent of change, a palliative for this genre’s many ills”—but also a recent statement by the country-radio consultant Keith Hill that women listeners, the prime demographic of country radio, preferred to hear male singers. To what degree is Ms. Musgraves’ album a satisfying response? Other singers discussed: Carrie Underwood, Miranda Lambert, Kelsea Ballerini, Ashley Monroe, Angaleena Presley, Laura Bell Bundy, Mickey Guyton, Jana Kramer, Cam, Maddie & Tae, Reba McEntire.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Popcast: Women and Country Music', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-06-19T17:39:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By BEN RATLIFF', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'RATLIFF', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_materia