 Rifle Assn', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Charleston (SC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-19T21:38:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Politics', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/05ba44d3-0408-591a-9fe5-64738ef3712f', 'word_count': 433, 'uri': 'nyt://article/05ba44d3-0408-591a-9fe5-64738ef3712f'}, {'abstract': 'Prime Minister Modi’s goal for International Yoga Day is to promote India’s brand, not the Hindu religion.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/20/opinion/india-and-the-politics-of-yoga.html', 'snippet': 'Prime Minister Modi’s goal for International Yoga Day is to promote India’s brand, not the Hindu religion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BALTIMORE — ATTENTION, downward-facing-dog enthusiasts. The International Day of Yoga is coming on Sunday. A brainchild of Narendra Modi, the Indian prime minister, it is being backed by more than 175 countries and heavily promoted worldwide. Last week, the Indian Embassy in Washington emailed me an invitation to celebrate on the National Mall, complete with a downloadable yoga “protocol” of exercises. Even Ban Ki-moon, the United Nations secretary general, has been seen, shoeless, practicing his tree pose in Delhi.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/20/opinion/20Suri/20Suri-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 927, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/20/opinion/20Suri/20Suri-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 927}}, {'rank': 0, 