ews_desk': 'Fashion & Style', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By BILL CUNNINGHAM', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'CUNNINGHAM', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/9969d7dd-9585-52df-8d1f-e9aa9bfcb23a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/9969d7dd-9585-52df-8d1f-e9aa9bfcb23a'}, {'abstract': 'Men have a new knot: the bow tie, which has been spotted at polo matches, at the Belmont Stakes, at picnics and at dance events.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/style/bill-cunningham-classy-topper.html', 'snippet': 'Men have a new knot: the bow tie, which has been spotted at polo matches, at the Belmont Stakes, at picnics and at dance events.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For generations, men have tried to get rid of the necktie noose around their necks. They haven’t succeeded. Now they have a new knot: the bow tie, which has been spotted at polo matches, at the Belmont Stakes, at picnics and at dance events. Along with the bow tie have come straw hats and suspenders. The trousers worn with these can be long or Bermuda length. It’s what’s on top that defines a new generation’s dress code for today’s casual weekend events. It stands in stark contrast to a few years ago, when men were flinging themselves into wrinkled shirts and cut-off jeans.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bill Cunningham |\xa0Bountiful Bow Ties', 'kicker': 'On the Street', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Classy Topper', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cunningham, Bill', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ties (Apparel)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Belmont Stakes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-19T22:34:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Cunningham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cunningham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0528d059-42ea-5656-87bc-476fab96b6e4', 'word_count': 105, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0528d059-42ea-5656-87bc-476fab96b6e4'}, {'abstract': 'Hesitant public speakers are engaging their own private Cyranos to write toasts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/style/toast-whisperers-ghostwriters-personal-speeches.html', 'snippet': 'Hesitant public speakers are engaging their own private Cyranos to write toasts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Tom Ruggiero was asked to offer a toast at the wedding of his friends Jessie and Ben last year, he panicked. A 32-year-old real estate lawyer in New York, Mr. Ruggiero was not accustomed to public speaking.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/fashion/21TOAST/21TOAST-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 486, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/21/fashion/21TOAST/21TOAST-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 486}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/fashion/21TOAST/21TOAST-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 802, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/fashion/21TOAST/21TOAST-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 802, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/fashion/21TOAST/21TOAST-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/21/fashion/21TOAST/21TOAST-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/fashion/21TOAST/21TOAST-thumbLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Turning to a Ghostwriter for a Personal Toast', 'kicker': 'This Life', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hear, Hear!', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Feiler, Bruce', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Public Speaking', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ghostwriting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oratory Laboratory', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-19T22:37:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Feiler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Feiler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/386903e4-5dd2-519f-9c89-e97fae887c5d', 'word_count': 1676, 'uri': 'nyt://article/386903e4-5dd2-519f-9c89-e97fae887c5d'}, {'abstract': 'The disaster of March 11, 2011, is the ostensible subject of the images on display, but their deeper subject is fear and anxiety.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/20/arts/design/review-japanese-photographers-reflect-on-the-fukushima-catastrophe.html', 'snippet': 'The disaster of March 11, 2011, is the ostensible subject of the images on display, but their deeper subject is fear and anxiety.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON — What’s the purpose, what’s the effect of creating “art” photographs about natural disasters after they happen? News photographs will have already stirred up horror; will this be more of the same? The curators of “In the Wake: Japanese Photographers Respond to 3/11” at the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, pose those questions and more in this provocative exhibition by 17 photographers who reacted to the earthquake, tsunami and breach of the Fukushima nuclear plant in northeastern Japan on March 11, 2011, after the news-media coverage.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Japanese Photographers Reflect on the Fukushima Catastrophe', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Catastrophe Reconsidered', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museum of Fine Arts (Boston)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Japan Earthquake and Tsunami (2011)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant (Japan)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-19T22:38:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vicki Goldberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vicki', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/efe47e99-6395-5150-b018-f19f6fb5b129', 'word_count': 1068, 'uri': 'nyt://article/efe47e99-6395-5150-b018-f19f6fb5b129'}, {'abstract': 'Few news reports mentioned the pioneering journalist who predated Lester Holt in the anchor’s chair.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/21/fashion/max-robinson-a-largely-forgotten-trailblazer-for-black-anchors.html', 'snippet': 'Few news reports mentioned the pioneering journalist who predated Lester Holt in the anchor’s chair.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When NBC announced on Thursday that Lester Holt would replace the beleaguered Brian Williams as anchor of “NBC Nightly News,” it was widely applauded.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/fashion/21ROBINSON1/21ROBINSON1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 434, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/21/fashion/21ROBINSON1/21ROBINSON1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 434}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/fashion/21ROBINSON1/21ROBINSON1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 741, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/fashion/21ROBINSON1/21ROBINSON1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1482, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/21/fashion/21ROBINSON1/21ROBINSON1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/21/fashion/21ROBINSON1/21ROBINSON1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge