usiness', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41377b7c-a0d0-59a4-93c9-a9e4f76c859b', 'word_count': 449, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41377b7c-a0d0-59a4-93c9-a9e4f76c859b'}, {'abstract': 'After the Civil War, black churches became what the scholar W. E. B. Du Bois and others have described as the “first social institution fully controlled by black men in America.” And before long, those institutions became targets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/20/us/in-charleston-black-church-is-target-again-for-deadly-strike-at-the-heart.html', 'snippet': 'After the Civil War, black churches became what the scholar W. E. B. Du Bois and others have described as the “first social institution fully controlled by black men in America.” And before long, those institutions became targets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Atlanta, the former slaves sang their hallelujahs in an abandoned railroad boxcar. In Lincoln County, N.C., they called out to Jesus in a building made of old pine poles. Near Cartersville, Ga., they raised their voices to the heavens from a roofless barrel shop.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Black Church Is Target Again for Deadly Strike at the Heart', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Black Church Is Target Again for Deadly Strike at the Heart', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Swarns, Rachel L', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Charleston, SC, Shooting (2015)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders, Attempted Murders and Homicides', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Emanuel African Methodist Episcopal Church (Charleston, SC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Churches (Buildings)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hate Crimes', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AFRICAN METHODIST EPISCOPAL CHURCH', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roof, Dylann Storm', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Charleston (SC)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-20T02:02:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel L. Swarns and Campbell Robertson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Swarns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Campbell', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robertson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/71006141-4a73-5a48-8b76-dd8e6e0fc8fc', 'word_count': 1108, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71006141-4a73-5a48-8b76-dd8e6e0fc8fc'}, {'abstract': 'In addressing the brutality of the punishment at length in an opinion, he raises a constitutional question some colleagues might prefer to avoid.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/20/opinion/justice-kennedy-on-solitary-confinement.html', 'snippet': 'In addressing the brutality of the punishment at length in an opinion, he raises a constitutional question some colleagues might prefer to avoid.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Supreme Court’s decision in the case of Hector Ayala, who has been on California’s death row for 25 years, turned on the question of whether a judge could hear a prosecutor’s reasons for dismissing people from the jury po