1:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles V. Bagli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': 'V.', 'lastname': 'Bagli', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f16d7ca-9418-53ee-b372-c005de7073db', 'word_count': 815, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f16d7ca-9418-53ee-b372-c005de7073db'}, {'abstract': 'Human rights groups questioned President Obama’s trip, saying that Ethiopia has silenced dissent with arbitrary arrests, mistreatment of detainees and repressive legislation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/20/us/politics/first-presidential-trip-to-ethiopia-planned.html', 'snippet': 'Human rights groups questioned President Obama’s trip, saying that Ethiopia has silenced dissent with arbitrary arrests, mistreatment of detainees and repressive legislation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama will travel next month to Ethiopia to meet with African Union leaders and discuss ways to lift economic growth, strengthen democratic institutions and improve security, the White House announced on Friday. The visit, the first to Ethiopia by an American president, will follow a stop in Kenya, the home country of his father’s family. Human rights groups questioned the trip, saying that Ethiopia has silenced dissent with arbitrary arrests, mistreatment of detainees and repressive legislation. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'First Presidential Trip to Ethiopia Planned', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Washington', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'First Presidential Trip to Ethiopia Planned', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baker, Peter', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ethiopia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AFRICAN UNION', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kenya', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-20T02:11:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f63ef04-0179-5781-a1a2-c864d8ef8371', 'word_count': 77, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f63ef04-0179-5781-a1a2-c864d8ef8371'}, {'abstract': 'Stumbles have been a common sight on the slick surface at Chambers Bay this week, but Day’s episode of vertigo was a much scarier moment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/20/sports/golf/us-open-2015-mood-turns-serious-after-jason-days-health-scare.html', 'snippet': 'Stumbles have been a common sight on the slick surface at Chambers Bay this week, but Day’s episode of vertigo was a much scarier moment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'UNIVERSITY PLACE, Wash. — The 115th United States Open had the wind briefly knocked out of it Friday afternoon when one of the leaders, Jason Day, fell in a heap on the ninth green. Day, sitting two strokes behind the pacesetters after a birdie at the eighth, was walking toward his ball, which had landed in a back bunker.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/20/sports/CROUSE1/CROUSE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/20/sports/CROUSE1/CROUSE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0,