06-21T22:03:36+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Arts', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/09b508c3-985c-52c4-90ed-899724eb7022', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/09b508c3-985c-52c4-90ed-899724eb7022'}, {'abstract': 'The opera, which will be given its first performance since 1928, offers a glimpse of a nearly forgotten chapter of African-American operatic achievement.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/22/arts/music/voodoo-opera-by-the-african-american-composer-h-lawrence-freeman-is-revived.html', 'snippet': 'The opera, which will be given its first performance since 1928, offers a glimpse of a nearly forgotten chapter of African-American operatic achievement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'He was described as a black Wagner in the late 19th century, went on to write more than 20 operas and formed the Negro Grand Opera Company, which he once conducted at Carnegie Hall. But after the pioneering African-American composer H. Lawrence Freeman died in 1954, he fell into obscurity, with his works unpublished, unrecorded and, for decades, unperformed. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Voodoo,’ Opera by the African-American Composer H. Lawrence Freeman, Is Revived', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bringing an Opera Back to Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cooper, Michael', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Freeman, Harry Lawrence (1869-1954)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Miller Theater at Columbia University', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Voodoo (Opera)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-21T22:03:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cooper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8eca843b-6668-59c1-8c7f-a943b0bd56f2', 'word_count': 1015, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8eca843b-6668-59c1-8c7f-a943b0bd56f2'}, {'abstract': 'The case of Curtis Baushke, who was knocked unconscious three times in 14 years while playing soccer, highlights the risks beyond boxing and football.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/22/sports/an-athlete-felled-by-concussions-despite-playing-a-safer-sport.html', 'snippet': 'The case of Curtis Baushke, who was knocked unconscious three times in 14 years while playing soccer, highlights the risks beyond boxing and football.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CLARKSVILLE, Tenn. — Months later, the father mustered the strength to sort through what was left in his dead son’s bedroom. A Little League photo collage. Mardi Gras beads from that soccer tournament in New Orleans. And a typewritten personal essay tucked into a yellow folder, with a single word pen-carved into its plastic cover:', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/22/sports/22barry1/22barry1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 378, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/22/sports/22barry1/22barry1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 378}}, {'ra