3c351940'}, {'abstract': 'A little-known husband-and-wife team, whose studio sits in a woodland outside Madrid, created a playful and unpretentious design for the project’s 15th year.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/22/serpentine-pavilion-selgas-cano/', 'snippet': 'A little-known husband-and-wife team, whose studio sits in a woodland outside Madrid, created a playful and unpretentious design for the project’s 15th year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the same month that Carsten Höller has transformed London’s South Bank into the cultural equivalent of a theme park (giant slides are the centerpiece of his Hayward Gallery show, “Decision“) comes one of the most playful and unpretentious Serpentine Gallery Pavilions in its 15-year history. A series of supersynthetic tunnels comprised of fluorine-based plastic, this latest commission looks like a psychedelic space camp parked on the immaculate lawns of Kensington Gardens. It’s the colorful creation of the Spanish architects Selgas Cano, a little-known husband-and-wife team whose studio sits in a woodland outside Madrid. (Previous architects have included Oscar Niemeyer and Zaha Hadid.) The hope is that visitors to the pavilion, which opens to the public this week, will be propelled on a voyage of discovery (or at the very least, to the cafe\xa0that sits at its heart).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/22/t-magazine/22serpentine-aimee/22serpentine-aimee-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/22/t-magazine/22serpentine-aimee/22serpentine-aimee-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/22/t-magazine/22serpentine-aimee/22serpentine-aimee-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'widt