le': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Sandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stevenson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01b4d920-c7a2-576b-9eaf-bb5451cf0550', 'word_count': 552, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01b4d920-c7a2-576b-9eaf-bb5451cf0550'}, {'abstract': 'The newly opened museum welcomed its first dance performance with this 1960 work, which looked right at home.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/23/arts/dance/review-merce-cunninghams-crises-opens-dance-at-the-new-whitney.html', 'snippet': 'The newly opened museum welcomed its first dance performance with this 1960 work, which looked right at home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the 1950s, the choreographer Merce Cunningham became an early exponent of what would become the Theater of the Absurd, alongside the playwrights Beckett, Ionesco and Pinter. With “Crises” (1960), he made a nonnarrative dance drama in which human behavior becomes inscrutable, absurd and dark. Its dances show one man and four women; what’s still startling is how wild, independent and driven the women are. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/23/arts/23CUNNINGHAM/23CUNNINGHAM-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 421, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/23/arts/23CUNNINGHAM/23CUNNINGHAM-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 421}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/23/arts/23CUNNINGHAM/23CUNNINGHAM-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 719, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'im