, 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a474b434-5033-52de-8f85-0562bb51bbbe', 'word_count': 898, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a474b434-5033-52de-8f85-0562bb51bbbe'}, {'abstract': 'An independent panel will examine how the United Nations handled an inquiry into accusations that French peacekeepers sexually abused children in the Central African Republic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/23/world/africa/panel-is-named-to-investigate-handling-of-allegations-against-french-troops.html', 'snippet': 'An independent panel will examine how the United Nations handled an inquiry into accusations that French peacekeepers sexually abused children in the Central African Republic.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A former justice of Canada’s Supreme Court will lead an independent panel that will examine how the United Nations handled an inquiry into accusations that French peacekeepers not under United Nations command sexually abused children in the Central African Republic, Secretary General Ban Ki-moon announced Monday. In a statement, he said the former justice, Marie Deschamps, would be joined on the panel by Hassan Jallow of Gambia, the prosecutor of the International Criminal Tribunal for Rwanda; and Yasmin Sooka, the executive director of the Foundation for Human Rights in South Africa. Mr. Ban created the panel because of questions over whether United Nations rights officials had acted appropriately when they learned of the accusations. The abuse is alleged to have taken place from December 2013 into mid-2014. Although the organization was quick to investigate the allegations, the French authorities did not learn about them until last July, when a United Nations human rights officer leaked the report to them. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Panel Is Named to Investigate Handling of Allegations Against French Troops', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicke