yt://article/4f8712d8-42e4-5668-8e7f-5e5d48f35348', 'word_count': 648, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f8712d8-42e4-5668-8e7f-5e5d48f35348'}, {'abstract': "Sommelier Laura Vidal and chef Harry Cummins's Paris Popup project comes to the Grand Hôtel Nord Pinus in Arles.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/23/t-magazine/laura-vidal-harry-cummins-provencal-meal.html', 'snippet': "Sommelier Laura Vidal and chef Harry Cummins's Paris Popup project comes to the Grand Hôtel Nord Pinus in Arles.", 'lead_paragraph': 'This summer, a British chef and a Montrealer sommelier aim to serve up the most Provençal meal in the south of France at a pop-up restaurant in the Grand Hôtel Nord Pinus in Arles. For her part, Laura Vidal, who founded the Paris Popup project with chef Harry Cummins in 2012, describes the four-course, prix-fixe bistro menu simply as an “ode to Provence.” But it is crafted almost entirely of local ingredients and with decidedly regional flavors. From the hotel’s dining room and an open-air terrace in the Place du Forum — a beautiful square beloved by locals in the small, ancient city that has recently been experiencing an intense revival of its traditional identity as a center for the arts (the Fondation Vincent Van Gogh reopened just last year there after an expansion and renovation) — the pair will serve lunch and dinner each Wednesday through Saturday until the end of August.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/22/t-magazine/22provence-lobrano/22provence-lobrano-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/22/t-magazine/22provence-lobrano/22provence-lobrano-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/22/t-magazine/22provence-lobrano/22provence-lobrano-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/22/t-magazine/22provence-lobrano/22provence-lobrano-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/22/t-magazine/22provence-lobrano/22provence-lobrano-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/22/t-magazine/22provence-lobrano/22provence-lobrano-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/22/t-magazine/22provence-lobrano/22provence-lobrano-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Most Provençal Meal', 'kicker': 'Food Matters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-06-23T04:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TDesign', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexander Lobrano', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexander', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lobrano', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f8f40a4f-6b66-5dcc-a0fe-2c258d5f173b', 'word_count': 548, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f8f40a4f-6b66-5dcc-a0fe-2c258d5f173b'}, {'abstract': 'A centuries-old technique and carefully developed ingredients go into each batch of Bread and Salt bread.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/23/t-magazine/bread-and-salt-bakery-recipe.html', 'snippet': 'A centuries-old technique and carefully developed ingredients go into each batch of Bread and Salt bread.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Barely six months old, Pittsburgh’s Bread and Salt bakery is fast becoming known for its pane locale (local bread), made in a former slaughterhouse in the centuries-old Italian baking tradition of using organic flour, natural fermentation and local grain. Its owner and founder, the baker Rick Easton, acknowledges that the loaves’ “ugly, formless and dark” appearance belies their addictive flavor — which he accredits to the 100% stone-milled, high-extraction wheat flour crop he develops with a farmer in Weatherbury Farms in Avella, Pa. At the beginning of the season, Easton experiments with different wheat crops and handling techniques to find the right texture for bread he will serve that year. The result is a pane that feels like whole wheat, but the germ is retained so that the richness and natural oils produce “an incredible amount of flavor and sweetness. From a customer’s perspective, it’s great to be able to think about wheat in that way,” Easton explains. “People are used to this supermarket bread that has a two-hour process. My process is 24 hours, and then we’re talking about crop planning that went in a year ago. So last year, someone was thinking about the grain that you would eat today.” Below, Easton shares a recipe for enjoying his signature loaf — or your own favorite, local bread.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/23/t-magazine/23bread-goncharova/23bread-goncharova-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/23/t-magazine/23bread-goncharova/23bread-goncharova-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 