value': 'Treaties', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Conservative Party (Great Britain)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Merkel, Angela', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Great Britain Withdrawal from EU (Brexit)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-23T10:50:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Castle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Castle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/89e5ab0b-5bbc-5231-88b0-028ea0a9c9e3', 'word_count': 1020, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89e5ab0b-5bbc-5231-88b0-028ea0a9c9e3'}, {'abstract': 'Trained as a psychiatrist, Mindy Thompson Fullilove now puts entire cities on the couch.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/magazine/the-town-shrink.html', 'snippet': 'Trained as a psychiatrist, Mindy Thompson Fullilove now puts entire cities on the couch.', 'lead_paragraph': 'High Bridge, spanning the Harlem River and connecting Manhattan to the Bronx, is the oldest bridge in New York City. It is also an aqueduct, or used to be. Built in the 1840s, when public health officials across the country were battling cholera, it carried clean water from upstate to a growing urban population. In just a few decades, planners would build not just aqueducts but the so-called sanitary greens that today we call parks, including Highbridge Park, on the Manhattan side of the bridge. A side benefit of High Bridge was the walkway above the aqueduct that allowed Bronx pedestrians to reach Manhattan. By the 1960s, though, the aqueduct was no longer in use, and city planners, working to fight what was then called urban blight, decided to disconnect the boroughs. The Parks Department closed the old bridge, cutting off an artery.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28cities/28cities-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 274, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28cities/28cities-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 274}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28cities/28cities-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 468, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28cities/28cities-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 936, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28cities/28cities-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28cities/28cities-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28cities/28cities-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Town Shrink', 'kicker': 'The Mental Health Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Town Shrink', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sullivan, Robert', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fullilove, Mindy Thompson', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Urban Areas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychiatry and Psychiatrists', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'High Bridge (NYC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York State Psychiatric Institute', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Area Planning and Renewal', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-23T11:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/024f74f7-10c0-5868-8ce5-3cd311ea3728', 'word_count': 1270, 'uri': 'nyt://article/024f74f7-10c0-5868-8ce5-3cd311ea3728'}, {'abstract': 'Decker, who works in theme park design for Cedar Fair Entertainment, talks about planning amusement parks and his favorite roller coasters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/travel/talking-with-rob-decker-a-real-life-roller-coaster-tycoon.html', 'snippet': 'Decker, who works in theme park design for Cedar Fair Entertainment, talks about planning amusement parks and his favorite roller coasters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From their earliest days, roller coasters have been designed to inspire awe (and an element of fear) among riders. Rob Decker, senior vice president of planning and design at Cedar Fair Entertainment, which operates 14 theme parks in the United States and Canada, tries to make sure that the roller coaster experience continues this tradition, while reinventing how theme parks can continue to attract visitors. “We have a broad group of guests,” Mr. Decker said. “We have roller coaster enthusiasts and our family audience, so we try to balance the two and create a great environment for everyone.”', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/travel/28QA1/28QA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/28/travel/28QA1/28QA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/travel/28QA1/28QA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/travel/28QA1/28QA1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1198, 'width': 1198, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/travel/28QA1/28QA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/28/travel/28QA1/28QA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/travel/28QA1/28QA1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Talking With Rob Decker, a Real-Life Roller Coaster Tycoon', 'kicker': 'Q&A', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rob Decker Enjoys Being Thrown for a Loop at His Parks.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dorman, John L', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Decker, Robert A (1961- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AMUSEMENT AND THEME PARKS', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cedar Fair LP', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roller Coasters', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-23T11:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By John L. Dorman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Dorman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/36eed614-c042-5fb1-9262-b936adc727a1', 'word_count': 704, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36eed614-c042-5fb1-9262-b936adc727a1'}, {'abstract': 'For years, the N.S.A.’s data grab has hinged on a reading of the word that encompasses every aspect of our lives.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/magazine/the-rabbit-hole-of-relevant.html', 'snippet': 'For years, the N.S.A.’s data grab has hinged on a reading of the word that encompasses every aspect of our lives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When a law has a name like “Patriot” or “Freedom,” it’s a sign that you should read the fine print. Somewhere down there, in the terraced subclauses of some forgettable subsection, is a word with a special meaning, a word that offers shelter and concealment to whatever it is that the law actually does. Consider the word “relevant.” You and I use the word to distinguish what matters (the relevant) from what does not (the irrelevant). But to the National Security Agency, there was no irrelevant. Pointing to one appearance of the word in a 2006 update of the USA Patriot Act, the agency argued it could demand records of any one of our phone calls, on the very slight chance of some distant connection to terrorism. A secret court agreed, ordering phone companies to serve up our records on an “ongoing daily basis’’ — 24 hours a day, hundreds of billions of calls in all. No one knew about it until Edward Snowden revealed the existence of the program in 2013. Earlier this month, President Obama signed the USA Freedom Act, which is supposed to close this loophole. Will it? That depends on a fresh pile of fine print.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28firstwords/28mag-28firstwords-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28firstwords/28mag-28firstwords-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28firstwords/28mag-28firstwords-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28firstwords/28mag-28firstwords-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28firstwords/28mag-28firstwords-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28firstwords/28mag-28firstwords-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28firstwords/28mag-28firstwords-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Rabbit-Hole of ‘Relevant’', 'kicker': 'First Words', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Everything Counts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Security Agency', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schwartz, Mattathias', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surveillance of Citizens by Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'English Language', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Bureau of Investigation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Telephones and Telecommunications', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Data-Mining and Database Marketing', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'USA Freedom Act', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wiretapping and Other Eavesdropping Devices and Methods', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-23T11:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mattathias Schwartz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mattathias', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwartz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8fb58ac7-2d90-5b7c-b61c-8af2a20c340e', 'word_count': 1210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8fb58ac7-2d90-5b7c-b61c-8af2a20c340e'}, {'abstract': 'The rich array of microbiota in our intestines can tell us more than you might think.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/magazine/can-the-bacteria-in-your-gut-explain-your-mood.html', 'snippet': 'The rich array of microbiota in our intestines can tell us more than you might think.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eighteen vials were rocking back and forth on a squeaky mechanical device the shape of a butcher scale, and Mark Lyte was beside himself with excitement. ‘‘We actually got some fresh yesterday — freshly frozen,’’ Lyte said to a lab technician. Each vial contained a tiny nugget of monkey feces that were collected at the Harlow primate lab near Madison, Wis., the day before and shipped to Lyte’s lab on the Texas Tech University Health Sciences Center campus in Abilene, Tex.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '46', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28psychobiotics-promo/28psychobiotics-promo-articleLarge-v12.jpg', 'height': 460, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28psychobiotics-promo/28psychobiotics-promo-articleLarge-v12.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 460}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28psychobiotics-promo/28psychobiotics-promo-jumbo-v12.jpg', 'height': 785, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28psychobiotics-promo/28psychobiotics-promo-superJumbo-v12.jpg', 'height': 1570, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28psychobiotics-promo/28psychobiotics-promo-thumbStandard-v12.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28psychobiotics-promo/28psychobiotics-promo-thumbStandard-v12.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbna