b', 'word_count': 1200, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd63c9ee-76ae-577b-99cf-5a781316fd2b'}, {'abstract': 'British files provided by Edward J. Snowden, a former National Security Agency contractor, include information on how terrorism suspects are targeted and how drone strikes can go awry.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/25/world/middleeast/us-drone-strike-said-to-kill-doctor-trying-to-implant-bombs.html', 'snippet': 'British files provided by Edward J. Snowden, a former National Security Agency contractor, include information on how terrorism suspects are targeted and how drone strikes can go awry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Early in 2012, worried that suicide bombers might pass through airline security undetected, American counterterrorism officials ordered a drone strike in Yemen to kill a doctor they believed was working with Al Qaeda to surgically implant explosives in operatives, according to British intelligence documents.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/25/world/25Drones-web/25Drones-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/25/world/25Drones-web/25Drones-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/25/world/25Drones-web/25Drones-web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/25/world/25Drones-web/25Drones-web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1360, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/25/world/25Drones-web/25Drones-web-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/25/world/25Drones-web/25Drones-web-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/25/world/25Drones-web/25Drones-web-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Documents on 2012 Drone Strike Detail How Terrorists Are Targeted', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Documents Detail Workings Behind Drone Strikes ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drones (Pilotless Planes)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Targeted Killings', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Government Communications Headquarters (Great Britain)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Security Agency', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Snowden, Edward J', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Abdulmutallab, Umar Farouk', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bombs and Explosives', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL QAEDA', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Al Qaeda in the Arabian Peninsula', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Yemen', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-24T11:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Shane', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/efa6a208-8a7b-5924-810e-fa2cd6b8c200', 'word_count': 1242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/efa6a208-8a7b-5924-810e-fa2cd6b8c200'}, {'abstract': 'Can brain-scanning help save Freudian psychoanalysis?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/magazine/tell-it-about-your-mother.html', 'snippet': 'Can brain-scanning help save Freudian psychoanalysis?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1900, Sigmund Freud, a Viennese specialist in nervous disorders, began treating the 18-year-old daughter of a rich acquaintance. Freud, who was 44, was just beginning to practice psychoanalysis, a form of therapy that would become known as ‘the talking cure.’ The girl, whom Freud would later refer to as ‘‘Dora,’’ had a cluster of inexplicable symptoms: Her mood was low, she was prone to losing consciousness and, for weeks at a time, she could not speak above a whisper. Freud diagnosed hysteria, a term commonly used in fin de siècle Europe to refer to a disorder in which psychological stress was expressed through physical symptoms. Freud traced Dora’s condition in part to her father’s sexual infidelities that she had felt obliged, from early adolescence, to help orchestrate. When Dora told Freud, in the course of the analysis, that her father’s friend — Herr K., a married man decades her senior — had accosted her in his empty office building, trying to kiss her on her lips, Freud, trained in biology and swept up in his own scientific detachment, couldn’t empathize with her distress. One day, after only 11 weeks of treatment and still far from cured, she stood up at the end of a session, wished Freud a happy new year and never returned.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '38', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28analysis1/28analysis1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28analysis1/28analysis1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28analysis1/28analysis1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 665, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28analysis1/28analysis1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1331, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28analysis1/28analysis1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28analysis1/28analysis1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28analysis1/28analysis1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tell It About Your Mother', 'kicker': 'The Mental Health Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tell It About Your Mother', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schwartz, Casey', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Brain', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Magnetic Resonance Imagers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Freud, Sigmund', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gerber, Andrew I', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychiatry and Psychiatrists', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York State Psychiatric Institute', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Andersen, Susan M (1955- )', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Peterson, Bradley Scott', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kernberg, Otto F (1928- )', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-24T11:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Casey Schwartz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Casey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwartz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/039931e8-08fe-5ed5-ae01-4882681e9896', 'word_count': 4821, 'uri': 'nyt://article/039931e8-08fe-5ed5-ae01-4882681e9896'}, {'abstract': 'How an old-school Chinese-American favorite found a new audience in L.A.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/magazine/chop-sueys-comeback.html', 'snippet': 'How an old-school Chinese-American favorite found a new audience in L.A.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you walk in the east side of the recently renovated Grand Central Market in downtown Los Angeles, you are confronted by the snaking lines at the trendy Eggslut, which sells, as far as I can tell, glorified Egg McMuffins. If you enter on the west side of the blockwide building, however, you come across a time capsule: China Café, a lunch counter that serves Chinese-American food — egg fu yeung (a.k.a. egg foo yong), chow mein, chop suey and other old-fashioned former standards — to a clientele of mostly Latinos and hipsters.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28eat1/28mag-28eat-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 449, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28eat1/28mag-28eat-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 449}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28eat1/28mag-28eat-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 767, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28eat1/28mag-28eat-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1534, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28eat1/28mag-28eat-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28eat1/28mag-28eat-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28eat1/28mag-28eat-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Chop Suey’s Comeback', 'kicker': 'Eat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chop Suey’s Comeback', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bittman, Mark', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'China Cafe (Los Angeles, Calif, Restaurant)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chinese Food (Cuisine)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Grand Central Market', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-24T11:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Bittman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bittman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eba6709a-5a9f-5d69-9ca4-1bc81850479f', 'word_count': 780, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eba6709a-5a9f-5d69-9ca4-1bc81850479f'}, {'abstract': 'A picture of a woman is often made for the pleasure of others; in the work of photographers of modern Africa, it is for her.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/magazine/portrait-of-a-lady.html', 'snippet': 'A picture of a woman is often made for the pleasure of others; in the work of photographers of modern Africa, it is for her.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A photographer working in a commercial studio in West Africa in the 20th century had a straightforward task: to please his clients. In that sense, the Malian photographer Seydou Keïta was — like his father, who worked as a blacksmith, carpenter, mechanic and electrician, among other jobs — a craftsman. He was paid by the public to make pictures. But like his esteemed Malian compatriot Malick Sidibé, Mama Casset of Senegal and Joseph Moise Agbodjelou of Benin, he produced such fine work that we now consider him a great African artist. These master photographers gave us panoramas of life in Bamako, Dakar and Porto-Novo, a vivid record of individual people, largely shorn of their names and stories but irrepressibly alive. Here are good clothes gracefully cut, glowing skin, beautifully coifed hair, polished shoes: all the familiar markers of a person taking pride in his or her appearance. Here’s someone who looks witty, here’s another who looks querulous, another who’s modest, or vain, or sweet. There we see a renegade bra strap slipping off a shoulder, there a large laughing man with a baby, a woman in a bathing suit, youths partying at night with their Afros, bell-bottoms, precious LPs and endless reserves of cool.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28onphotography_ss-slide-VMV9/28onphotography_ss-slide-VMV9-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 758, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28onphotography_ss-slide-VMV9/28onphotography_ss-slide-VMV9-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 758}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28onphotography_ss-slide-VMV9/28onphotography_ss-slide-VMV9-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 811, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28onphotography_ss-slide-VMV9/28onphotography_ss-slide-VMV9-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1622, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28onphotography_ss-slide-VMV9/28onphotography_ss-slide-VMV9-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28onphotography_ss-slide-VMV9/28onphotography_ss-slide-VMV9-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28onphotography_ss-slide-VMV9/28onphotography_ss-slide-VMV9-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Portrait of a Lady', 'kicker': 'On Photography', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Portrait of a Lady', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sidibe, Malick', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFRICA', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mali', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-24T11:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Teju Cole', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Teju', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cole', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3fb9132-7ba9-52f2-86cf-691fde2fdbfa', 'word_count': 1583, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3fb9132-7ba9-52f2-86cf-691fde2fdbfa'}, {'abstract': 'The Ethicists consider how to reconcile two wildly conflicting stories about a deceased parent.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/magazine/how-do-i-counter-my-sisters-abuse-claims-against-our-father.html', 'snippet': 'The Ethicists consider how to reconcile two wildly conflicting stories about a deceased parent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My sister has become a personality in the media primarily due to her colorful past. She gives lectures and has a self-help book coming out soon. One of her minor themes concerns our deceased father and is particularly nasty. Although there is no way to positively refute statements she has made, neither I nor my two brothers and my mother believe my father capable of the events my sister describes. My father was a kind and giving person. He has living brothers, sisters, nieces and nephews who loved him dearly and would be very upset by my sister’s claims. My husband suggests I confront my sister, but that will most likely not yield a fruitful outcome or change anything. My concern is to protect positive memories. I propose to write about him myself, posting my memories on the Internet. I would not attempt to refute my sister, merely focus on my father and memories of him. This seems the least aggressive path and least likely to create any stir around my sister or her supporters. Since the Internet seems destined to live forever, at least an alternative memorial for my father would exist. Your thoughts are appreciated. NAME WITHHELD', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28ethicists/28mag-28ethicists-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 596, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28ethicists/28mag-28ethicists-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 596}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28ethicists/28mag-28ethicists-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1017, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28ethicists/28mag-28ethicists-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height'