re we doing?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Most of the Way With Obamacare', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-06-24T14:34:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d2a4236-19e1-542b-a45d-80f67169a7ab', 'word_count': 318, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d2a4236-19e1-542b-a45d-80f67169a7ab'}, {'abstract': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: Saigon severs diplomatic links with France in 1965.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/24/1965-saigon-breaks-with-france/', 'snippet': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: Saigon severs diplomatic links with France in 1965.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAIGON — South Vietnam’s ruling military junta today [June 24] severed diplomatic relations with France and for the first time officially declared war against the Communist Viet Cong. The government also announced it was introducing harsh measures including: 1. Closing down all newspapers published in the country. Saigon alone has had 36 newspapers, but now the nation as a whole will only have two, one for civilians and one for soldiers. 2. Clamping an ‘‘anti-luxury’’ 10 p.m. to 2 a.m. curfew on the capital. Premier Nguyen Cao Ky warned that his government would deal severely with anyone who raised his voice in protest. — New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, June 25, 1965', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/25/blogs/25iht-oldjune25-saigon/25iht-oldjune