article/de0488a7-a845-5df2-a20e-3a5b0a2c8534', 'word_count': 210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de0488a7-a845-5df2-a20e-3a5b0a2c8534'}, {'abstract': '“Sargent: Portraits of Artists and Friends,” which gives a sense of the painter’s social circle, opens Tuesday at the Metropolitan Museum of Art.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/arts/design/the-friends-john-singer-sargent-painted.html', 'snippet': '“Sargent: Portraits of Artists and Friends,” which gives a sense of the painter’s social circle, opens Tuesday at the Metropolitan Museum of Art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'John Singer Sargent may be known for squandering his considerable gifts on paintings of socialites that he dashed off with nearly effortless panache, but “Sargent: Portraits of Artists and Friends,” opening Tuesday at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, sets him in a different milieu than that of the merely moneyed and pedigreed. Organized by the National Portrait Gallery, London, in collaboration with the Met, the exhibition of more than 90 paintings, watercolors and drawings places Sargent in the trans-Atlantic vanguard of art and culture of the late 19th and early 20th centuries. Subjects include other artists like Claude Monet and Auguste Rodin and writers like Robert Louis Stevenson and Henry James. The collector Isabella Stewart Gardner appears in a portrait that rarely leaves the Gardner Museum in Boston. The Shakespearean actress Ellen Terry is pictured performing and in a formal portrait. These and other works, which reveal Sargent in unguarded moments responding to people he loved and admired, should be a crowd-pleaser. (Through Oct. 4; 212-535-7710, metmuseum.org.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/arts/28GUIDE2/28GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 495, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xla