t theater, and its first visit to New York in 11 years, the city that was its second home from 1949 to 1976 — is in some ways problematic. But at the opener on Tuesday, with a performance of Frederick Ashton’s Shakespeare-fairyland “The Dream” (1964), many aspects of style brightly flooded the theater.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/25/arts/25ROYALDREAM/25ROYALDREAM-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 572, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/25/arts/25ROYALDREAM/25ROYALDREAM-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 572}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/25/arts/25ROYALDREAM/25ROYALDREAM-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 976, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/25/arts/25ROYALDREAM/25ROYALDREAM-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1952, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/25/arts/25ROYALDREAM/25ROYALDREAM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/25/arts/25ROYALDREAM/25ROYALDREAM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/25/arts/25ROYALDREAM/25ROYALDREAM-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: The Royal Ballet Returns to New York With ‘The Dream’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Still So Familiar After Quite a Spell', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ashton, Frederick', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Dream (Ballet)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Ballet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-24T21:51:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a6e9e51f-6264-5958-b245-3ce955f01e2a', 'word_count': 842, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a6e9e51f-6264-5958-b245-3ce955f01e2a'}, {'abstract': 'The hull of a slave ship is central to “Malungos,” a work by the Brazilian choreographer Jelon Vieira.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/25/arts/dance/review-dancebrazil-offers-a-middle-passage-lesson-at-the-joyce-theater.html', 'snippet': 'The hull of a slave ship is central to “Malungos,” a work by the Brazilian choreographer Jelon Vieira.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Malungos,” a new work by Jelon Vieira, the founder and artistic director of DanceBrazil, represents the Middle Passage, the awful transit of enslaved Africans from their homelands to settlements in the New World. In the piece, performers bid sad farewells to ancestral grounds, but then, trapped in the hull of a slaver’s ship, they rediscover the bonding force of dance. “Malungo” was a word that slaves employed to address one another, a recognition of solidarity. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None