November Revolution of 1918, which brought about the Weimar Republic, Germany’s first democracy. Somehow they were confident that merely by rejecting the sentimentality of prewar German Expressionism, and substituting a more realistic, sober view of the life around them, they could not only bring about a new society, but usher in a “new man.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28SOREL1/28SOREL1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 891, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28SOREL1/28SOREL1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 891}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28SOREL1/28SOREL1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 690, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28SOREL1/28SOREL1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1380, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28SOREL1/28SOREL1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28SOREL1/28SOREL1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28SOREL1/28SOREL1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘New Objectivity’ and ‘Max Beckmann: The Still Lifes’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Degenerate Art’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beckmann, Max', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-25T13:44:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Sorel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sorel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1125e861-d6db-5233-8a90-6ae022e6a74a', 'word_count': 1091, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1125e861-d6db-5233-8a90-6ae022e6a74a'}, {'abstract': 'Holland Cotter reviews four new books about the 21st-century art world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/books/review/the-contemporaries-painting-now-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Holland Cotter reviews four new books about the 21st-century art world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Everyone says the art world has changed, which is true, though your view of how, and how much, will depend on your entry point. Mine was long ago, in the early 1970s, when New York City was utterly unlike the High Line town of today. It was on the verge of bankruptcy. Physically, Manhattan was unraveling at the seams, and “urban renewal” had trashed vast stretches of the other boroughs, leaving working-class residents, many of them new immigrants, marooned.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28COTTER/28COTTER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 822, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_nam