_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holland Cotter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holland', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cotter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b8b28391-1c80-562a-9da8-c06b7fc83806', 'word_count': 2360, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b8b28391-1c80-562a-9da8-c06b7fc83806'}, {'abstract': 'New books about the theft and forgery of artworks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/books/review/master-thieves-and-the-art-of-the-con.html', 'snippet': 'New books about the theft and forgery of artworks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On March 18, 1990, at 1:20 in the \xadmorning, two men dressed in police uniforms demanded entrance to the Isabella Stewart Gardner Museum. When the guard at the front desk complied, they bound him and his partner in handcuffs. “This is a robbery,” the intruders announced. “Don’t give us any problems, and you won’t get hurt.” The senior guard was quick to respond. “Don’t worry,” he said. “They don’t pay me enough to get hurt.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28subKEATS/28subKEATS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 415, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28subKEATS/28subKEATS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 415}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28subKEATS/28subKEATS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 708, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28subKEATS/28subKEATS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1415, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28subKEATS/28subKEATS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28subKEATS/28subKEATS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28subKEATS/28subKEATS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Master Thieves’ and ‘The Art of the Con’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Crimes of the Art', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kurkjian, Stephen A (1943- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Amore, Anthony M', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forgery', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gardner, Isabella Stewart, Museum', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-25T13:44:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathon Keats', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keats', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8308a224-1cda-51aa-a209-7d131299b799', 'word_count': 1286, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8308a224-1cda-51aa-a209-7d131299b799'}, {'abstract': 'Annie Cohen-Solal argues that Rothko’s Jewishness is at the core of his life and art.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/books/review/mark-rothko-toward-the-light-in-the-chapel-by-annie-cohen-solal.html', 'snippet': 'Annie Cohen-Solal argues that Rothko’s Jewishness is at the core of his life and art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The contribution of Jewish writers, musicians, movie directors and art critics from Eastern Europe to American cultural life in the 20th century is undeniable and has been copiously documented. Jewish artists, however, have largely escaped this scrutiny. Perhaps this is strictly owing to a scarcity of significant figures until the rise of abstraction in the second half of the century, which occurred, first and foremost, because of Judaism’s notorious aniconism and its vexed relationship with art.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28AZAGURY/28Azagury-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 786, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28AZAGURY/28Azagury-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 786}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28AZAGURY/28Azagury-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 782, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28AZAGURY/28Azagury-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1135, 'width': 866, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28AZAGURY/28Azagury-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28AZAGURY/28Azagury-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28AZAGURY/28Azagury-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Mark Rothko: Toward the Light in the Chapel,’ by Annie Cohen-Solal', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rothko', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cohen-Solal, Annie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rothko, Mark', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-25T13:45:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Yaëlle Azagury', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Yaëlle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Azagury', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/704ca9fd-e766-536d-8d7d-997d6c42877b', 'word_count': 760, 'uri': 'nyt://article/704ca9fd-e766-536d-8d7d-997d6c42877b'}, {'abstract': 'Marilyn Minter’s body of work is playful, nasty and full of surprise, and interested in confusing our networks of disgust and desire.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/books/review/marilyn-minter-pretty-dirty.html', 'snippet': 'Marilyn Minter’s body of work is playful, nasty and full of surprise, and interested in confusing our networks of disgust and desire.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Marilyn Minter is partial to spit, \xadspatter and redheads. Hers is a “Black Mass brand of femininity,” in the words of the poet Eileen Myles, and she’s become best known for candy-colored shots of dirty feet in designer heels, puffy pubic hair, navels ringed with beads of sweat; paintings and photographs that blur high art and high fashion, photorealism and abstraction. It’s a body of work that’s playful and nasty and full of surprise.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28SEHGAL/28SEHGAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28SEHGAL/28SEHGAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28SEHGAL/28SEHGAL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28SEHGAL/28SEHGAL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1365, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28SEHGAL/28SEHGAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28SEHGAL/28SEHGAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28SEHGAL/28SEHGAL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Marilyn Minter: Pretty/Dirty’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Glitter and Grunge', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sehgal, Parul', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Minter, Marilyn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-25T13:45:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Parul Sehgal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Parul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sehgal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/25f51887-3451-5aa4-b824-34d5234ee569', 'word_count': 1007, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25f51887-3451-5aa4-b824-34d5234ee569'}, {'abstract': 'Deborah Solomon on two new books that offer group portraits of the Paris art scene in its heyday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/books/review/montmartre-montparnasse.html', 'snippet': 'Deborah Solomon on two new books that offer group portraits of the Paris art scene in its heyday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today, when the art world can seem like a subdivision of the investment banking business, it is easy to romanticize Paris in the early 1900s. When you think of the beginnings of modern art, you think back to a golden age of creativity, a time when every waking moment, it seemed, was \xaddevoted to painting and poetry and the carnal pleasures of the bohemian life.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28SOLOMON1/28SOLOMON1-articleLarg