', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Matisse, Henri', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Soutine, Chaim', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chagall, Marc', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-25T13:46:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deborah Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deborah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e48e8c2-49a8-5cd4-ae84-a99f87aa02f0', 'word_count': 1583, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e48e8c2-49a8-5cd4-ae84-a99f87aa02f0'}, {'abstract': 'In Nancy Reisman’s new novel, a family is irrevocably changed when a child dies in a tragic accident.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/books/review/trompe-loeil-by-nancy-reisman.html', 'snippet': 'In Nancy Reisman’s new novel, a family is irrevocably changed when a child dies in a tragic accident.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Genuinely satisfying, heart-shaking novels combine an absolute narrative authority with an apparently effortless style. You question nothing, savor every phrase and nuance, live willingly within their pages from the first word to the last. They can also be slippery things: impossible to wrap up or pin down. Just read it, you urge the uninitiated, already envying them the experience to come.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28MEYERSON/28MEYERSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 804, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28MEYERSON/28MEYERSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 804}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28MEYERSON/28MEYERSON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 764, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28MEYERSON/28MEYERSON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1528, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28MEYERSON/28MEYERSON-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28MEYERSON/28MEYERSON-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28MEYERSON/28MEYERSON-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Trompe L’Oeil,’ by Nancy Reisman', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fooling the Eye', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reisman, Nancy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "Trompe L'Oeil (Book)", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-25T13:47:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Myerson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Myerson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b44ca60-da9f-5159-b43d-6739f49d9039', 'word_count': 878, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b44ca60-da9f-5159-b43d-6739f49d9039'}, {'abstract': 'Yiadom-Boakye’s paintings, which feature invented characters, summon the triumphs and its omissions of the genre of portraiture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/books/review/lynette-yiadom-boakye.html', 'snippet': 'Yiadom-Boakye’s paintings, which feature invented characters, summon the triumphs and its omissions of the genre of portraiture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The figures who inhabit Lynette Yiadom-Boakye’s paintings reveal little of themselves. Seen mostly alone, sometimes interacting in pairs or small groups, they pose in bare grounds composed of loose brush strokes that turn emptiness into imminence. Her work taps a deep well in Western painting, recalling expressive devices in classic portraits by Velázquez, Manet and Degas, with one big difference: Nearly every one of Yiadom-Boakye’s characters is black. They are also entirely fictional, inventions of a British artist whose canny deployment of the genre of portraiture summons both its triumphs and its omissions.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28HIRSCH/28HIRSCH-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 487, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28HIRSCH/28HIRSCH-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 487}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28HIRSCH/28HIRSCH-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 831, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'ima