c', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Forsythe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Forsythe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d042b37-2dea-5090-8e59-4c73aafca975', 'word_count': 1168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d042b37-2dea-5090-8e59-4c73aafca975'}, {'abstract': 'The narrator of Gerard Woodward’s novel is an enigmatic and eccentric artist employed by the British Army to help with camouflage during World War II.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/books/review/vanishing-by-gerard-woodward.html', 'snippet': 'The narrator of Gerard Woodward’s novel is an enigmatic and eccentric artist employed by the British Army to help with camouflage during World War II.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Forget a “likable” character. Forget even mildly appealing. “Vanishing” is a book that sets out to destroy your sympathies for its narrator. This shape-shifting man is a baby and a bully, a liar and a layabout, a tease and a tormentor. He might even be a spy — unless, of course, he’s just woefully, dramatically misunderstood.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28SCHAMA/28SCHAMA-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 500, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28SCHAMA/28SCHAMA-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 500}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/books/review/28SCHAMA/28SCHAMA-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 853, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/20