e/59bf2ec2-a37d-5e70-bf29-f74c7472559f', 'word_count': 530, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59bf2ec2-a37d-5e70-bf29-f74c7472559f'}, {'abstract': 'The Royal Ballet performed two sharply contrasting 1960s works, Frederick Ashton’s “The Dream” and Kenneth MacMillan’s “Song of the Earth,” at the Koch Theater.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/26/arts/dance/review-from-royal-ballet-a-blend-of-enchantment-and-intensity.html', 'snippet': 'The Royal Ballet performed two sharply contrasting 1960s works, Frederick Ashton’s “The Dream” and Kenneth MacMillan’s “Song of the Earth,” at the Koch Theater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The diverse beauties and eloquence of Royal Ballet style became doubly evident on Wednesday, when the company presented a double bill of contemporaneous ballets by Frederick Ashton and Kenneth MacMillan that is the centerpiece of its season. The ballerina Marianela Nuñez was deeply impressive in MacMillan’s “Song of the Earth” (1965), the first performance of this important ballet in New York since 1976. As Titania in Ashton’s Shakespearean adaptation “The Dream” (1964), Sarah Lamb, more relaxed than she had been in Tuesday’s gala account of the same ballet, rose with the rest of the cast to make the ballet’s poetic comedy all the more spellbinding.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/26/arts/26ROYALEARTH/0626ROYALEARTH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 474, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/26/arts/26ROYALEARTH/0626ROYALEARTH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 474}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/26/arts/26ROYALEARTH/0626ROYALEARTH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 809, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/26/arts/26ROYALEARTH/0626ROYALEARTH-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1617, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/26/arts/26ROYALEARTH/0626ROYALEARTH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/26/arts/26ROYALEARTH/0626ROYALEARTH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/26/arts/26ROYALEARTH/0626ROYALEARTH-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: From Royal Ballet, a Blend of Enchantment and Intensity', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Menu of Poetic Magic and Philosophical Sweep', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ashton, Frederick', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'MacMillan, Kenneth', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Ballet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-25T22:07:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ca4d390-b173-5b5d-9acb-1c47726e1fe7', 'word_count': 827, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ca4d390-b173-5b5d-9acb-1c47726e1fe7'}, {'abstract': 'The choreographer finds commonalities in the sensibilities of three extremely different visual artists and interprets them through dance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/26/arts/dance/review-escher-bacon-rothko-a-dance-triptych-from-zvi-gotheiner.html', 'snippet': 'The choreographer finds commonalities in the sensibilities of three extremely different visual artists and interprets them through dance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Apart from being visual artists with powerful visions and immediately recognizable styles, M. C. Escher, Francis Bacon and Mark Rothko wouldn’t seem to have much in common. But each has been an inspiration to the choreographer Zvi Gotheiner, who has now made a three-part dance that sets his interpretations of those artists side by side like the panels of a triptych. The connecting sensibility is his. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/26/arts/ZVIDANCE/ZVIDANCE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 397, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/26/arts/ZVIDANCE/ZVIDANCE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 397}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/26/arts/ZVIDANCE/ZVIDANCE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 678, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/26/arts/ZVIDANCE/ZVIDANCE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1356, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/26/arts/ZVIDANCE/ZVIDANCE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thum