dline': {'main': 'Celebrating Love, Grieving Loss as Charleston Funerals Begin', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Charleston, SC, Shooting (2015)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Funerals and Memorials', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Charleston (SC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-26T04:59:51+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/5d46d1a4-d407-574a-ab27-c9fb43ec8272', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/5d46d1a4-d407-574a-ab27-c9fb43ec8272'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/26/arts/television/whats-on-tv-friday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (Disney) TEEN BEACH 2 (2015) Their endless summer has ended. In this sequel to the 2013 Disney Channel smash musical, it’s been three months since Mack (Maia Mitchell) and Brady (Ross Lynch) surfed 50 years into the past and onto the set of “Wet Side Story.” But in real life, the only epic wave is the one drowning their love. Enter Lela (Grace Phipps) and Tanner (Garrett Clayton), reprising their Annette Funicello-Frankie Avalon impression, and a magic necklace that zaps them into the present, where Lela discovers an aptitude for calculus, a taste for flavored lip gloss and a desire to be more than a dreamboat’s girlfriend. But if she and Tanner don’t return to 1962, Mack and Brady might never meet. Like the original, “Teen Beach 2” mashes up “Beach Blanket Bingo,” “Back to the Future,” “Grease” and “Glee” in bubble-gum tracks like “Best Summer Ever” and “Gotta Be Me.” Its big-kid stars are young adults by now, but it’s still the little kids (and their parents) who will want to sing along.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On TV Friday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What&#8217;s On TV Friday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-26T05:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b4bf44c-e330-568e-a1d6-46e530e6834d', 'word_count': 724, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b4bf44c-e330-568e-a1d6-46e530e6834d'}, {'abstract': 'With New York set to add nearly 1,300 new officers, Police Commissioner William J. Bratton and Mayor Bill de Blasio spoke on Thursday about the reinvigorated approach to community policing that the reinforcements would aid.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/26/nyregion/to-police-new-york-in-the-future-bratton-looks-to-his-past.html', 'snippet': 'With New York set to add nearly 1,300 new officers, Police Commissioner William J. Bratton and Mayor Bill de Blasio spoke on Thursday about the reinvigorated approach to community policing that the reinforcements would aid.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It has been billed as a blueprint for policing New York in the decades to come, an opportunity to get more officers back into the city’s neighborhoods and connected to the people they are assigned to serve. And as William J. Bratton, the police commissioner, laid out his vision for the future on Thursday, he made it clear that it was very much informed by the experience of the past.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'capti