'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f0d7be0-43bc-51da-9958-74c428196df5', 'word_count': 3045, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f0d7be0-43bc-51da-9958-74c428196df5'}, {'abstract': 'Nonfiction filmmakers say that making something “more true” sometimes means playing with time or production methods.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/movies/documentary-filmmakers-talk-about-manipulation-in-their-work.html', 'snippet': 'Nonfiction filmmakers say that making something “more true” sometimes means playing with time or production methods.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The opening of “Cartel Land” drops viewers into an outdoor Mexican meth lab at night; the head chef sits down for an interview. The scene might be enough of a jolt to viewers, but what they won’t know — unless they’re familiar with meth cooking — is that it was shot over two nights. The filmmaker, having gone to great lengths to secure access once, insisted on going back a second time to film more of the days-long drugmaking process.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/arts/28DOCDIRECTING3SUB/28DOCDIRECTING3SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 255, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/28/arts/28DOCDIRECTING3SUB/28DOCDIRECTING3SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 255}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/arts/28DOCDIRECTING3SUB/28DOCDIRECTING3SUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 436, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/arts/28DOCDIRECTING3SUB/28DOCDI