/06/28/magazine/post-time.html', 'snippet': 'Selected by Natasha Trethewey.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What are the instances in which perception changes, in which we suddenly see our parents in a different light? Reading this poem, I thought of what I’ve come to understand about my own father’s life that I could not before his death, and how a long-ago moment between us now seems like a “last-minute window,” when any outcome was still possible.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28poem/28mag-28poem-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 310, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28poem/28mag-28poem-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 310}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28poem/28mag-28poem-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 529, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28poem/28mag-28poem-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 844, 'width': 1633, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28poem/28mag-28poem-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28poem/28mag-28poem-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28poem/28mag-28poem-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Post Time’', 'kicker': 'Poem', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Post Time', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trethewey, Natasha', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Becker, Robin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-26T10:59:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Becker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Becker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d32d4d24-14b5-5444-ab5a-95df12c546a6', 'word_count': 66, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d32d4d24-14b5-5444-ab5a-95df12c546a6'}, {'abstract': 'Be a listener, not a talker.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/magazine/how-to-go-undercover.html', 'snippet': 'Be a listener, not a talker.', 'lead_paragraph': '‘‘Learn to walk the walk and talk the talk,’’ says Cody Carlson, who wore a hidden miniature video camera during the year and a half he worked stints at places like a dairy farm in upstate New York, a pork producer in Pennsylvania and two egg farms in Iowa. His goal was to capture irrefutable evidence of mistreatment that animal rights groups could later use in lawsuits and public-awareness campaigns. Before going undercover, the 31-year-old Carlson spent weeks in anthropologist mode, observing livestock auctions, volunteering at pet stores, studying the lingo and aesthetics that would allow a New York City kid to blend in at hog barns.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'i