y Malia Wollan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Malia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wollan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a21391d5-d50d-5f88-98df-4582c16705c4', 'word_count': 341, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a21391d5-d50d-5f88-98df-4582c16705c4'}, {'abstract': 'An author fears losing control of her own story.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/magazine/why-im-afraid-of-therapy.html', 'snippet': 'An author fears losing control of her own story.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I was growing up, my father was fascinated by people talking about themselves. Books, movies, television — if it had an extended scene of self-analysis, he was reading it or watching it. Part of this enchantment was pure anthropological interest: My father grew up poor in Honolulu, a third-generation Japanese-American who’d gotten up and out of his neighborhood. His idea of culture was shaped largely by his discovery of writers and filmmakers — Philip Roth, Woody Allen, Wallace Shawn — whose definition of an intellectual life included exposing their own fantasies and grotesqueries to an audience that was assumed to be (and in fact was) interested, even beguiled, by them. To my father, the very idea of talking about himself to a stranger for 50 minutes must have seemed equal parts horrifying and seductive, the equivalent of spending a day eating nothing but fudge.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '53', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28resistanceside/28mag-28resistanceside-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 597, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/28/magazine/28resistanceside/28mag-28resistanceside-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 597}}, 