nk': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cruz, Ted', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jindal, Bobby', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Malley, Martin J", 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-26T11:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Politics', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/456dc69e-50d5-5812-a964-6fdb3f6fb2be', 'word_count': 1501, 'uri': 'nyt://article/456dc69e-50d5-5812-a964-6fdb3f6fb2be'}, {'abstract': 'Deep in the rugged heart of eastern Anatolia, the resilient Alevi Kurds open their hearts and homes to a visitor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/travel/finding-paradise-in-turkeys-munzur-valley.html', 'snippet': 'Deep in the rugged heart of eastern Anatolia, the resilient Alevi Kurds open their hearts and homes to a visitor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Deep in the rugged heart of eastern Anatolia, the Munzur River flows from the base of a skyscraping limestone massif, wending its way into the world across a grassy valley cradled between dog-toothed peaks and forested hills. The water is impossibly clear and numbingly cold and, to most of those who visit its source, sacred. “It’s easy to feel close to God here,” I was told by one follower of the mystical Alevi religion, who, like hundreds of other women, men and children, had come to the springs — called Munzur Gozeleri — on a scorching July afternoon.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Finding Paradise in Turkey’s Munzur Valley', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Their Side of Paradise', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Benanav, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kurds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Anatolia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-26T11:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Benanav', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Benanav', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d359341d-a235-5618-8df4-2a08646a93d2', 'word_count': 2925, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d359341d-a235-5618-8df4-2a08646a93d2'}, {'abstract': 'California’s Empty Quarter has caves and lava fields, waterfalls and wheat fields, and the remains of internment camps set against the rugged landscape.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/travel/exploring-tule-lake-an-austere-land-with-a-complex-history.html', 'snippet': 'California’s Empty Quarter has caves and lava fields, waterfalls and wheat fields, and the remains of internment camps set against the rugged landscape.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At night during the 1940s, Tule Lake in California used to be lit up like Las Vegas, a sprawling megalopolis, which was filled with wooden barracks, tanks, guard towers and a mess hall instead of casinos.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Exploring Tule Lake, an Austere Land With a Complex History', 'kicker': 'Road Trip', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Austere Land With a Complex History', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zach, Eli