it City', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mosques', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kuwait City (Kuwait)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-26T18:48:46+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/e53dea30-7428-58da-9b2d-a0fe532943ee', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/e53dea30-7428-58da-9b2d-a0fe532943ee'}, {'abstract': 'It took 10 years to get the holiday on the city’s school calendar, the culmination of a campaign by officials and advocates representing a divided and often politically reticent population.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/27/nyregion/for-asian-new-yorkers-a-persistent-but-quiet-campaign-for-lunar-new-year.html', 'snippet': 'It took 10 years to get the holiday on the city’s school calendar, the culmination of a campaign by officials and advocates representing a divided and often politically reticent population.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Wednesday night at the Yung Wing School, a public elementary school in Chinatown, students performed songs from the Disney musical “Mulan,” dancing crisply in traditional silk costumes ironed that morning by their parents. These were the voices of the next Asian-American generation in New York City — and they were loud.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/27/nyregion/17lunary1sub/17lunary1sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/27/nyregion/17lunary1sub/17lunary1sub-articleLar