'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-26T18:54:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liz Robbins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liz', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robbins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a4276c4-04e0-52c0-b722-ca263d6eaa89', 'word_count': 1204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a4276c4-04e0-52c0-b722-ca263d6eaa89'}, {'abstract': 'The agency’s use of internal trials has raised questions about fairness because defendants may not have all the same rights they have in federal court.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/business/secs-in-house-justice-raises-questions.html', 'snippet': 'The agency’s use of internal trials has raised questions about fairness because defendants may not have all the same rights they have in federal court.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the top cop on Wall Street, the Securities and Exchange Commission usually enjoys broad support for its work fighting fraud in the nation’s financial markets. But a recent agency stance has generated such criticism that even some of the S.E.C.’s high-powered alumni are calling for a do-over.', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Crying Foul on Plans to Expand the S.E.C.’s In-House Court System', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Crying Foul on In-House S.E.C. Courts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morgenson, Gretchen', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Securities and Commodities Violations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Courts and the Judiciary', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rako