nson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ecfd4374-942f-5d2e-9373-a2dc4100c841', 'word_count': 459, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ecfd4374-942f-5d2e-9373-a2dc4100c841'}, {'abstract': 'The Dyckman Farmhouse Museum, in Inwood, reopened June 5 after a six-month sprucing up. Nearby is Darling Coffee, featuring desserts and ceremonious pour-overs for coffee obsessives.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/28/nyregion/two-reasons-to-visit-inwood-dyckman-farmhouse-museum-and-darling-coffee.html', 'snippet': 'The Dyckman Farmhouse Museum, in Inwood, reopened June 5 after a six-month sprucing up. Nearby is Darling Coffee, featuring desserts and ceremonious pour-overs for coffee obsessives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The confluence of the Harlem and Hudson Rivers shapes a pocket of land at the northernmost tip of Manhattan, a portal to the island’s oldest forest once roamed by Native Americans. In the 1600s, warring Lenapes and Mohawks and a smallpox epidemic cleared the way for a Dutch settler, Jan Dyckman. He exercised his ax skills to sever virgin trees and built a homestead that still beats in the heart of Inwood.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/nyregion/28REASONS1/28REASONS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/28/nyregion/28REASONS1/28REASONS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/nyregion/28REASONS1/28REASONS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 