, 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Quotation of the Day', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quotation of the Day', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-06-30T03:14:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Quote', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b12f4d8-22e1-5eac-8528-a5c8f60f76b2', 'word_count': 46, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b12f4d8-22e1-5eac-8528-a5c8f60f76b2'}, {'abstract': 'The publisher, who releases a new book today, shares with T his view of the nearby break and personal collection of projects and mementos.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/30/t-magazine/tom-adler-california-home.html', 'snippet': 'The publisher, who releases a new book today, shares with T his view of the nearby break and personal collection of projects and mementos.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like any good hideout, the publisher Tom Adler’s redwood-frame house on Padaro Lane near Carpinteria, Calif., keeps a view of its own freedom. Though the longtime surfer doesn’t quite “chase it like he used to,” the window due south from his writing desk, past a large painted portrait of the poet Sadakichi Hartmann, opens onto a stretch of the Pacific Ocean, where Adler “surfs when there’s surf.” To live for decades at famed breaks like Rincon Point and this one “has been a charmed life, and on top of that I’ve had friendships,” says the former creative director, of finding long-riding waves as a teenager and never leaving.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tom Adler’s Home Sweet Surf Abode', 'kicker': 'Home and Work', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-06-30T04:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TDesign', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Su Wu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Su', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7980c11d-fb3b-54ce-b797-54acda4c8f45', 'word_count': 1093, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7980c11d-fb3b-54ce-b797-54acda4c8f45'}, {'abstract': 'The AARP RealPad promises “no confusion and no frustration” for older adults. Starting with the on button, it delivers the opposite.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/30/senior-tech-a-tablet-for-aging-hands-fall-short/', 'snippet': 'The AARP RealPad promises “no confusion and no frustration” for older adults. Starting with the on button, it delivers the opposite.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A tablet can be the perfect gateway gadget when it comes to connecting an aging mom, dad or tech-reluctant grandparent with the rest of the wired world. Small, light and simple, a tablet requires less technical skill and manual dexterity than, say, a laptop or a smartphone.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/30/science/30AARP/30AARP-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 500, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/30/science/30AARP/30AARP-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 500}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/30/science/30AARP/30AARP-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 854, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/30/science/30AARP/30AARP-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1200, 'width': 1439, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail'