ine': {'main': 'A Southern Chef’s Recipe for Pickled Magnolia Petals', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flowers and Plants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Athens (Ga)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-06-30T13:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rinne Allen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rinne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Allen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5874be7f-246c-5386-be48-6526b106b62f', 'word_count': 322, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5874be7f-246c-5386-be48-6526b106b62f'}, {'abstract': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: The Germans are using a wireless station in Long Island in 1915.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/30/1915-germans-using-wireless-code/', 'snippet': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: The Germans are using a wireless station in Long Island in 1915.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — The New York edition of the Herald charges that the Germans are making use of the wireless station at Sayville, Long Island, to inform their submarines off the English coast of the departure of vessels from the American side. The station is under government censorship, and the transmission of military information is forbidden. It said that the messages, although purporting to be ordinary commercial telegrams, conceal a military code. The Washington correspondent of the Herald takes the government to task in connection with Germany’s forthcoming answer to the Lusitania Note, which is expected to be favorable. — The New York Herald, European Edition, July 1, 1915'